window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("1peter", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"1 Peter","chapters":"5","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"A Living Hope for Exiles","paragraphs":["Picture small groups of believers scattered across the northern half of what is now Turkey — in harbour towns, mountain villages and Roman cities. A few years ago they worshipped idols, drank at the feasts of the guilds and lived like everyone around them. Then they heard about Jesus, were baptised and began to live differently. Now their neighbours call them strange. Their old friends slander them. Some husbands are angry with their believing wives; some masters beat their believing slaves. And rumours are coming from Rome that the emperor himself may turn against the Christians. They are at home in their own towns, yet suddenly they feel like foreigners.","To these people the apostle Peter writes a letter of hope. He calls them “the chosen ones who are living as foreigners in the Dispersion” (1 Peter 1:1). They have lost their place in the world, but they have gained something better: God “caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (1 Peter 1:3). Their home is not here. Their inheritance is kept in heaven. And their suffering is not a sign that God has forgotten them, but the path their Lord walked before them.","The letter's great theme is suffering followed by glory. Peter uses the language of suffering more than any other New Testament letter, and he always ties it to Christ: “For to this you were called, because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example, that you should follow his steps” (1 Peter 2:21). He calls believers to holy living, good conduct before unbelievers, humble submission, love for one another and courageous hope. He does not promise escape from the fire. He promises that the fire will prove their faith and end in glory.","The writer is Simon Peter, the fisherman Jesus called to be a fisher of men, the disciple who confessed “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God” (Matthew 16:16) and then denied Him three times. After the resurrection Jesus restored him with three questions and three commands to feed His sheep. In this letter the restored shepherd feeds the flock. He writes “Through Silvanus” (1 Peter 5:12) — Silas, Paul's travelling companion, who probably helped with the writing or carried the letter — from “Babylon” (1 Peter 5:13), most likely a code name for Rome. Church tradition says Peter was crucified there under Nero not long afterwards. When he tells suffering Christians to stand firm, he is preparing for his own martyrdom."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"James","intro":"James ended by pointing ahead to 1 Peter and 2 Peter. The two letters stand side by side among the letters written to the whole scattered church, and they share many themes — but where James tests faith by its works, Peter anchors faith in hope.","points":[{"title":"From ‘The Twelve Tribes in the Dispersion’ to ‘Foreigners in the Dispersion’","body":"James wrote “to the twelve tribes which are in the Dispersion” (James 1:1). Peter writes “to the chosen ones who are living as foreigners in the Dispersion” (1 Peter 1:1). Both use the same word, διασπορά, the scattering — a word that once described Israel in exile. Now it describes the whole church, Jewish and Gentile believers together, living as strangers in a world that is not their final home."},{"title":"From Faith Proved by Testing to Faith Proved by Fire","body":"James said, “knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance” (James 1:3). Peter uses the same word and adds the image: “that the proof of your faith, which is more precious than gold that perishes even though it is tested by fire, may be found to result in praise, glory, and honor at the revelation of Jesus Christ” (1 Peter 1:7). Both begin their letters with joy in trials. James shows what trials produce now; Peter shows what they will produce when Christ appears."},{"title":"From ‘Humble Yourselves’ to ‘Under the Mighty Hand of God’","body":"Both apostles quote the same Proverb. James wrote, “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble” (James 4:6), and “Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he will exalt you” (James 4:10). Peter writes, “Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time” (1 Peter 5:6), and then, like James, moves straight to resisting the devil (1 Peter 5:8–9). The two letters agree that humility and spiritual warfare go together."},{"title":"From Faith That Works to Good Works the World Can See","body":"James insisted that faith must be shown by works. Peter turns those works towards a watching world: “having good behavior among the nations, so in that of which they speak against you as evildoers, they may by your good works, which they see, glorify God in the day of visitation” (1 Peter 2:12). For Peter, a holy and gracious life is the church's answer to slander, and its most powerful witness."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"Born Again to a Living Hope — Through Suffering to Glory","anchorRef":"1 Peter 1:3; 4:12-13; 5:10","paragraphs":["The letter opens with praise: “Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to his great mercy caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (1 Peter 1:3). That living hope is the anchor of everything Peter says. Because Jesus rose, believers have a new birth, an imperishable inheritance and a future glory, and their present sufferings are short and purposeful.","Peter never pretends that suffering is pleasant. He writes, “Beloved, don’t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you, to test you, as though a strange thing happened to you” (1 Peter 4:12). “But because you are partakers of Christ’s sufferings, rejoice; that at the revelation of his glory you also may rejoice with exceeding joy” (1 Peter 4:13). The pattern is the pattern of Christ Himself — first the cross, then the crown. The prophets searched for it, “when he predicted the sufferings of Christ, and the glories that would follow them” (1 Peter 1:11).","The letter closes with a promise that gathers the whole theme: “But may the God of all grace, who called you to his eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a little while, perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you” (1 Peter 5:10). Suffering is ‘a little while’; glory is ‘eternal’. The God who allows the fire is the God of all grace, and He will finish what He began.","Peter's theme runs through the whole New Testament. Paul wrote, “For I consider that the sufferings of this present time are not worthy to be compared with the glory which will be revealed toward us” (Romans 8:18). Jesus told the disciples on the Emmaus road, “Didn’t the Christ have to suffer these things and to enter into his glory?” (Luke 24:26). And Peter, who once rebuked Jesus for speaking of the cross, now teaches the church to follow Him on the same road."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 5 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"Your Identity Is Secure","body":"Before Peter tells believers how to live, he tells them who they are: chosen by the Father, set apart by the Spirit, sprinkled with the blood of Jesus (1:2); born again to a living hope (1:3); “a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession” (1 Peter 2:9). The world may reject you, but God has chosen you. Your security does not depend on your circumstances."},{"title":"Holiness and Good Conduct Are Your Witness","body":"Peter calls believers to be holy because God is holy (1:15–16), to abstain from fleshly desires “which war against the soul” (1 Peter 2:11), and to live such good lives among unbelievers that slander is silenced. In marriage, at work and under authority, the believer's conduct is meant to point people to God. Your life is the first sermon most people will hear."},{"title":"Suffering With Christ Leads to Glory With Christ","body":"Peter does not explain away suffering. He shows that Christ suffered first — “Because Christ also suffered for sins once, the righteous for the unrighteous, that he might bring you to God” (1 Peter 3:18) — and that believers who suffer for doing good share in His sufferings and will share in His glory. You can cast all your anxieties on Him, “because he cares for you” (1 Peter 5:7)."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"Where Do You Feel Like a Foreigner?","body":"Where has following Jesus made you feel out of place — at work, in your family, among old friends, in your culture? Peter will tell you that this feeling is not a mistake but a sign of who you are. Bring that place to God, and read this letter as His word to exiles."},{"title":"What Fire Are You Facing?","body":"Name the trial, pressure, criticism or suffering you are facing because of your faith or simply because life is hard. As you read 1 Peter, look for how God wants to use it to prove your faith and prepare you for glory — and for the living hope that can hold you while it lasts."}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Blessed be You, God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to Your great mercy caused me to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus from the dead. As I open the first letter of Peter, I thank You that I am chosen, sprinkled with the blood of Jesus and kept by Your power for an inheritance that cannot fade. Where I feel like a foreigner in this world, remind me that my home is with You. Where I am facing fire, prove my faith and make it more precious than gold. Make me holy as You are holy. Let my good conduct silence slander and lead others to glorify You. Teach me to follow in the steps of Jesus, who suffered for me. Humble me under Your mighty hand, and help me cast all my anxieties on You, because You care for me. And when I have suffered a little while, perfect, establish, strengthen and settle me, as You have promised. Thank You that Peter, who once denied You, was restored to feed Your sheep. Feed me now through his words. In Jesus' name, Amen.”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Peter","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Born Again to a Living Hope","themeTitle":"“You Shall Be Holy; for I Am Holy”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ, to the chosen ones who are living as foreigners in the Dispersion in Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia,","understanding":"Πέτρος ἀπόστολος Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ” — the name Jesus gave Simon, Πέτρος, ‘rock’ (John 1:42). ἀπόστολος, one sent with authority. ἐκλεκτοῖς παρεπιδήμοις διασπορᾶς, “to the chosen ones who are living as foreigners in the Dispersion” — ἐκλεκτός, chosen by God; παρεπίδημος, a resident alien, someone living temporarily in a land that is not his home; διασπορά, the scattering, the word James used (James 1:1). The five regions — Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia and Bithynia — cover most of northern and western Asia Minor, perhaps listed in the order a messenger would travel.","words":[{"strongs":"G4074","hebrew":"Πέτρος","translit":"Pétros","english":"Peter"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen ones"},{"strongs":"G3927","hebrew":"παρεπίδημος","translit":"parepídēmos","english":"foreigners"},{"strongs":"G1290","hebrew":"διασπορά","translit":"diasporá","english":"Dispersion"}]},{"v":2,"text":"according to the foreknowledge of God the Father, in sanctification of the Spirit, that you may obey Jesus Christ and be sprinkled with his blood: Grace to you and peace be multiplied.","understanding":"κατὰ πρόγνωσιν θεοῦ πατρός, “according to the foreknowledge of God the Father” — πρόγνωσις, knowing beforehand, the loving choice of a Father, not mere information. ἐν ἁγιασμῷ πνεύματος, “in sanctification of the Spirit” — ἁγιασμός, being set apart as holy. εἰς ὑπακοὴν καὶ ῥαντισμὸν αἵματος Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “that you may obey Jesus Christ and be sprinkled with his blood” — ὑπακοή, obedience, and ῥαντισμός, the sprinkling of covenant blood, as at Sinai (Exodus 24:7–8): the people promised to obey, and were sprinkled. Father, Spirit and Son work together in one sentence. πληθυνθείη, “be multiplied” — grace and peace growing more and more.","words":[{"strongs":"G4268","hebrew":"πρόγνωσις","translit":"prógnōsis","english":"foreknowledge"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G38","hebrew":"ἁγιασμός","translit":"hagiasmós","english":"sanctification"},{"strongs":"G5218","hebrew":"ὑπακοή","translit":"hypakoḗ","english":"obey"},{"strongs":"G4473","hebrew":"ῥαντισμός","translit":"rhantismós","english":"sprinkled"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G4129","hebrew":"πληθύνω","translit":"plēthýnō","english":"multiplied"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to his great mercy caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead,","understanding":"εὐλογητὸς ὁ θεός, “Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ” — εὐλογητός, worthy of praise, a word used only of God. κατὰ τὸ πολὺ αὐτοῦ ἔλεος, “who according to his great mercy” — ἔλεος, from the mercy family: compassion that acts. ἀναγεννήσας ἡμᾶς, “caused us to be born again” — ἀναγεννάω, from the birth family: to beget again, Peter's word for what Jesus called being born ἄνωθεν (John 3:3). εἰς ἐλπίδα ζῶσαν, “to a living hope” — ζάω, from the life family: a hope that is alive because it rests on a living Lord, διὰ ἀναστάσεως, “through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” — ἀνάστασις, from the rise family.","words":[{"strongs":"G2128","hebrew":"εὐλογητός","translit":"eulogētós","english":"Blessed"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G1656","hebrew":"ἔλεος","translit":"éleos","english":"mercy"},{"strongs":"G313","hebrew":"ἀναγεννάω","translit":"anagennáō","english":"born again"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"}]},{"v":4,"text":"to an incorruptible and undefiled inheritance that doesn’t fade away, reserved in Heaven for you,","understanding":"εἰς κληρονομίαν, “to an … inheritance” — κληρονομία, the allotted portion, as each tribe received its land in Canaan. Three negatives describe it: ἄφθαρτος, “incorruptible” — it cannot decay; ἀμίαντος, “undefiled” — it cannot be stained, the word James used for pure religion (James 1:27); ἀμάραντος, “that doesn’t fade away” — it cannot wither, unlike the rich man who ‘fades away’ (μαραίνω, James 1:11). τετηρημένην ἐν οὐρανοῖς, “reserved in Heaven for you” — τηρέω, from the hold family: kept under guard, already waiting with your name on it.","words":[{"strongs":"G862","hebrew":"ἄφθαρτος","translit":"áphthartos","english":"incorruptible"},{"strongs":"G283","hebrew":"ἀμίαντος","translit":"amíantos","english":"undefiled"},{"strongs":"G2817","hebrew":"κληρονομία","translit":"klēronomía","english":"inheritance"},{"strongs":"G263","hebrew":"ἀμάραντος","translit":"amárantos","english":"doesn’t fade away"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"reserved"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"Heaven"}]},{"v":5,"text":"who by the power of God are guarded through faith for a salvation ready to be revealed in the last time.","understanding":"τοὺς ἐν δυνάμει θεοῦ φρουρουμένους, “who by the power of God are guarded” — δύναμις, from the power family: God's ability in action; φρουρέω, also from the hold family: a military word, to guard as a garrison guards a city. The inheritance is kept (τηρέω, v. 4); the heirs are guarded (φρουρέω). διὰ πίστεως, “through faith” — God's power works through your trust. εἰς σωτηρίαν ἑτοίμην ἀποκαλυφθῆναι ἐν καιρῷ ἐσχάτῳ, “for a salvation ready to be revealed in the last time” — ἀποκαλύπτω, from the appear family: to unveil what already exists; καιρός, from the time family: the appointed moment, not χρόνος, a span of time.","words":[{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5432","hebrew":"φρουρέω","translit":"phrouréō","english":"guarded"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G2092","hebrew":"ἕτοιμος","translit":"hétoimos","english":"ready"},{"strongs":"G601","hebrew":"ἀποκαλύπτω","translit":"apokalýptō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Wherein you greatly rejoice, though now for a little while, if need be, you have been put to grief in various trials,","understanding":"ἐν ᾧ ἀγαλλιᾶσθε, “Wherein you greatly rejoice” — ἀγαλλιάω, exultant, leaping joy. ὀλίγον ἄρτι, “though now for a little while” — the same ‘little’ James used of the vapour of life (James 4:14). εἰ δέον, “if need be” — trials are never random; there is a ‘must’ in God's purpose. λυπηθέντες ἐν ποικίλοις πειρασμοῖς, “you have been put to grief in various trials” — λυπέω, to cause grief; ποικίλοι πειρασμοί, the exact words of James 1:2, ‘various temptations’: πειρασμός, from the test family, here as outward trials.","words":[{"strongs":"G21","hebrew":"ἀγαλλιάω","translit":"agalliáō","english":"greatly rejoice"},{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"little"},{"strongs":"G3076","hebrew":"λυπέω","translit":"lypéō","english":"put to grief"},{"strongs":"G4164","hebrew":"ποικίλος","translit":"poikílos","english":"various"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"trials"}]},{"v":7,"text":"that the proof of your faith, which is more precious than gold that perishes even though it is tested by fire, may be found to result in praise, glory, and honor at the revelation of Jesus Christ—","understanding":"ἵνα τὸ δοκίμιον ὑμῶν τῆς πίστεως, “that the proof of your faith” — δοκίμιον, from the test family, the same word as James 1:3, here the result of the assay: genuine faith. πολυτιμότερον χρυσίου τοῦ ἀπολλυμένου, “more precious than gold that perishes” — χρυσίον, gold. διὰ πυρὸς δὲ δοκιμαζομένου, “even though it is tested by fire” — δοκιμάζω, the verb of the test family. If perishable gold is worth refining, how much more imperishable faith. It will result in ἔπαινος, δόξα, τιμή — “praise, glory, and honor” — ἐν ἀποκαλύψει Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “at the revelation of Jesus Christ” — ἀποκάλυψις, from the appear family: the unveiling of what is now hidden.","words":[{"strongs":"G1383","hebrew":"δοκίμιον","translit":"dokímion","english":"proof"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perishes"},{"strongs":"G1381","hebrew":"δοκιμάζω","translit":"dokimázō","english":"tested"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G1868","hebrew":"ἔπαινος","translit":"épainos","english":"praise"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"revelation"}]},{"v":8,"text":"whom not having known you love; in whom, though now you don’t see him, yet believing, you rejoice greatly with joy unspeakable and full of glory—","understanding":"ὃν οὐκ εἰδότες ἀγαπᾶτε, “whom not having known you love” — εἴδω, from the know family and the see family: the WEB follows manuscripts reading εἰδότες, ‘known’; most early manuscripts read ἰδόντες, ‘having seen’. Either way: unlike Peter, they never walked with Jesus in Galilee, yet they love Him — ἀγαπάω, from the love family. εἰς ὃν ἄρτι μὴ ὁρῶντες, “though now you don’t see him” — ὁράω, from the see family. πιστεύοντες δὲ ἀγαλλιᾶσθε χαρᾷ ἀνεκλαλήτῳ καὶ δεδοξασμένῃ, “yet believing, you rejoice greatly with joy unspeakable and full of glory” — ἀνεκλάλητος, beyond words; δοξάζω, glorified, joy already touched by heaven. Jesus called them blessed (John 20:29).","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"known"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G3708","hebrew":"ὁράω","translit":"horáō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believing"},{"strongs":"G21","hebrew":"ἀγαλλιάω","translit":"agalliáō","english":"rejoice greatly"},{"strongs":"G5479","hebrew":"χαρά","translit":"chará","english":"joy"},{"strongs":"G412","hebrew":"ἀνεκλάλητος","translit":"aneklálētos","english":"unspeakable"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"full of glory"}]},{"v":9,"text":"receiving the result of your faith, the salvation of your souls.","understanding":"κομιζόμενοι τὸ τέλος τῆς πίστεως, “receiving the result of your faith” — κομίζω, to carry off as a prize; τέλος, from the perfect family: the end, the goal faith was always aiming at, as in James 5:11. σωτηρίαν ψυχῶν, “the salvation of your souls” — ψυχή, from the life family: your whole self, the life that outlasts the body.","words":[{"strongs":"G2865","hebrew":"κομίζω","translit":"komízō","english":"receiving"},{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"result"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Concerning this salvation, the prophets sought and searched diligently, who prophesied of the grace that would come to you,","understanding":"περὶ ἧς σωτηρίας ἐξεζήτησαν καὶ ἐξηραύνησαν προφῆται, “Concerning this salvation, the prophets sought and searched diligently” — ἐκζητέω, to seek out; ἐξερευνάω, to search through, as a miner searches for ore. οἱ περὶ τῆς εἰς ὑμᾶς χάριτος προφητεύσαντες, “who prophesied of the grace that would come to you” — the Old Testament prophets spoke of a grace they themselves would not see.","words":[{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G1567","hebrew":"ἐκζητέω","translit":"ekzētéō","english":"sought"},{"strongs":"G1830","hebrew":"ἐξερευνάω","translit":"exereunáō","english":"searched diligently"},{"strongs":"G4395","hebrew":"προφητεύω","translit":"prophēteúō","english":"prophesied"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"}]},{"v":11,"text":"searching for who or what kind of time the Spirit of Christ, which was in them, pointed to, when he predicted the sufferings of Christ, and the glories that would follow them.","understanding":"ἐρευνῶντες εἰς τίνα ἢ ποῖον καιρόν, “searching for who or what kind of time” — καιρός, from the time family: the appointed season, as in verse 5. τὸ ἐν αὐτοῖς πνεῦμα Χριστοῦ, “the Spirit of Christ, which was in them” — the Spirit who inspired the prophets is the Spirit of Christ, a striking witness to Christ's pre-existence. προμαρτυρόμενον τὰ εἰς Χριστὸν παθήματα καὶ τὰς μετὰ ταῦτα δόξας, “when he predicted the sufferings of Christ, and the glories that would follow them” — πάθημα, from the suffer family: what one undergoes; δόξα, glory. The theme of the whole letter in one line: sufferings, then glories.","words":[{"strongs":"G2045","hebrew":"ἐρευνάω","translit":"ereunáō","english":"searching"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1213","hebrew":"δηλόω","translit":"dēlóō","english":"pointed to"},{"strongs":"G4303","hebrew":"προμαρτύρομαι","translit":"promartýromai","english":"predicted"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glories"}]},{"v":12,"text":"To them it was revealed, that not to themselves, but to you, they ministered these things, which now have been announced to you through those who preached the Good News to you by the Holy Spirit sent out from heaven; which things angels desire to look into.","understanding":"οἷς ἀπεκαλύφθη, “To them it was revealed” — ἀποκαλύπτω, from the appear family. ὅτι οὐχ ἑαυτοῖς ὑμῖν δὲ διηκόνουν αὐτά, “that not to themselves, but to you, they ministered these things” — διακονέω, from the servant family: the prophets were servants, waiting tables for a feast they would not eat. ἀνηγγέλη, “announced,” εὐαγγελισαμένων, “preached the Good News.” εἰς ἃ ἐπιθυμοῦσιν ἄγγελοι παρακύψαι, “which things angels desire to look into” — παρακύπτω, from the see family: to stoop and peer in, the word used of the disciples looking into the empty tomb and of the one who ‘looks into’ the perfect law (James 1:25). Even angels bend down to look at the gospel. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G601","hebrew":"ἀποκαλύπτω","translit":"apokalýptō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G1247","hebrew":"διακονέω","translit":"diakonéō","english":"ministered"},{"strongs":"G312","hebrew":"ἀναγγέλλω","translit":"anangéllō","english":"announced"},{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"preached the Good News"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1937","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμέω","translit":"epithyméō","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G3879","hebrew":"παρακύπτω","translit":"parakýptō","english":"look into"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Therefore prepare your minds for action, be sober, and set your hope fully on the grace that will be brought to you at the revelation of Jesus Christ—","understanding":"διὸ ἀναζωσάμενοι τὰς ὀσφύας τῆς διανοίας ὑμῶν, “Therefore prepare your minds for action” — literally ‘gird up the loins of your mind’: tuck your long robe into your belt so you can run, as Israel ate the Passover dressed for travel (Exodus 12:11). διάνοια, the thinking mind. νήφοντες, “be sober” — νήφω, from the self-control family: clear-headed, not intoxicated by anything. τελείως ἐλπίσατε, “set your hope fully” — τελείως, from the perfect family: completely, without holding anything back. ἐπὶ τὴν φερομένην ὑμῖν χάριν ἐν ἀποκαλύψει Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “on the grace that will be brought to you at the revelation of Jesus Christ” — ἀποκάλυψις, from the appear family, as in verse 7.","words":[{"strongs":"G328","hebrew":"ἀναζώννυμι","translit":"anazṓnnymi","english":"prepare"},{"strongs":"G1271","hebrew":"διάνοια","translit":"diánoia","english":"minds"},{"strongs":"G3525","hebrew":"νήφω","translit":"nḗphō","english":"be sober"},{"strongs":"G5049","hebrew":"τελείως","translit":"teleíōs","english":"fully"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"set your hope"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"revelation"}]},{"v":14,"text":"as children of obedience, not conforming yourselves according to your former lusts as in your ignorance,","understanding":"ὡς τέκνα ὑπακοῆς, “as children of obedience” — a Hebrew way of saying ‘obedient children’, children whose family likeness is obedience. μὴ συσχηματιζόμενοι, “not conforming yourselves” — συσχηματίζω, from the image family: to shape yourself to an outward mould (σχῆμα), the word Paul uses in Romans 12:2. ταῖς πρότερον ἐν τῇ ἀγνοίᾳ ὑμῶν ἐπιθυμίαις, “according to your former lusts as in your ignorance” — ἐπιθυμία, desire; ἄγνοια, the ignorance of people who did not yet know God, a sign that many readers were Gentiles. τέκνον is from the children family — a child — one born of a parent, family by birth.","words":[{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G5218","hebrew":"ὑπακοή","translit":"hypakoḗ","english":"obedience"},{"strongs":"G4964","hebrew":"συσχηματίζω","translit":"syschēmatízō","english":"conforming"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G52","hebrew":"ἄγνοια","translit":"ágnoia","english":"ignorance"}]},{"v":15,"text":"but just as he who called you is holy, you yourselves also be holy in all of your behavior;","understanding":"ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὸν καλέσαντα ὑμᾶς ἅγιον, “but just as he who called you is holy” — καλέω, to call, God's effective summons. ἅγιος, holy: set apart, different, belonging wholly to God. καὶ αὐτοὶ ἅγιοι ἐν πάσῃ ἀναστροφῇ γενήθητε, “you yourselves also be holy in all of your behavior” — ἀναστροφή, way of life, the same word James used for ‘good conduct’ (James 3:13). Holiness is not one compartment of life; it is ‘all’. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"behavior"}]},{"v":16,"text":"because it is written, “You shall be holy; for I am holy.”","understanding":"διότι γέγραπται, “because it is written, ‘You shall be holy; for I am holy.’” — from Leviticus 11:44–45 and 19:2. ἅγιος twice. The command rests on God's character: His people are to resemble Him. The holiness code once separated Israel from the nations by food and ritual; now it separates the church from the world by a holy life. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"}]},{"v":17,"text":"If you call on him as Father, who without respect of persons judges according to each man’s work, pass the time of your living as foreigners here in reverent fear:","understanding":"καὶ εἰ πατέρα ἐπικαλεῖσθε, “If you call on him as Father” — ἐπικαλέομαι, to call upon in prayer. τὸν ἀπροσωπολήμπτως κρίνοντα, “who without respect of persons judges” — ἀπροσωπολήπτως, without ‘receiving faces’, the opposite of the favouritism James condemned (James 2:1). κατὰ τὸ ἑκάστου ἔργον, “according to each man’s work” — ἔργον, from the work family: the whole of a life's work, singular. ἐν φόβῳ τὸν τῆς παροικίας ὑμῶν χρόνον ἀναστράφητε, “pass the time of your living as foreigners here in reverent fear” — χρόνος, from the time family: the span of your stay, not καιρός, the appointed moment of verses 5 and 11; παροικία, temporary residence. Your Father is also the Judge, and your time here is short. κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G1941","hebrew":"ἐπικαλέομαι","translit":"epikaléomai","english":"call on"},{"strongs":"G678","hebrew":"ἀπροσωπολήπτως","translit":"aprosōpolḗptōs","english":"without respect of persons"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judges"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"work"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"reverent fear"},{"strongs":"G3940","hebrew":"παροικία","translit":"paroikía","english":"living as foreigners"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G390","hebrew":"ἀναστρέφω","translit":"anastréphō","english":"pass"}]},{"v":18,"text":"knowing that you were redeemed, not with corruptible things, with silver or gold, from the useless way of life handed down from your fathers,","understanding":"εἰδότες ὅτι οὐ φθαρτοῖς, ἀργυρίῳ ἢ χρυσίῳ, ἐλυτρώθητε, “knowing that you were redeemed, not with corruptible things, with silver or gold” — εἰδότες, from εἴδω, of the know family (and the see family): knowing as settled fact. λυτρόω, from the redeem family: to set free by paying a ransom, the price paid for a slave or captive. φθαρτός, perishable, the opposite of the ‘incorruptible’ inheritance (v. 4). ἐκ τῆς ματαίας ὑμῶν ἀναστροφῆς πατροπαραδότου, “from the useless way of life handed down from your fathers” — μάταιος, empty, the word James used for worthless religion (James 1:26). Tradition is not freedom; Christ is.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G3084","hebrew":"λυτρόω","translit":"lytróō","english":"redeemed"},{"strongs":"G5349","hebrew":"φθαρτός","translit":"phthartós","english":"corruptible"},{"strongs":"G694","hebrew":"ἀργύριον","translit":"argýrion","english":"silver"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G3152","hebrew":"μάταιος","translit":"mátaios","english":"useless"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"way of life"},{"strongs":"G3970","hebrew":"πατροπαράδοτος","translit":"patroparádotos","english":"handed down from your fathers"}]},{"v":19,"text":"but with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ;","understanding":"ἀλλὰ τιμίῳ αἵματι, “but with precious blood” — τίμιος, of great value, honoured. ὡς ἀμνοῦ ἀμώμου καὶ ἀσπίλου Χριστοῦ, “as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” — ἀμνός, a lamb, the Passover lamb that had to be perfect (Exodus 12:5), and the Lamb John the Baptist pointed to (John 1:29). ἄμωμος, without inner defect; ἄσπιλος, without outward spot, the word James used for being ‘unstained by the world’ (James 1:27). ἀμνός is from the lamb family — a lamb, especially the sacrificial lamb.","words":[{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G129","hebrew":"αἷμα","translit":"haîma","english":"blood"},{"strongs":"G286","hebrew":"ἀμνός","translit":"amnós","english":"lamb"},{"strongs":"G299","hebrew":"ἄμωμος","translit":"ámōmos","english":"without blemish"},{"strongs":"G784","hebrew":"ἄσπιλος","translit":"áspilos","english":"spot"}]},{"v":20,"text":"who was foreknown indeed before the foundation of the world, but was revealed in this last age for your sake,","understanding":"προεγνωσμένου μὲν πρὸ καταβολῆς κόσμου, “who was foreknown indeed before the foundation of the world” — προγινώσκω, to know beforehand, as in verse 2; καταβολή, laying down a foundation; κόσμος, from the world family: the ordered creation. The cross was not God's emergency plan. φανερωθέντος δὲ ἐπ᾽ ἐσχάτου τῶν χρόνων, “but was revealed in this last age for your sake” — φανερόω, from the appear family: to make visible, to bring into the open, as distinct from ἀποκαλύπτω (to unveil, vv. 5, 12). χρόνος, from the time family, here rendered ‘age’: the last span of time before the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G4267","hebrew":"προγινώσκω","translit":"proginṓskō","english":"foreknown"},{"strongs":"G2602","hebrew":"καταβολή","translit":"katabolḗ","english":"foundation"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"age"}]},{"v":21,"text":"who through him are believers in God, who raised him from the dead, and gave him glory; so that your faith and hope might be in God.","understanding":"τοὺς δι᾽ αὐτοῦ πιστοὺς εἰς θεόν, “who through him are believers in God” — πιστός, faithful, believing. τὸν ἐγείραντα αὐτὸν ἐκ νεκρῶν καὶ δόξαν αὐτῷ δόντα, “who raised him from the dead, and gave him glory” — ἐγείρω, from the rise family: to raise up. Sufferings, then glory (v. 11). ὥστε τὴν πίστιν ὑμῶν καὶ ἐλπίδα εἶναι εἰς θεόν, “so that your faith and hope might be in God” — faith rests on what God has done; hope rests on what He will do.","words":[{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"believers"},{"strongs":"G1453","hebrew":"ἐγείρω","translit":"egeírō","english":"raised"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"}]},{"v":22,"text":"Seeing you have purified your souls in your obedience to the truth through the Spirit in sincere brotherly affection, love one another from the heart fervently:","understanding":"τὰς ψυχὰς ὑμῶν ἡγνικότες, “Seeing you have purified your souls” — ψυχή, from the life family: the whole inner self; ἁγνίζω, to make clean, the word James used: ‘purify your hearts’ (James 4:8). ἐν τῇ ὑπακοῇ τῆς ἀληθείας, “in your obedience to the truth” — conversion is obedience to the gospel. εἰς φιλαδελφίαν ἀνυπόκριτον, “in sincere brotherly affection” — φιλαδελφία, from the love family: the warm family affection of brothers and sisters; ἀνυπόκριτος, unfeigned. ἐκ καρδίας ἀλλήλους ἀγαπήσατε ἐκτενῶς, “love one another from the heart fervently” — ἀγαπάω, also from the love family: the committed, self-giving love of the will. Peter puts the two side by side: you already have φιλαδελφία; now stretch it into ἀγάπη. ἐκτενῶς, ‘stretched out’, like a runner straining for the line.","words":[{"strongs":"G48","hebrew":"ἁγνίζω","translit":"hagnízō","english":"purified"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G5218","hebrew":"ὑπακοή","translit":"hypakoḗ","english":"obedience"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G505","hebrew":"ἀνυπόκριτος","translit":"anypókritos","english":"sincere"},{"strongs":"G5360","hebrew":"φιλαδελφία","translit":"philadelphía","english":"brotherly affection"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G1619","hebrew":"ἐκτενῶς","translit":"ektenōs","english":"fervently"}]},{"v":23,"text":"having been born again, not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible, through the word of God, which lives and remains forever.","understanding":"ἀναγεγεννημένοι, “having been born again” — ἀναγεννάω, from the birth family, as in verse 3. οὐκ ἐκ σπορᾶς φθαρτῆς ἀλλὰ ἀφθάρτου, “not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible” — σπορά, seed, sowing. διὰ λόγου ζῶντος θεοῦ καὶ μένοντος, “through the word of God, which lives and remains forever” — λόγος, from the word family: the whole message God has spoken; ζάω, from the life family. The new life is as permanent as the seed it grows from. James said the same: God ‘gave birth to us by the word of truth’ (James 1:18).","words":[{"strongs":"G313","hebrew":"ἀναγεννάω","translit":"anagennáō","english":"born again"},{"strongs":"G5349","hebrew":"φθαρτός","translit":"phthartós","english":"corruptible"},{"strongs":"G4701","hebrew":"σπορά","translit":"sporá","english":"seed"},{"strongs":"G862","hebrew":"ἄφθαρτος","translit":"áphthartos","english":"incorruptible"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"lives"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"remains"}]},{"v":24,"text":"For, “All flesh is like grass, and all of man’s glory like the flower in the grass. The grass withers, and its flower falls;","understanding":"“For, ‘All flesh is like grass, and all of man’s glory like the flower in the grass.’” — from Isaiah 40:6–8, the passage James also echoed (James 1:10–11). σάρξ, human life in its frailty; χόρτος, grass; ἄνθος, flower. ἐξηράνθη ὁ χόρτος καὶ τὸ ἄνθος ἐξέπεσεν, “The grass withers, and its flower falls” — ξηραίνω, to dry up; ἐκπίπτω, to fall off.","words":[{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G5528","hebrew":"χόρτος","translit":"chórtos","english":"grass"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G438","hebrew":"ἄνθος","translit":"ánthos","english":"flower"},{"strongs":"G3583","hebrew":"ξηραίνω","translit":"xēraínō","english":"withers"},{"strongs":"G1601","hebrew":"ἐκπίπτω","translit":"ekpíptō","english":"falls"}]},{"v":25,"text":"but the Lord’s word endures forever.” This is the word of Good News which was preached to you.","understanding":"τὸ δὲ ῥῆμα κυρίου μένει εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα, “but the Lord’s word endures forever” — ῥῆμα, from the word family: the spoken word, a particular utterance, as distinct from λόγος in verse 23, the whole message. Peter uses both. αἰών, in both the time family and the world family: the age, unending, as distinct from κόσμος, the ordered world. τοῦτο δέ ἐστιν τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ εὐαγγελισθὲν εἰς ὑμᾶς, “This is the word of Good News which was preached to you” — the eternal word of Isaiah is the very gospel these exiles heard.","words":[{"strongs":"G4487","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G3306","hebrew":"μένω","translit":"ménō","english":"endures"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"preached"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Inheritance in the Vault","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture a great vault deep inside a fortress. Inside, on a table, lies a sealed document with your name on it — the title to an inheritance. Moths cannot reach it, rust cannot touch it, thieves cannot break in. Outside the vault, soldiers stand guard. And on the road towards the fortress, you yourself are walking, surrounded by a second company of soldiers who guard you every step of the journey home.","meaning":"“to an incorruptible and undefiled inheritance that doesn’t fade away, reserved in Heaven for you” (v. 4), “who by the power of God are guarded through faith for a salvation ready to be revealed in the last time” (v. 5). God guards both ends: the inheritance is kept for you, and you are kept for the inheritance. Your living hope does not depend on how strong you feel today, but on the power of God that guards you."},{"title":"Gold in the Furnace","verseRef":"v. 6-7","image":"Picture a goldsmith's furnace glowing white-hot. He drops a lump of ore into the crucible. It melts, bubbles, and impurities rise to the surface. He skims them off, again and again, until the gold is pure. Then he holds it up to the light — and it gleams. Yet even that refined gold will one day be lost or melted down. What Peter describes is more precious still.","meaning":"“that the proof of your faith, which is more precious than gold that perishes even though it is tested by fire, may be found to result in praise, glory, and honor at the revelation of Jesus Christ” (v. 7). If people put gold through fire to prove it, God will put faith through fire to prove it — and faith, unlike gold, lasts forever. Your trial is ‘for a little while’ (v. 6); its result is eternal."},{"title":"Angels Bending Down","verseRef":"v. 10-12","image":"Picture the prophets of old bent over their scrolls by lamplight, searching their own words for clues — when will Messiah come? Who will He be? What will His sufferings mean? Then picture the angels of heaven leaning over the edge of eternity, stooping to look into what God has done for human beings, longing to understand it.","meaning":"“Concerning this salvation, the prophets sought and searched diligently, who prophesied of the grace that would come to you” (v. 10). Their message was “not to themselves, but to you” (v. 12), and it contains “things angels desire to look into” (v. 12). You hold in your hands what prophets longed to see and angels long to understand. Never treat the gospel as ordinary."},{"title":"The Spotless Lamb","verseRef":"v. 18-19","image":"Picture a slave market in a Roman city. A man stands in chains, waiting to be sold. A stranger steps forward and pays the price — not with a bag of silver, but by giving his own life. The chains fall off. Then picture an Israelite family on Passover night, choosing a perfect, unblemished lamb, and painting its blood on their doorposts.","meaning":"“knowing that you were redeemed, not with corruptible things, with silver or gold, from the useless way of life handed down from your fathers” (v. 18), “but with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” (v. 19). You were bought out of an empty, inherited way of life by the most precious price in the universe. That is why you can live differently."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting (v. 1-2)","body":"Peter, an apostle, writes to the chosen exiles scattered through Asia Minor, chosen by the Father, set apart by the Spirit, for obedience to Jesus Christ and sprinkling with His blood."},{"label":"A Living Hope (v. 3-9)","body":"Praise God, who gave new birth to a living hope through the resurrection, to an imperishable inheritance kept in heaven for those guarded by His power; though grieved by trials for a little while, their tested faith will bring praise and glory when Christ is revealed; they love and trust Him without seeing Him, receiving the salvation of their souls."},{"label":"The Prophets and the Angels (v. 10-12)","body":"The prophets searched for the time of Christ's sufferings and glories, serving not themselves but us; angels long to look into the gospel now preached by the Spirit."},{"label":"Be Holy (v. 13-21)","body":"With minds prepared and sober, set your hope fully on the coming grace; as obedient children, do not conform to former desires but be holy in all conduct, because God is holy; live in reverent fear, knowing you were redeemed not with silver or gold but with the precious blood of Christ, the spotless Lamb foreknown before creation."},{"label":"Love from a Pure Heart (v. 22-25)","body":"Having purified yourselves by obeying the truth, love one another earnestly, for you have been born again of imperishable seed through the living and enduring word of God, the gospel preached to you."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Living Hope for People Without a Home","paragraphs":["Peter begins by telling his readers who they are. They are ‘foreigners’ and ‘scattered’ — words that describe their social situation. But they are also “chosen ones” (v. 1), chosen by the Father, set apart by the Spirit, sprinkled with the blood of Jesus (v. 2). Their identity is not defined by their neighbours' opinion but by the work of the Trinity. Before Peter gives a single command, he establishes their security.","Then he bursts into praise: “Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to his great mercy caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (v. 3). Every word matters. New birth is God's mercy, not human achievement. The hope is ‘living’ because it rests on a risen Saviour. Peter knew what a dead hope felt like: after the crucifixion, he and the other disciples had hidden behind locked doors. The resurrection made their hope alive again — and Peter never forgot it.","This hope looks forward to an inheritance that cannot decay, be stained or fade (v. 4). Everything in this world does all three. But Peter adds something even more comforting: believers themselves are “guarded through faith” by the power of God (v. 5). The inheritance is kept for you, and you are kept for it. Paul says the same: “And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your thoughts in Christ Jesus” (Philippians 4:7) — using the same military word."]},{"heading":"Joy and Grief Together","paragraphs":["Peter is honest: believers both rejoice and grieve. “Wherein you greatly rejoice, though now for a little while, if need be, you have been put to grief in various trials” (v. 6). Joy does not cancel grief, and grief does not cancel joy. Both are real at the same time. The phrase ‘if need be’ is important: trials are not random. God permits them for a purpose.","That purpose is the proving of faith (v. 7). Peter uses the same words as James — ‘various trials’ (James 1:2) and ‘the testing of your faith’ (James 1:3) — and adds the picture of gold in the furnace. The goal is not destruction but revelation: “praise, glory, and honor at the revelation of Jesus Christ” (v. 7). On that day, the faith that held on in the fire will be honoured by the One it trusted.","Then Peter says something deeply personal: “whom not having known you love; in whom, though now you don’t see him, yet believing, you rejoice greatly with joy unspeakable and full of glory” (v. 8). Peter had seen Jesus — walked with Him, eaten with Him, denied Him and been restored by Him. His readers had never seen Him. Yet they loved Him just as truly. Jesus had told Thomas, “Blessed are those who have not seen, and have believed” (John 20:29). That blessing belongs to every believer who reads this letter."]},{"heading":"Holy Because He Is Holy","paragraphs":["With verse 13 the letter turns from what God has done to how believers should live. The first command is about the mind: “Therefore prepare your minds for action, be sober, and set your hope fully on the grace that will be brought to you at the revelation of Jesus Christ” (v. 13). Before you can live differently, you must think differently — alert, clear-headed and fixed on the future grace.","The second command is holiness: “but just as he who called you is holy, you yourselves also be holy in all of your behavior” (v. 15). This is not a new idea; it comes from Leviticus. But it has a new setting. Once, Israel was set apart from the nations by food laws and rituals. Now, the church is set apart by its whole way of life. Peter's readers had been shaped by “your former lusts as in your ignorance” (v. 14). Now they are to be shaped by the character of the God who called them. Hebrews says the same: “Follow after peace with all men, and the sanctification without which no man will see the Lord” (Hebrews 12:14).","The motive for holiness is the cost of redemption. “knowing that you were redeemed, not with corruptible things, with silver or gold” (v. 18), “but with precious blood, as of a lamb without blemish or spot, the blood of Christ” (v. 19). Peter had once tried to stop Jesus going to the cross (Matthew 16:22). Now he understands that the cross was planned “before the foundation of the world” (v. 20). The price was infinite, so the purchase is precious. You cannot treat lightly a life that cost the blood of God's Son."]},{"heading":"Born of an Imperishable Word","paragraphs":["The chapter ends with love and the word. “Seeing you have purified your souls in your obedience to the truth through the Spirit in sincere brotherly affection, love one another from the heart fervently” (v. 22). Peter uses two love words: φιλαδελφία, the warm affection of family, which they already have, and ἀγάπη, the committed love of the will, which must be stretched to its full length. Church family affection must grow into fervent, sacrificial love.","The reason is their new birth: “having been born again, not of corruptible seed, but of incorruptible, through the word of God, which lives and remains forever” (v. 23). Peter quotes Isaiah to show the contrast between human frailty and God's permanence: “For, ‘All flesh is like grass, and all of man’s glory like the flower in the grass. The grass withers, and its flower falls’” (v. 24), “but the Lord’s word endures forever” (v. 25). Everything human fades; God's word does not. And that eternal word is not far away: “This is the word of Good News which was preached to you” (v. 25)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"New Birth, the Lamb and the Seeking Prophets","paragraphs":["Peter's teaching on new birth echoes Jesus' words to Nicodemus: “Most certainly, I tell you, unless one is born anew, he can’t see God’s Kingdom” (John 3:3). James uses a different verb for the same truth: “Of his own will he gave birth to us by the word of truth” (James 1:18). Both James and Peter tie new birth to God's word — the seed that produces life.","The spotless Lamb of verse 19 is the Lamb John the Baptist announced: “Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!” (John 1:29). Hebrews explains what His blood achieved: “nor yet through the blood of goats and calves, but through his own blood, entered in once for all into the Holy Place, having obtained eternal redemption” (Hebrews 9:12). And in heaven the redeemed sing: “for you were killed, and bought us for God with your blood, out of every tribe, language, people, and nation” (Revelation 5:9). Peter's ‘precious blood’ is heaven's eternal song.","The searching prophets of verses 10–12 recall the risen Jesus on the road to Emmaus: “Didn’t the Christ have to suffer these things and to enter into his glory?” (Luke 24:26). “Beginning from Moses and from all the prophets, he explained to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning himself” (Luke 24:27). Peter heard Jesus explain the same things after the resurrection (Acts 1:3). The sufferings and the glories that the prophets glimpsed are the heart of the gospel Peter now preaches — and the pattern his readers must follow."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Born again","hebrew":"ἀναγεννάω","translit":"anagennáō","strongs":"G313 (born γεννάω, gennáō, G1080)","verses":"v. 3, 23","insight":"Strong's: “to beget or (by extension) bear (again).” ἀναγεννάω appears only twice in the New Testament, both times in this chapter. It describes what Jesus told Nicodemus, using γεννάω with ἄνωθεν: a birth from above, a second beginning. Peter emphasises the cause (God's great mercy, v. 3), the means (the living word, v. 23) and the result (a living hope and an imperishable inheritance). This study gathers these words into a new birth family. New birth is not a religious upgrade; it is a new life from a new Father."},{"english":"Guarded","hebrew":"φρουρέω","translit":"phrouréō","strongs":"G5432 (reserved τηρέω, tēréō, G5083)","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “to be a watcher in advance, i.e. to mount guard as a sentinel (post spies at gates); figuratively, to hem in, protect.” φρουρέω is a military word: a garrison guarding a city. Paul uses it of the peace of God that guards hearts (Philippians 4:7). Peter pairs it with τηρέω in verse 4 — both from the hold family. The inheritance is kept; the heirs are guarded. You are not holding on to God by your own strength; His power is holding you."},{"english":"Foreigners","hebrew":"παρεπίδημος","translit":"parepídēmos","strongs":"G3927 (temporary residence παροικία, paroikía, G3940)","verses":"v. 1, 17; 2:11","insight":"Strong's: “an alien alongside, i.e. a resident foreigner.” παρεπίδημος describes someone living away from home, a sojourner. Peter uses it with παροικία (v. 17), a temporary stay in a place that is not your country. Abraham called himself a stranger and sojourner among the Hittites (Genesis 23:4), and Hebrews says the heroes of faith confessed they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth. Christians are not citizens who happen to be religious; they are citizens of heaven living for a time abroad."},{"english":"Redeemed","hebrew":"λυτρόω","translit":"lytróō","strongs":"G3084 (ransom λύτρον, lýtron, G3083)","verses":"v. 18","insight":"Strong's: “to ransom (literally or figuratively).” λυτρόω comes from λύτρον, the ransom price paid to free a slave or prisoner. Jesus said the Son of Man came “to give his life as a ransom for many” (Matthew 20:28). Peter stresses the price: not silver or gold, which perish, but precious blood. This study adds a new redeem family, with the market words ἀγοράζω and ἐξαγοράζω. Redemption is not merely forgiveness; it is a change of ownership."},{"english":"Word","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","strongs":"G4487 (word λόγος, lógos, G3056)","verses":"v. 23, 25","insight":"Strong's: “an utterance (individually, collectively or specially).” Peter uses both members of the word family within three verses. In verse 23 it is λόγος — the word of God as a whole message, the seed of new life. In verse 25, quoting Isaiah, it is ῥῆμα — the word spoken, a particular utterance. And he identifies that ῥῆμα with the gospel preached to them. The message as a whole and every spoken promise within it share the same quality: they live and remain forever."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is lifting your eyes from your circumstances to your inheritance — and calling you to live a holy life that matches your new birth.","items":[{"title":"Your Hope Is Alive","body":"Is your hope tired, fading or almost dead? The Spirit reminds you that your hope is living because Jesus is alive. “who according to his great mercy caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (v. 3). Whatever has died in your life — a dream, a relationship, a plan — your hope in Christ has not. Ask Him to breathe life into your hope again today."},{"title":"You Are Being Guarded","body":"You may feel that you are barely holding on. The Spirit wants you to know that God is holding you. You are “guarded through faith” by the power of God (v. 5). Your trial is ‘for a little while’; your inheritance is forever. Where have you been trusting your own strength to keep going? Rest in His."},{"title":"Holy in All Your Conduct","body":"The Spirit is gently pointing to an area where you are still being shaped by “your former lusts” (v. 14) instead of by God's holiness. It may be a habit, a pattern of speech, a way of spending money, or an inherited way of life from your family or culture. “you yourselves also be holy in all of your behavior” (v. 15). You were bought with precious blood. What does He want you to lay down?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Hope Check","body":"Where is your hope set — on your plans, your finances, your relationships, or “fully on the grace that will be brought to you at the revelation of Jesus Christ” (v. 13)? What would change if your hope were fully fixed on His coming?"},{"title":"The Conformity Check","body":"In what areas are you still “conforming yourselves according to your former lusts” (v. 14)? What ‘useless way of life handed down from your fathers’ (v. 18) still shapes you?"},{"title":"The Love Check","body":"Is your love for other believers sincere and fervent (v. 22)? Who in your church family do you find hard to love from the heart? What would ‘stretched out’ love look like towards them?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write Your Inheritance Statement","body":"Tonight, write at the top of a page: ‘My inheritance — incorruptible, undefiled, unfading, kept in heaven for me.’ Below it, list the things in your life that are perishing (possessions, health, status, plans) and, opposite them, the things God has promised that cannot perish. Read verses 3–9 aloud over the list and thank God for each promise. Keep it in your Bible for days of trial."},{"title":"A Week of Holy Conduct","body":"Choose one area of your conduct — speech, entertainment, money, work, relationships — and for one week ask before every decision in that area: ‘Is this holy, as He who called me is holy?’ (v. 15). Write down one change you made each day. At the end of the week, thank Christ for the precious blood that bought you, and ask Him to make the change permanent."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Blessed be You, God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ. According to Your great mercy You caused me to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus from the dead. Thank You for an inheritance that is incorruptible, undefiled and unfading, kept in heaven for me, and thank You that by Your power I am guarded through faith until the salvation that is ready to be revealed. When I am grieved by various trials for a little while, let my faith be proved like gold in the fire and found to bring praise and glory to Jesus at His revelation. I have not seen Him, yet I love Him; I do not see Him now, yet I believe and rejoice with joy beyond words. Prepare my mind for action, make me sober, and help me set my hope fully on the grace that is coming. You are holy, Lord, so make me holy in all my conduct. Thank You that I was redeemed not with silver or gold but with the precious blood of Christ, the spotless Lamb. Let me love my brothers and sisters fervently from a pure heart, because I have been born again of the imperishable seed of Your living and enduring word. In Jesus' name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Peter's Dead Hope Comes Alive","ref":"Luke 24:1-12, 33-35; John 20:1-10","relatedVerses":"v. 3, 8, 21","summary":"After the crucifixion Peter's hope was dead. He had denied Jesus three times and wept bitterly; now his Lord was in a tomb. On the first day of the week the women brought word that the tomb was empty. Peter ran to it, stooped down and saw the linen cloths lying by themselves, and went home wondering. That evening the disciples were saying, “The Lord is risen indeed, and has appeared to Simon!” (Luke 24:34). When Peter later writes that God has caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection, he is writing about the day his own hope came back to life."},{"title":"The Passover Lamb","ref":"Exodus 12:1-13","relatedVerses":"v. 18-19","summary":"On the night God brought Israel out of Egypt, each household was to take a lamb ‘without blemish, a male a year old’, keep it for four days, then kill it at twilight and put its blood on the doorposts. That night the Lord passed through Egypt in judgement, but when He saw the blood He passed over the house. The people ate the meal dressed for travel, with their belts fastened and their sandals on — ready to leave slavery behind. Peter's words echo the whole scene: redeemed by the blood of a lamb without blemish (v. 19), minds girded for action (v. 13), and called out of an old way of life as pilgrims (vv. 1, 17)."},{"title":"Peter Restored to Feed the Sheep","ref":"John 21:15-19","relatedVerses":"v. 8, 22","summary":"By the Sea of Galilee the risen Jesus asked Peter three times whether he loved Him. The first two times He used ἀγαπάω, and Peter answered with φιλέω, ‘you know that I have affection for you’; the third time Jesus used Peter's own word. Each time He gave a command: “Feed my lambs” (John 21:15), “Tend my sheep” (John 21:16), “Feed my sheep” (John 21:17). First Peter is that command being obeyed. The man who was asked about love now tells the flock, “love one another from the heart fervently” (v. 22) — and speaks of a Christ whom they love though they have not seen Him."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Peter","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"Living Stones, a Royal Priesthood, and the Steps of Christ","themeTitle":"“You Are a Chosen Race, a Royal Priesthood, a Holy Nation”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Putting away therefore all wickedness, all deceit, hypocrisies, envies, and all evil speaking,","understanding":"ἀποθέμενοι οὖν, “Putting away therefore” — the same verb James used, like taking off dirty clothes (James 1:21). Five things to strip off: κακία, “wickedness” — from the evil family: malice, ill-will; δόλος, “deceit” — bait, trickery; ὑπόκρισις, “hypocrisies” — acting a part; φθόνος, “envies”; καταλαλιά, “evil speaking” — slander, talking people down, as in James 4:11. Every one of them poisons the love commanded in 1:22.","words":[{"strongs":"G659","hebrew":"ἀποτίθημι","translit":"apotíthēmi","english":"Putting away"},{"strongs":"G2549","hebrew":"κακία","translit":"kakía","english":"wickedness"},{"strongs":"G1388","hebrew":"δόλος","translit":"dólos","english":"deceit"},{"strongs":"G5272","hebrew":"ὑπόκρισις","translit":"hypókrisis","english":"hypocrisies"},{"strongs":"G5355","hebrew":"φθόνος","translit":"phthónos","english":"envies"},{"strongs":"G2636","hebrew":"καταλαλία","translit":"katalalía","english":"evil speaking"}]},{"v":2,"text":"as newborn babies, long for the pure milk of the Word, that with it you may grow,","understanding":"ὡς ἀρτιγέννητα βρέφη, “as newborn babies” — ἀρτιγέννητος, from the birth family: just born, following the new birth of 1:23. τὸ λογικὸν ἄδολον γάλα ἐπιποθήσατε, “long for the pure milk of the Word” — ἐπιποθέω, to crave intensely, as a baby cries for milk. ἄδολος, ‘without deceit’, the opposite of δόλος in verse 1. λογικός can mean ‘rational’ or ‘spiritual’ (Romans 12:1 ‘spiritual service’); the WEB renders it ‘of the Word’, connecting it with the λόγος of 1:23 — the nourishment is God's word. ἵνα ἐν αὐτῷ αὐξηθῆτε, “that with it you may grow” — many manuscripts add ‘to salvation’.","words":[{"strongs":"G738","hebrew":"ἀρτιγέννητος","translit":"artigénnētos","english":"newborn"},{"strongs":"G1025","hebrew":"βρέφος","translit":"bréphos","english":"babies"},{"strongs":"G1971","hebrew":"ἐπιποθέω","translit":"epipothéō","english":"long for"},{"strongs":"G97","hebrew":"ἄδολος","translit":"ádolos","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G1051","hebrew":"γάλα","translit":"gála","english":"milk"},{"strongs":"G3050","hebrew":"λογικός","translit":"logikós","english":"of the Word"},{"strongs":"G837","hebrew":"αὐξάνω","translit":"auxánō","english":"grow"}]},{"v":3,"text":"if indeed you have tasted that the Lord is gracious:","understanding":"εἰ ἐγεύσασθε ὅτι χρηστὸς ὁ κύριος, “if indeed you have tasted that the Lord is gracious” — γεύομαι, to taste by experience; χρηστός, from the good family: kind, gracious, good in its kindness, as distinct from ἀγαθός (useful good) and καλός (beautiful good). An echo of Psalm 34:8. Some early Christians heard a wordplay: χρηστός (kind) and Χριστός (Christ).","words":[{"strongs":"G1089","hebrew":"γεύομαι","translit":"geúomai","english":"tasted"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G5543","hebrew":"χρηστός","translit":"chrēstós","english":"gracious"}]},{"v":4,"text":"coming to him, a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God, precious.","understanding":"πρὸς ὃν προσερχόμενοι, “coming to him” — προσέρχομαι, the word for approaching God in worship (Hebrews 4:16). λίθον ζῶντα, “a living stone” — ζάω, from the life family: Christ the risen stone. ὑπὸ ἀνθρώπων μὲν ἀποδεδοκιμασμένον, “rejected indeed by men” — ἀποδοκιμάζω, from the test family: tested and thrown out as unfit. παρὰ δὲ θεῷ ἐκλεκτὸν ἔντιμον, “but chosen by God, precious” — what the builders discarded, God selected.","words":[{"strongs":"G4334","hebrew":"προσέρχομαι","translit":"prosérchomai","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stone"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G593","hebrew":"ἀποδοκιμάζω","translit":"apodokimázō","english":"rejected"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen"},{"strongs":"G1784","hebrew":"ἔντιμος","translit":"éntimos","english":"precious"}]},{"v":5,"text":"You also, as living stones, are built up as a spiritual house, to be a holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God through Jesus Christ.","understanding":"καὶ αὐτοὶ ὡς λίθοι ζῶντες, “You also, as living stones” — ζάω, from the life family: alive with Christ's life. Peter, the ‘rock’, calls every believer a living stone. οἰκοδομεῖσθε οἶκος πνευματικός, “are built up as a spiritual house” — a temple made of people. εἰς ἱεράτευμα ἅγιον, “to be a holy priesthood” — ἱεράτευμα, a body of priests. ἀνενέγκαι πνευματικὰς θυσίας, “to offer up spiritual sacrifices” — ἀναφέρω and θυσία, from the offering family: no longer animals, but praise, good works and our own bodies (Hebrews 13:15–16; Romans 12:1). εὐπροσδέκτους, “acceptable,” only διὰ Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “through Jesus Christ.” ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stones"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"built up"},{"strongs":"G4152","hebrew":"πνευματικός","translit":"pneumatikós","english":"spiritual"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"house"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G2406","hebrew":"ἱεράτευμα","translit":"hieráteuma","english":"priesthood"},{"strongs":"G399","hebrew":"ἀναφέρω","translit":"anaphérō","english":"offer up"},{"strongs":"G2378","hebrew":"θυσία","translit":"thysía","english":"sacrifices"},{"strongs":"G2144","hebrew":"εὐπρόσδεκτος","translit":"euprósdektos","english":"acceptable"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Because it is contained in Scripture, “Behold, I lay in Zion a chief cornerstone, chosen, and precious: He who believes in him will not be disappointed.”","understanding":"“Because it is contained in Scripture, ‘Behold, I lay in Zion a chief cornerstone, chosen, and precious’” — from Isaiah 28:16. ἀκρογωνιαῖος, the corner foundation stone that fixes the line of the whole building. ἐκλεκτὸν ἔντιμον, the same two words as verse 4. “He who believes in him will not be disappointed” — καταισχύνω, to put to shame: trust in Him will never leave you embarrassed.","words":[{"strongs":"G4023","hebrew":"περιέχω","translit":"periéchō","english":"contained"},{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scripture"},{"strongs":"G4622","hebrew":"Σιών","translit":"Siṓn","english":"Zion"},{"strongs":"G204","hebrew":"ἀκρογωνιαῖος","translit":"akrogōniaîos","english":"chief cornerstone"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen"},{"strongs":"G1784","hebrew":"ἔντιμος","translit":"éntimos","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believes"},{"strongs":"G2617","hebrew":"καταισχύνω","translit":"kataischýnō","english":"disappointed"}]},{"v":7,"text":"For you who believe therefore is the honor, but for those who are disobedient, “The stone which the builders rejected, has become the chief cornerstone,”","understanding":"ὑμῖν οὖν ἡ τιμή τοῖς πιστεύουσιν, “For you who believe therefore is the honor” — τιμή, the noun of ἔντιμος, ‘precious’: the stone's honour is shared with those who trust it. ἀπειθοῦσιν δέ, “but for those who are disobedient” — ἀπειθέω, refusing to be persuaded. Then Psalm 118:22: “The stone which the builders rejected, has become the chief cornerstone” — ἀποδοκιμάζω again, from the test family; κεφαλὴ γωνίας, ‘head of the corner’. Jesus quoted this psalm about Himself (Matthew 21:42), and Peter quoted it before the Sanhedrin (Acts 4:11).","words":[{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G4100","hebrew":"πιστεύω","translit":"pisteúō","english":"believe"},{"strongs":"G544","hebrew":"ἀπειθέω","translit":"apeithéō","english":"disobedient"},{"strongs":"G3037","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","english":"stone"},{"strongs":"G593","hebrew":"ἀποδοκιμάζω","translit":"apodokimázō","english":"rejected"},{"strongs":"G3618","hebrew":"οἰκοδομέω","translit":"oikodoméō","english":"builders"},{"strongs":"G2776","hebrew":"κεφαλή","translit":"kephalḗ","english":"chief"},{"strongs":"G1137","hebrew":"γωνία","translit":"gōnía","english":"cornerstone"}]},{"v":8,"text":"and, “a stone of stumbling, and a rock of offense.” For they stumble at the word, being disobedient, to which also they were appointed.","understanding":"“and, ‘a stone of stumbling, and a rock of offense.’” — from Isaiah 8:14. πρόσκομμα, something you strike your foot against; πέτρα, a rock — the word Jesus used in Matthew 16:18; σκάνδαλον, the trigger of a trap. The same stone is a foundation for some and a stumbling block for others. οἳ προσκόπτουσιν τῷ λόγῳ ἀπειθοῦντες, “For they stumble at the word, being disobedient” — λόγος, from the word family: they trip over the message by refusing to obey it. εἰς ὃ καὶ ἐτέθησαν, “to which also they were appointed” — what God appointed is that disobedience to the word leads to stumbling; Scripture holds both God's sovereignty and human responsibility here without explaining away either.","words":[{"strongs":"G4348","hebrew":"πρόσκομμα","translit":"próskomma","english":"stumbling"},{"strongs":"G4073","hebrew":"πέτρα","translit":"pétra","english":"rock"},{"strongs":"G4625","hebrew":"σκάνδαλον","translit":"skándalon","english":"offense"},{"strongs":"G4350","hebrew":"προσκόπτω","translit":"proskóptō","english":"stumble"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G544","hebrew":"ἀπειθέω","translit":"apeithéō","english":"disobedient"},{"strongs":"G5087","hebrew":"τίθημι","translit":"títhēmi","english":"appointed"}]},{"v":9,"text":"But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession, that you may proclaim the excellence of him who called you out of darkness into his marvelous light:","understanding":"ὑμεῖς δὲ γένος ἐκλεκτόν, “But you are a chosen race” — four titles once given to Israel at Sinai (Exodus 19:5–6; Isaiah 43:20–21), now given to the church of Jews and Gentiles. γένος, a family line; βασίλειον ἱεράτευμα, “a royal priesthood” — kings and priests together; ἔθνος ἅγιον, “a holy nation”; λαὸς εἰς περιποίησιν, “a people for God’s own possession” — περιποίησις, a treasure acquired and kept. The purpose: ὅπως τὰς ἀρετὰς ἐξαγγείλητε, “that you may proclaim the excellence” — ἀρετή, the praiseworthy deeds and virtues of God. ἐκ σκότους … εἰς τὸ θαυμαστὸν αὐτοῦ φῶς, “out of darkness into his marvelous light.” σκότος is from the darkness family — darkness as a realm or condition — the opposite of light. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G1085","hebrew":"γένος","translit":"génos","english":"race"},{"strongs":"G1588","hebrew":"ἐκλεκτός","translit":"eklektós","english":"chosen"},{"strongs":"G934","hebrew":"βασίλειος","translit":"basíleios","english":"royal"},{"strongs":"G2406","hebrew":"ἱεράτευμα","translit":"hieráteuma","english":"priesthood"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nation"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G4047","hebrew":"περιποίησις","translit":"peripoíēsis","english":"possession"},{"strongs":"G1804","hebrew":"ἐξαγγέλλω","translit":"exangéllō","english":"proclaim"},{"strongs":"G703","hebrew":"ἀρέτη","translit":"arétē","english":"excellence"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G4655","hebrew":"σκότος","translit":"skótos","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G2298","hebrew":"θαυμαστός","translit":"thaumastós","english":"marvelous"},{"strongs":"G5457","hebrew":"φῶς","translit":"phōs","english":"light"}]},{"v":10,"text":"who in time past were no people, but now are God’s people, who had not obtained mercy, but now have obtained mercy.","understanding":"οἵ ποτε οὐ λαός, νῦν δὲ λαὸς θεοῦ, “who in time past were no people, but now are God’s people” — from Hosea 1:10 and 2:23, where God named Hosea's children ‘Not my people’ and ‘Not pitied’, then promised to reverse the names. οἱ οὐκ ἠλεημένοι, νῦν δὲ ἐλεηθέντες, “who had not obtained mercy, but now have obtained mercy” — ἐλεέω, from the mercy family: mercy that acts. Gentiles who were never God's people are now included.","words":[{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1653","hebrew":"ἐλεέω","translit":"eleéō","english":"obtained mercy"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Beloved, I beg you as foreigners and pilgrims, to abstain from fleshly lusts, which war against the soul;","understanding":"ἀγαπητοί, παρακαλῶ, “Beloved, I beg you” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family. ὡς παροίκους καὶ παρεπιδήμους, “as foreigners and pilgrims” — two words for not belonging: πάροικος, a resident alien, and παρεπίδημος, a temporary visitor, as in 1:1. ἀπέχεσθαι τῶν σαρκικῶν ἐπιθυμιῶν, “to abstain from fleshly lusts” — σαρκικός, belonging to the fallen nature. αἵτινες στρατεύονται κατὰ τῆς ψυχῆς, “which war against the soul” — στρατεύομαι, the same military verb James used for pleasures that ‘war in your members’ (James 4:1). ψυχή, from the life family.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"Beloved"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"beg"},{"strongs":"G3941","hebrew":"πάροικος","translit":"pároikos","english":"foreigners"},{"strongs":"G3927","hebrew":"παρεπίδημος","translit":"parepídēmos","english":"pilgrims"},{"strongs":"G567","hebrew":"ἀπέχομαι","translit":"apéchomai","english":"abstain"},{"strongs":"G4559","hebrew":"σαρκικός","translit":"sarkikós","english":"fleshly"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G4754","hebrew":"στρατεύομαι","translit":"strateúomai","english":"war"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"}]},{"v":12,"text":"having good behavior among the nations, so in that of which they speak against you as evildoers, they may by your good works, which they see, glorify God in the day of visitation.","understanding":"τὴν ἀναστροφὴν ὑμῶν ἐν τοῖς ἔθνεσιν ἔχοντες καλήν, “having good behavior among the nations” — καλός, from the good family: attractive, beautiful goodness that people can see. ἐν ᾧ καταλαλοῦσιν ὑμῶν ὡς κακοποιῶν, “so in that of which they speak against you as evildoers” — καταλαλέω, to slander; κακοποιός, from the evil family: a wrongdoer, perhaps even a criminal. ἐκ τῶν καλῶν ἔργων ἐποπτεύοντες, “they may by your good works, which they see” — καλὰ ἔργα, the good family and the work family together; ἐποπτεύω, from the see family: to watch closely, as an eyewitness. “glorify God in the day of visitation” — ἡμέρα, from the time family; ἐπισκοπή, God's visit, whether in mercy now or in judgement at the end.","words":[{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"behavior"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"nations"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2635","hebrew":"καταλαλέω","translit":"katalaléō","english":"speak against"},{"strongs":"G2555","hebrew":"κακοποιός","translit":"kakopoiós","english":"evildoers"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2029","hebrew":"ἐποπτεύω","translit":"epopteúō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorify"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G1984","hebrew":"ἐπισκοπή","translit":"episkopḗ","english":"visitation"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Therefore subject yourselves to every ordinance of man for the Lord’s sake: whether to the king, as supreme;","understanding":"ὑποτάγητε πάσῃ ἀνθρωπίνῃ κτίσει, “Therefore subject yourselves to every ordinance of man” — ὑποτάσσω, to rank yourself under, the same verb as ‘Be subject … to God’ (James 4:7). κτίσις, a creation, an institution set up by people. διὰ τὸν κύριον, “for the Lord’s sake” — the motive is not fear of the state but loyalty to Christ. “whether to the king, as supreme” — βασιλεύς, the emperor, at this time Nero. ὑπερέχω, to be above.","words":[{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"subject"},{"strongs":"G442","hebrew":"ἀνθρώπινος","translit":"anthrṓpinos","english":"of man"},{"strongs":"G2937","hebrew":"κτίσις","translit":"ktísis","english":"ordinance"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"king"},{"strongs":"G5242","hebrew":"ὑπερέχω","translit":"hyperéchō","english":"supreme"}]},{"v":14,"text":"or to governors, as sent by him for vengeance on evildoers and for praise to those who do well.","understanding":"“or to governors, as sent by him” — ἡγεμών, a provincial governor, like Pilate or Felix. Their task: εἰς ἐκδίκησιν κακοποιῶν, “for vengeance on evildoers” — κακοποιός, from the evil family, as in verse 12; ἔπαινον δὲ ἀγαθοποιῶν, “and for praise to those who do well” — ἀγαθοποιός, from the good family: those who do good. Peter describes what government is meant to do (Romans 13:3–4), even when it fails.","words":[{"strongs":"G2232","hebrew":"ἡγεμών","translit":"hēgemṓn","english":"governors"},{"strongs":"G3992","hebrew":"πέμπω","translit":"pémpō","english":"sent"},{"strongs":"G1557","hebrew":"ἐκδίκησις","translit":"ekdíkēsis","english":"vengeance"},{"strongs":"G2555","hebrew":"κακοποιός","translit":"kakopoiós","english":"evildoers"},{"strongs":"G1868","hebrew":"ἔπαινος","translit":"épainos","english":"praise"},{"strongs":"G17","hebrew":"ἀγαθοποιός","translit":"agathopoiós","english":"do well"}]},{"v":15,"text":"For this is the will of God, that by well-doing you should put to silence the ignorance of foolish men:","understanding":"ὅτι οὕτως ἐστὶν τὸ θέλημα τοῦ θεοῦ, “For this is the will of God” — θέλημα, what God desires. ἀγαθοποιοῦντας φιμοῦν, “that by well-doing you should put to silence” — ἀγαθοποιέω, from the good family: doing good; φιμόω, to muzzle, as an ox is muzzled. τὴν τῶν ἀφρόνων ἀνθρώπων ἀγνωσίαν, “the ignorance of foolish men” — ἄφρων, senseless; ἀγνωσία, not-knowing. Slander is best answered not by argument but by a good life.","words":[{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G15","hebrew":"ἀγαθοποιέω","translit":"agathopoiéō","english":"well-doing"},{"strongs":"G5392","hebrew":"φιμόω","translit":"phimóō","english":"put to silence"},{"strongs":"G56","hebrew":"ἀγνωσία","translit":"agnōsía","english":"ignorance"},{"strongs":"G878","hebrew":"ἄφρων","translit":"áphrōn","english":"foolish"}]},{"v":16,"text":"as free, and not using your freedom for a cloak of wickedness, but as bondservants of God.","understanding":"ὡς ἐλεύθεροι, “as free” — ἐλεύθερος: believers are truly free (James 1:25; 2:12). καὶ μὴ ὡς ἐπικάλυμμα ἔχοντες τῆς κακίας τὴν ἐλευθερίαν, “and not using your freedom for a cloak of wickedness” — ἐπικάλυμμα, a veil or cover-up; κακία, from the evil family, as in verse 1. Freedom is not a disguise for sin (Galatians 5:13). ἀλλ᾽ ὡς θεοῦ δοῦλοι, “but as bondservants of God” — δοῦλος, from the servant family: wholly owned. True freedom is slavery to God.","words":[{"strongs":"G1658","hebrew":"ἐλεύθερος","translit":"eleútheros","english":"free"},{"strongs":"G1942","hebrew":"ἐπικάλυμα","translit":"epikályma","english":"cloak"},{"strongs":"G2549","hebrew":"κακία","translit":"kakía","english":"wickedness"},{"strongs":"G1657","hebrew":"ἐλευθερία","translit":"eleuthería","english":"freedom"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservants"}]},{"v":17,"text":"Honor all men. Love the brotherhood. Fear God. Honor the king.","understanding":"Four short commands. πάντας τιμήσατε, “Honor all men” — τιμάω: every person, made in God's image, deserves respect. τὴν ἀδελφότητα ἀγαπᾶτε, “Love the brotherhood” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family; ἀδελφότης, the whole family of believers. τὸν θεὸν φοβεῖσθε, “Fear God” — φοβέω: reverence is reserved for God alone. τὸν βασιλέα τιμᾶτε, “Honor the king” — the same τιμάω as for all people: the emperor gets honour, but only God gets fear.","words":[{"strongs":"G5091","hebrew":"τιμάω","translit":"timáō","english":"Honor"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"Love"},{"strongs":"G81","hebrew":"ἀδελφότης","translit":"adelphótēs","english":"brotherhood"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"Fear"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G935","hebrew":"βασιλεύς","translit":"basileús","english":"king"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Servants, be in subjection to your masters with all fear; not only to the good and gentle, but also to the wicked.","understanding":"οἱ οἰκέται, “Servants” — οἰκέτης, from the servant family: a household slave, a member of the house staff, as distinct from δοῦλος (v. 16), a slave as someone owned. ὑποτασσόμενοι ἐν παντὶ φόβῳ τοῖς δεσπόταις, “be in subjection to your masters with all fear” — δεσπότης, an owner with full authority; φόβος here is reverence towards God (v. 17). οὐ μόνον τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς καὶ ἐπιεικέσιν, “not only to the good and gentle” — ἀγαθός, from the good family; ἐπιεικής, the ‘gentle’ of James 3:17. ἀλλὰ καὶ τοῖς σκολιοῖς, “but also to the wicked” — σκολιός, crooked, perverse. Peter addresses slaves directly as responsible people with dignity before God; he regulates the situation they are in without approving of slavery itself.","words":[{"strongs":"G3610","hebrew":"οἰκέτης","translit":"oikétēs","english":"Servants"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"be in subjection"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G1203","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","english":"masters"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G1933","hebrew":"ἐπιεικής","translit":"epieikḗs","english":"gentle"},{"strongs":"G4646","hebrew":"σκολιός","translit":"skoliós","english":"wicked"}]},{"v":19,"text":"For it is commendable if someone endures pain, suffering unjustly, because of conscience toward God.","understanding":"τοῦτο γὰρ χάρις, “For it is commendable” — literally ‘this is grace’: favour in God's sight. εἰ διὰ συνείδησιν θεοῦ, “because of conscience toward God” — συνείδησις, awareness of God's presence. ὑποφέρει τις λύπας πάσχων ἀδίκως, “if someone endures pain, suffering unjustly” — ὑποφέρω, to bear up under; λύπη, grief, pain; πάσχω, from the suffer family: to undergo something painful done to you; ἀδίκως, unfairly.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"commendable"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5297","hebrew":"ὑποφέρω","translit":"hypophérō","english":"endures"},{"strongs":"G3077","hebrew":"λύπη","translit":"lýpē","english":"pain"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffering"},{"strongs":"G95","hebrew":"ἀδίκως","translit":"adíkōs","english":"unjustly"}]},{"v":20,"text":"For what glory is it if, when you sin, you patiently endure beating? But if, when you do well, you patiently endure suffering, this is commendable with God.","understanding":"ποῖον γὰρ κλέος, “For what glory is it” — κλέος, fame, credit. εἰ ἁμαρτάνοντες καὶ κολαφιζόμενοι ὑπομενεῖτε, “if, when you sin, you patiently endure beating” — ἁμαρτάνω, from the sin family; κολαφίζω, to strike with the fist, the word used of the soldiers striking Jesus (Matthew 26:67); ὑπομένω, from the endurance family: to remain under. ἀλλ᾽ εἰ ἀγαθοποιοῦντες καὶ πάσχοντες ὑπομενεῖτε, “But if, when you do well, you patiently endure suffering” — ἀγαθοποιέω, from the good family, and πάσχω, from the suffer family. τοῦτο χάρις παρὰ θεῷ, “this is commendable with God” — grace again, as in verse 19.","words":[{"strongs":"G2811","hebrew":"κλέος","translit":"kléos","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G2852","hebrew":"κολαφίζω","translit":"kolaphízō","english":"beating"},{"strongs":"G5278","hebrew":"ὑπομένω","translit":"hypoménō","english":"patiently endure"},{"strongs":"G15","hebrew":"ἀγαθοποιέω","translit":"agathopoiéō","english":"do well"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffering"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"commendable"}]},{"v":21,"text":"For to this you were called, because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example, that you should follow his steps,","understanding":"εἰς τοῦτο γὰρ ἐκλήθητε, “For to this you were called” — καλέω: suffering for doing good is part of the calling. ὅτι καὶ Χριστὸς ἔπαθεν ὑπὲρ ὑμῶν, “because Christ also suffered for us” — πάσχω, from the suffer family. ὑμῖν ὑπολιμπάνων ὑπογραμμόν, “leaving you an example” — ὑπογραμμός, from the pattern family: a writing-copy, the line of letters a teacher wrote for a child to trace underneath, as distinct from τύπος (a model) or ὑπόδειγμα (an example to copy). ἵνα ἐπακολουθήσητε τοῖς ἴχνεσιν αὐτοῦ, “that you should follow his steps” — ἴχνος, a footprint. Trace His letters; walk in His footprints.","words":[{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"},{"strongs":"G5261","hebrew":"ὑπογραμμός","translit":"hypogrammós","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G1872","hebrew":"ἐπακολουθέω","translit":"epakolouthéō","english":"follow"},{"strongs":"G2487","hebrew":"ἴχνος","translit":"íchnos","english":"steps"}]},{"v":22,"text":"who did not sin, “neither was deceit found in his mouth.”","understanding":"ὃς ἁμαρτίαν οὐκ ἐποίησεν, “who did not sin” — ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. Then Isaiah 53:9: “neither was deceit found in his mouth” — δόλος, the deceit of verse 1. Peter, who lived with Jesus for three years, testifies to His sinlessness. Isaiah 53 runs through verses 22–25.","words":[{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G1388","hebrew":"δόλος","translit":"dólos","english":"deceit"},{"strongs":"G4750","hebrew":"στόμα","translit":"stóma","english":"mouth"}]},{"v":23,"text":"Who, when he was cursed, didn’t curse back. When he suffered, didn’t threaten, but committed himself to him who judges righteously.","understanding":"ὃς λοιδορούμενος οὐκ ἀντελοιδόρει, “Who, when he was cursed, didn’t curse back” — λοιδορέω, to abuse verbally; ἀντιλοιδορέω, to insult in return. πάσχων οὐκ ἠπείλει, “When he suffered, didn’t threaten” — πάσχω, from the suffer family. Peter watched it happen at the trials (Matthew 26:63; 27:12–14). παρεδίδου δὲ τῷ κρίνοντι δικαίως, “but committed himself to him who judges righteously” — παραδίδωμι, to hand over: Jesus handed His cause to the Father. That is how to suffer without revenge. κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G3058","hebrew":"λοιδορέω","translit":"loidoréō","english":"cursed"},{"strongs":"G486","hebrew":"ἀντιλοιδορέω","translit":"antiloidoréō","english":"curse back"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"},{"strongs":"G546","hebrew":"ἀπειλέω","translit":"apeiléō","english":"threaten"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"committed"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judges"},{"strongs":"G1346","hebrew":"δικαίως","translit":"dikaíōs","english":"righteously"}]},{"v":24,"text":"He himself bore our sins in his body on the tree, that we, having died to sins, might live to righteousness; by whose stripes you were healed.","understanding":"ὃς τὰς ἁμαρτίας ἡμῶν αὐτὸς ἀνήνεγκεν ἐν τῷ σώματι αὐτοῦ ἐπὶ τὸ ξύλον, “He himself bore our sins in his body on the tree” — ἀναφέρω, from the offering family: the same verb as ‘offer up’ in verse 5, used of carrying a sacrifice up to the altar; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family; ξύλον, wood, the cross, recalling Deuteronomy 21:23. ἵνα ταῖς ἁμαρτίαις ἀπογενόμενοι τῇ δικαιοσύνῃ ζήσωμεν, “that we, having died to sins, might live to righteousness” — ζάω, from the life family. οὗ τῷ μώλωπι ἰάθητε, “by whose stripes you were healed” — μώλωψ, the welt left by a whip; ἰάομαι, from the heal family: the physician's word. From Isaiah 53:5. Writing to beaten slaves, Peter points them to the beaten Saviour whose wounds healed the deepest wound, sin.","words":[{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G399","hebrew":"ἀναφέρω","translit":"anaphérō","english":"bore"},{"strongs":"G4983","hebrew":"σῶμα","translit":"sōma","english":"body"},{"strongs":"G3586","hebrew":"ξύλον","translit":"xýlon","english":"tree"},{"strongs":"G581","hebrew":"ἀπογενόμενος","translit":"apogenómenos","english":"having died"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G3468","hebrew":"μώλωψ","translit":"mṓlōps","english":"stripes"},{"strongs":"G2390","hebrew":"ἰάομαι","translit":"iáomai","english":"healed"}]},{"v":25,"text":"For you were going astray like sheep; but now have returned to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls.","understanding":"ἦτε γὰρ ὡς πρόβατα πλανώμενοι, “For you were going astray like sheep” — πλανάω, to wander off, the verb Isaiah uses of straying sheep (Isaiah 53:6) and James of the straying believer (James 5:19). ἀλλὰ ἐπεστράφητε νῦν, “but now have returned” — ἐπιστρέφω, to turn back, the word James used for restoring a wanderer (James 5:19–20). ἐπὶ τὸν ποιμένα καὶ ἐπίσκοπον τῶν ψυχῶν ὑμῶν, “to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls” — ποιμήν, shepherd; ἐπίσκοπος, the one who watches over; ψυχή, from the life family. Peter, told three times to feed Christ's sheep, now points the sheep to the Chief Shepherd (5:4).","words":[{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"going astray"},{"strongs":"G4263","hebrew":"πρόβατον","translit":"próbaton","english":"sheep"},{"strongs":"G1994","hebrew":"ἐπιστρέφω","translit":"epistréphō","english":"returned"},{"strongs":"G4166","hebrew":"ποιμήν","translit":"poimḗn","english":"Shepherd"},{"strongs":"G1985","hebrew":"ἐπίσκοπος","translit":"epískopos","english":"Overseer"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Hungry Newborn","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a newborn baby a few hours old. She does not care about comfort, toys or attention; she wants one thing. When she is hungry she cries with her whole body until she is fed — and then, satisfied, she sleeps and grows. Before feeding, her mother gently removes the soiled clothes and wraps her in clean ones.","meaning":"“Putting away therefore all wickedness, all deceit, hypocrisies, envies, and all evil speaking” (v. 1), “as newborn babies, long for the pure milk of the Word, that with it you may grow” (v. 2). Spiritual growth needs two things: putting off what poisons and craving what nourishes. If you have “tasted that the Lord is gracious” (v. 3), you will want more. Is your appetite for God's word as strong as a newborn's for milk?"},{"title":"The Stone the Builders Rejected","verseRef":"v. 4-8","image":"Picture builders on a great construction site examining stones from the quarry. One stone seems the wrong shape. They test it, frown, and toss it aside onto the rubble heap. Later, when the chief architect arrives, he walks straight to the rubble heap, picks up that very stone and sets it at the corner, where every wall will be measured from it. Then other stones — rough, ordinary, alive — are fitted to it one by one, until a temple rises.","meaning":"“The stone which the builders rejected, has become the chief cornerstone” (v. 7). “You also, as living stones, are built up as a spiritual house, to be a holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God through Jesus Christ” (v. 5). Jesus was rejected by men but chosen by God. Now you are a living stone in His temple. Your life is lined up by Him and joined to others."},{"title":"Out of Darkness into Light","verseRef":"v. 9-10","image":"Picture people stumbling in a deep, dark cave, unable to see their hands, lost and afraid. Then a voice calls their names, a door opens, and they walk out blinking into the brilliant light of morning. Someone places a royal robe on their shoulders and a priest's turban on their heads, and says, ‘You belong to the King.’","meaning":"“But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession, that you may proclaim the excellence of him who called you out of darkness into his marvelous light” (v. 9). You were once ‘no people’; now you are God's people. Your identity is royal and priestly, and your purpose is to declare His excellence."},{"title":"Tracing the Letters","verseRef":"v. 21-24","image":"Picture a child in an ancient schoolroom with a wax tablet. At the top the teacher has written a line of perfect letters. The child presses the stylus into the wax and slowly traces underneath, letter by letter, following every curve. Then picture a man walking ahead on a dusty road, his footprints pressed into the dirt, and others stepping exactly where he stepped.","meaning":"“For to this you were called, because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example, that you should follow his steps” (v. 21). When He was insulted, He did not insult back; when He suffered, He did not threaten, “but committed himself to him who judges righteously” (v. 23). And “He himself bore our sins in his body on the tree” (v. 24). Christ is both your example to trace and your Saviour who bore what you never could."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Grow by the Word (v. 1-3)","body":"Put away malice, deceit, hypocrisy, envy and slander, and like newborn babies crave the pure milk of the word, so that you grow, since you have tasted that the Lord is good."},{"label":"Living Stones and a Spiritual House (v. 4-8)","body":"Come to Christ, the living stone rejected by people but chosen by God, and be built as living stones into a spiritual house and holy priesthood offering spiritual sacrifices; Scripture calls Him the precious cornerstone for believers and a stone of stumbling for the disobedient."},{"label":"God's Own People (v. 9-10)","body":"You are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation and God's own people, called out of darkness into light to proclaim His excellence; once not a people, now God's people; once without mercy, now shown mercy."},{"label":"Good Conduct Among the Nations (v. 11-17)","body":"As foreigners, abstain from fleshly desires; live good lives so that slanderers see your good works and glorify God; submit to human authorities for the Lord's sake; silence ignorance by doing good; live as free people who are God's servants; honour all, love the brotherhood, fear God, honour the king."},{"label":"Suffering Like Christ (v. 18-25)","body":"Servants should submit to masters, even harsh ones, since enduring unjust suffering with a conscience towards God is grace; Christ suffered for us, leaving an example: He did not sin, retaliate or threaten, but entrusted Himself to the righteous Judge; He bore our sins on the tree, and by His wounds we are healed; we were straying sheep, now returned to the Shepherd and Overseer of our souls."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Newborns Who Become a Temple","paragraphs":["Chapter 1 ended with new birth by the living word. Chapter 2 continues the picture: newborns need milk. First comes a list of things to throw off — malice, deceit, hypocrisy, envy and slander (v. 1). These are the sins that destroy the brotherly love Peter just commanded (1:22). Then comes the craving: “as newborn babies, long for the pure milk of the Word, that with it you may grow” (v. 2). The word ‘pure’ (ἄδολος, without deceit) is the opposite of ‘deceit’ (δόλος) in verse 1. What you take in shapes what you become.","Then the image changes from a nursery to a building site. Believers come to Jesus, “a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God, precious” (v. 4), and they themselves become “living stones” built into “a spiritual house, to be a holy priesthood” (v. 5). Peter, whose name means ‘rock’, never claims to be the foundation; he gives that place to Christ alone, and calls every believer a stone. Paul uses the same picture: “in whom the whole building, fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord” (Ephesians 2:21).","The cornerstone divides people. For believers He is honour; for the disobedient He is “a stone of stumbling, and a rock of offense” (v. 8). The same Jesus who saves some causes others to stumble — not because He has failed, but because they refuse to obey the word. Jesus quoted Psalm 118 about Himself: “Did you never read in the Scriptures, ‘The stone which the builders rejected, the same was made the head of the corner’” (Matthew 21:42), and Peter quoted it to the very leaders who had rejected Him: He is “‘the stone which was regarded as worthless by you, the builders, which has become the head of the corner’” (Acts 4:11)."]},{"heading":"The Identity of God's People","paragraphs":["Verse 9 is one of the great identity statements of Scripture: “But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession, that you may proclaim the excellence of him who called you out of darkness into his marvelous light.” Every title was first given to Israel at Mount Sinai. Peter now gives them to a church made up largely of Gentiles. This is not because God has abandoned Israel, but because in Christ, Jews and Gentiles together form the people of God.","The point of the identity is mission. A priesthood exists to bring people to God and God to people. A royal priesthood represents the King. Believers are chosen not merely to enjoy God's favour but to “proclaim the excellence” of the One who rescued them. John saw the same in heaven: Christ “made us to be a Kingdom, priests to his God and Father” (Revelation 1:6).","And the identity is pure grace. “who in time past were no people, but now are God’s people, who had not obtained mercy, but now have obtained mercy” (v. 10). Peter borrows Hosea's names for his children — ‘Not my people’ and ‘No mercy’ — and shows them reversed. If you feel like an outsider, unwanted or forgotten, this verse is God's answer: you have been named and claimed."]},{"heading":"Living Well in a Hostile World","paragraphs":["From verse 11 Peter turns to the believer's life among unbelievers. He reminds them again that they are “foreigners and pilgrims” (v. 11), and that the first battle is inside: “to abstain from fleshly lusts, which war against the soul.” Then he turns outward: “having good behavior among the nations, so in that of which they speak against you as evildoers, they may by your good works, which they see, glorify God in the day of visitation” (v. 12). This is Jesus' teaching: “Even so, let your light shine before men; that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven” (Matthew 5:16).","That includes respect for authority: “Therefore subject yourselves to every ordinance of man for the Lord’s sake” (v. 13). Remarkably, the king Peter tells them to honour was Nero, who would later execute Christians, possibly including Peter himself. Submission does not mean obeying commands to sin — Peter himself had said, ‘We must obey God rather than men’ (Acts 5:29) — but it means respecting the role of government and living as good citizens, so that “by well-doing you should put to silence the ignorance of foolish men” (v. 15).","Verse 16 guards against two errors: believers are free, so they are not slaves to human institutions; but they are God's slaves, so their freedom is never a cover for sin. Verse 17 sums it up in four short commands: “Honor all men. Love the brotherhood. Fear God. Honor the king.” Notice the careful balance: honour for everyone, including the emperor; love for the church; fear for God alone."]},{"heading":"Following the Suffering Shepherd","paragraphs":["Peter then speaks to household slaves, many of whom were believers serving unbelieving masters. He does not endorse slavery; he addresses slaves as moral agents with dignity before God and shows them how to live faithfully within an unjust situation. If they suffer for doing wrong, there is no credit in enduring it. “But if, when you do well, you patiently endure suffering, this is commendable with God” (v. 20). The principle reaches every believer who suffers unfairly — at work, at home or in society.","The reason is Christ: “For to this you were called, because Christ also suffered for us, leaving you an example, that you should follow his steps” (v. 21). Peter had seen it with his own eyes. Jesus “did not sin” (v. 22), and when He was cursed, He “didn’t curse back” and did not threaten, “but committed himself to him who judges righteously” (v. 23). Jesus prayed for His executioners: “Father, forgive them, for they don’t know what they are doing” (Luke 23:34). The answer to injustice is not revenge but trust in the righteous Judge.","But Christ is more than an example. “He himself bore our sins in his body on the tree, that we, having died to sins, might live to righteousness; by whose stripes you were healed” (v. 24). Peter is quoting Isaiah 53, the song of the Suffering Servant. To slaves who knew the whip, Peter says: your Lord was whipped too, and His wounds have healed your deepest wound. “For you were going astray like sheep; but now have returned to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls” (v. 25). Jesus said, “I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep” (John 10:11)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Cornerstone, the Priesthood and the Suffering Servant","paragraphs":["The stone sayings of verses 6–8 appear across the New Testament. Paul combines the same two Isaiah passages: “even as it is written, ‘Behold, I lay in Zion a stumbling stone and a rock of offense; and no one who believes in him will be disappointed’” (Romans 9:33). He calls Christ “the chief cornerstone” of the church built “on the foundation of the apostles and prophets” (Ephesians 2:20). And Jesus' words to Peter stand behind it all: “I also tell you that you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my assembly” (Matthew 16:18). Peter, the ‘rock’, now points away from himself to the true cornerstone.","The royal priesthood offering spiritual sacrifices (vv. 5, 9) is the fulfilment of Hebrews' teaching: “Through him, then, let us offer up a sacrifice of praise to God continually, that is, the fruit of lips which proclaim allegiance to his name” (Hebrews 13:15). Paul calls the believer's whole life a sacrifice: “present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable to God, which is your spiritual service” (Romans 12:1). Every believer is a priest; every life is an altar.","Verses 22–25 are woven from Isaiah 53, the clearest Old Testament prophecy of Christ's atoning death. Peter quotes it not as a distant text but as an eyewitness of its fulfilment. The Gospels record the silence (Matthew 27:12), the beating (Mark 15:19) and the prayer for forgiveness (Luke 23:34). And the Shepherd who bore our sins is the Shepherd who said, “I am the good shepherd. I know my own, and I’m known by my own” (John 10:14)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Stone","hebrew":"λίθος","translit":"líthos","strongs":"G3037 (rock πέτρα, pétra, G4073; cornerstone ἀκρογωνιαῖος, akrogōniaîos, G204)","verses":"v. 4, 5, 6, 7, 8","insight":"Strong's: “a stone (literally or figuratively).” Peter uses λίθος five times in five verses: Christ the living stone, believers the living stones, the cornerstone of Isaiah 28, the rejected stone of Psalm 118, and the stumbling stone of Isaiah 8. The fisherman whom Jesus named Πέτρος, ‘rock’, and to whom He said ‘on this πέτρα I will build my assembly’, never claims the title for himself here. He gives the cornerstone to Christ and makes every believer a stone in the same house."},{"english":"Priesthood","hebrew":"ἱεράτευμα","translit":"hieráteuma","strongs":"G2406 (royal βασίλειος, basíleios, G934)","verses":"v. 5, 9","insight":"Strong's: “the priestly fraternity, i.e. sacerdotal order (figuratively).” ἱεράτευμα means a body or community of priests. In Israel only the descendants of Aaron could serve as priests. Peter says the whole church is a holy priesthood (v. 5) and a royal priesthood (v. 9) — echoing Exodus 19:6. Every believer has direct access to God through Christ and a priestly calling: to offer spiritual sacrifices and to represent God to the world."},{"english":"Example","hebrew":"ὑπογραμμός","translit":"hypogrammós","strongs":"G5261","verses":"v. 21","insight":"Strong's: “an underwriting, i.e. copy for imitation (figuratively).” ὑπογραμμός was a schoolroom word: the line of letters a teacher wrote at the top of a child's tablet for the child to trace underneath. This is its only New Testament appearance. This study adds it to the pattern family beside τύπος (a model or imprint) and ὑπόδειγμα (an example). Christ's patient suffering is the line written at the top of your page. You follow it stroke by stroke, not by inventing your own letters."},{"english":"Suffer","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","strongs":"G3958 (sufferings πάθημα, páthēma, G3804)","verses":"v. 19, 20, 21, 23","insight":"Strong's: “to experience a sensation or impression (usually painful).” πάσχω is the key verb of 1 Peter, used twelve times, more than in any other New Testament letter. It means to undergo something done to you. Peter uses it both of Christ (v. 21, 23) and of believers (v. 19–20). This study gathers it with πάθημα (sufferings), συμπάσχω (suffer with Christ) and κακοπαθέω (endure hardship) into a new suffer family. Believers' suffering is never pointless when it is suffering with and for Christ."},{"english":"Stripes","hebrew":"μώλωψ","translit":"mṓlōps","strongs":"G3468 (healed ἰάομαι, iáomai, G2390)","verses":"v. 24","insight":"Strong's: “a mole (\"black eye\") or blow-mark.” μώλωψ is the swollen bruise or welt left by a whip — the result of a flogging. Peter takes the word from Isaiah 53:5. To slaves who knew such marks on their own backs, he says that Christ bore them too, and that His wounds heal. ἰάομαι, from the heal family, is the physician's word. The primary healing is from sin, as the context shows; but the cross is also the ground of every healing God gives."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is telling you who you are — and showing you how to live that identity before a watching world.","items":[{"title":"How Is Your Appetite?","body":"The Spirit asks whether you are craving the word like a newborn craves milk. If your appetite is weak, it may be because something else is filling you — malice, envy, slander or other poisons of verse 1. Ask Him to show you what you need to put away, and to give you a fresh hunger for His word. “if indeed you have tasted that the Lord is gracious” (v. 3), you were made for more."},{"title":"You Belong","body":"Do you feel rejected, overlooked or unwanted? Jesus was rejected by people but chosen and precious to God (v. 4). So are you. “who in time past were no people, but now are God’s people” (v. 10). The Spirit wants you to hear your names: chosen, royal, priestly, holy, God's own possession. Let that identity change how you walk into this week."},{"title":"Who Are You Tempted to Curse Back?","body":"Someone has treated you unfairly. The Spirit brings Jesus' example before you: “Who, when he was cursed, didn’t curse back. When he suffered, didn’t threaten, but committed himself to him who judges righteously” (v. 23). He is not asking you to pretend it does not hurt. He is asking you to hand the case to the righteous Judge. Who do you need to entrust to Him today?"}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Milk Check","body":"How much of God's word are you taking in each day compared with news, entertainment and social media (v. 2)? Which of the sins in verse 1 is spoiling your appetite?"},{"title":"The Witness Check","body":"If your unbelieving neighbours, colleagues or family watched your conduct for a week, would they see good works that point to God (v. 12)? Where is your behaviour giving them reason to speak against you?"},{"title":"The Retaliation Check","body":"When you are treated unfairly, do you insult back, threaten or plot revenge (v. 23)? How do you speak about authorities, bosses or leaders you disagree with (vv. 13–17)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Feed Like a Newborn","body":"For the next two weeks, set a fixed time each day — before your phone — to read one chapter of the New Testament slowly. Write one sentence about what it shows you of the Lord's goodness and one sentence about how you will obey it. Notice whether your appetite grows."},{"title":"Trace His Letters","body":"Write verse 23 on a card and carry it with you. The next time someone wrongs you, read it, pray it, and respond with a blessing instead of a curse. Then write the situation on the back of the card and pray, ‘Father, I commit this to You, the righteous Judge.’ Keep the card as a record of every time you followed His steps."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, I have tasted that You are gracious, and I want more of You. Help me put away all malice, deceit, hypocrisy, envy and slander, and give me a newborn's hunger for the pure milk of Your word, so that I may grow. Thank You that You are the living stone, rejected by people but chosen and precious to God. Build me as a living stone into Your spiritual house, and make me a holy priest who offers spiritual sacrifices acceptable through You. Thank You that I am chosen, royal, holy and Your own possession. I was no people, and now I am Yours; I had no mercy, and now I have received mercy. Teach me to proclaim Your excellence. Help me abstain from desires that war against my soul and to live such a good life among unbelievers that they glorify God. Give me grace to honour all people, love Your family, fear God and honour those in authority. When I suffer unjustly, let me follow Your steps. You did not sin, You did not curse back, You did not threaten; You entrusted Yourself to the righteous Judge. Thank You that You bore my sins in Your body on the tree, and that by Your wounds I am healed. I was a wandering sheep; now I have returned to You, the Shepherd and Overseer of my soul. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Rejected Stone Before the Council","ref":"Acts 4:1-22","relatedVerses":"v. 4-8","summary":"After Peter and John healed a lame man at the temple, they were arrested and brought before the same council that had condemned Jesus. Filled with the Spirit, Peter declared that the man was healed in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom they crucified and God raised. Then he quoted Psalm 118 to their faces: Jesus is “‘the stone which was regarded as worthless by you, the builders, which has become the head of the corner’” (Acts 4:11). The leaders were amazed, because Peter and John were ordinary, unschooled men, and “They recognized that they had been with Jesus” (Acts 4:13). Years later Peter wrote the same truth to the scattered churches."},{"title":"Jesus Silent Before His Accusers","ref":"Matthew 26:57-68; 27:11-14","relatedVerses":"v. 22-23","summary":"At His trial before the high priest, false witnesses lined up against Jesus, but “Jesus held his peace” (Matthew 26:63). They spat in His face and struck Him with their fists. Before Pilate, “When he was accused by the chief priests and elders, he answered nothing” (Matthew 27:12), and “He gave him no answer, not even one word, so that the governor marveled greatly” (Matthew 27:14). Peter was in the courtyard that night, denying Him. Now he writes as an eyewitness of how Jesus suffered: no sin, no deceit, no curse, no threat — only trust in the One who judges righteously."},{"title":"The Lost Sheep Brought Home","ref":"Luke 15:3-7","relatedVerses":"v. 25","summary":"Jesus told of a shepherd with a hundred sheep who lost one. He left the ninety-nine in the wilderness and searched until he found it, then laid it on his shoulders rejoicing and called his friends to celebrate. Jesus said there is more joy in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine who need no repentance. Peter knew what it was to stray; the Shepherd had found him by a charcoal fire and brought him home. So he writes, “For you were going astray like sheep; but now have returned to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls” (v. 25)."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Peter","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"Homes That Preach, Hearts Ready to Answer, and the Victorious Christ","themeTitle":"“Always Be Ready to Give an Answer … Concerning the Hope That Is in You”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"In the same way, wives, be in subjection to your own husbands; so that, even if any don’t obey the Word, they may be won by the behavior of their wives without a word;","understanding":"ὁμοίως, “In the same way” — as with citizens (2:13) and servants (2:18), the pattern of Christ's suffering and trust (2:21–25) now shapes the home. γυναῖκες, ὑποτασσόμεναι τοῖς ἰδίοις ἀνδράσιν, “wives, be in subjection to your own husbands” — ὑποτάσσω, to rank oneself under, voluntary and ‘your own’, not every man. ἵνα καὶ εἴ τινες ἀπειθοῦσιν τῷ λόγῳ, “so that, even if any don’t obey the Word” — husbands who reject the gospel. διὰ τῆς τῶν γυναικῶν ἀναστροφῆς ἄνευ λόγου κερδηθήσονται, “they may be won by the behavior of their wives without a word” — a wordplay in the word family: those who refuse the λόγος (the message) may be won ἄνευ λόγου (without speech). κερδαίνω, to gain — the merchant's ‘make a profit’ of James 4:13, now used for winning a soul.","words":[{"strongs":"G3668","hebrew":"ὁμοίως","translit":"homoíōs","english":"In the same way"},{"strongs":"G1135","hebrew":"γυνή","translit":"gynḗ","english":"wives"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"be in subjection"},{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husbands"},{"strongs":"G544","hebrew":"ἀπειθέω","translit":"apeithéō","english":"don’t obey"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"Word"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"behavior"},{"strongs":"G2770","hebrew":"κερδαίνω","translit":"kerdaínō","english":"won"}]},{"v":2,"text":"seeing your pure behavior in fear.","understanding":"ἐποπτεύσαντες, “seeing” — ἐποπτεύω, from the see family: to watch closely as an eyewitness, the word used in 2:12 of unbelievers watching good works. τὴν ἐν φόβῳ ἁγνὴν ἀναστροφὴν ὑμῶν, “your pure behavior in fear” — ἁγνός, clean, chaste; φόβος, reverence towards God, not fear of the husband (v. 6). The unbelieving husband is watching.","words":[{"strongs":"G2029","hebrew":"ἐποπτεύω","translit":"epopteúō","english":"seeing"},{"strongs":"G53","hebrew":"ἁγνός","translit":"hagnós","english":"pure"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"behavior"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"}]},{"v":3,"text":"Let your beauty be not just the outward adorning of braiding the hair, and of wearing jewels of gold, or of putting on fine clothing;","understanding":"ὧν ἔστω οὐχ ὁ ἔξωθεν, “Let your beauty be not just the outward adorning” — ὁ … κόσμος: here κόσμος, from the world family, keeps its original meaning, ‘order, arrangement, adornment’ — the root of ‘cosmetic’. ἐμπλοκῆς τριχῶν, “of braiding the hair” — elaborate braided hairstyles woven with gold; περιθέσεως χρυσίων, “of wearing jewels of gold”; ἐνδύσεως ἱματίων, “of putting on fine clothing.” Peter does not forbid these; the WEB's ‘not just’ catches the sense: they must not be where beauty lies (1 Timothy 2:9).","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"adorning"},{"strongs":"G1708","hebrew":"ἐμπλοκή","translit":"emplokḗ","english":"braiding"},{"strongs":"G2359","hebrew":"θρίξ","translit":"thríx","english":"hair"},{"strongs":"G4025","hebrew":"περίθεσις","translit":"períthesis","english":"wearing"},{"strongs":"G5553","hebrew":"χρυσίον","translit":"chrysíon","english":"gold"},{"strongs":"G1745","hebrew":"ἔνδυσις","translit":"éndysis","english":"putting on"},{"strongs":"G2440","hebrew":"ἱμάτιον","translit":"himátion","english":"clothing"}]},{"v":4,"text":"but in the hidden person of the heart, in the incorruptible adornment of a gentle and quiet spirit, which is in the sight of God very precious.","understanding":"ἀλλ᾽ ὁ κρυπτὸς τῆς καρδίας ἄνθρωπος, “but in the hidden person of the heart” — the inner self God sees. ἐν τῷ ἀφθάρτῳ, “in the incorruptible adornment” — ἄφθαρτος, imperishable, like the inheritance (1:4) and the seed (1:23); gold fades, this does not. τοῦ πραέος καὶ ἡσυχίου πνεύματος, “of a gentle and quiet spirit” — πραΰς, from the humble family: meek, strength under control, the word Jesus used of Himself (Matthew 11:29); ἡσύχιος, calm, settled, not anxious. ὅ ἐστιν ἐνώπιον τοῦ θεοῦ πολυτελές, “which is in the sight of God very precious” — πολυτελής, costly, the word used of the expensive perfume poured on Jesus (Mark 14:3).","words":[{"strongs":"G2927","hebrew":"κρυπτός","translit":"kryptós","english":"hidden"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G862","hebrew":"ἄφθαρτος","translit":"áphthartos","english":"incorruptible"},{"strongs":"G4239","hebrew":"πραΰς","translit":"praÿs","english":"gentle"},{"strongs":"G2272","hebrew":"ἡσύχιος","translit":"hēsýchios","english":"quiet"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"},{"strongs":"G1799","hebrew":"ἐνώπιον","translit":"enṓpion","english":"in the sight of"},{"strongs":"G4185","hebrew":"πολυτελής","translit":"polytelḗs","english":"very precious"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For this is how the holy women before, who hoped in God also adorned themselves, being in subjection to their own husbands:","understanding":"“For this is how the holy women before, who hoped in God also adorned themselves” — ἅγιαι, holy, set apart to God; ἐλπίζω, to hope, the living hope of 1:3; κοσμέω, the verb of the κόσμος of verse 3: to adorn, to put in order. Their beauty grew from their hope in God, not their fear of man. ἅγιος is from the holy family — holy, set apart for God; as a noun, the saints — God’s holy people.","words":[{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G1679","hebrew":"ἐλπίζω","translit":"elpízō","english":"hoped"},{"strongs":"G2885","hebrew":"κοσμέω","translit":"kosméō","english":"adorned"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"being in subjection"}]},{"v":6,"text":"as Sarah obeyed Abraham, calling him lord, whose children you now are, if you do well, and are not put in fear by any terror.","understanding":"ὡς Σάρρα ὑπήκουσεν τῷ Ἀβραάμ, “as Sarah obeyed Abraham” — ὑπακούω, to listen and respond. κύριον αὐτὸν καλοῦσα, “calling him lord” — in Genesis 18:12 she says it to herself, a respectful word for ‘my husband’. ἧς ἐγενήθητε τέκνα, “whose children you now are” — Gentile women become daughters of Sarah by faith, as believers are children of Abraham. ἀγαθοποιοῦσαι, “if you do well” — ἀγαθοποιέω, from the good family, the ‘well-doing’ of 2:15, 20. καὶ μὴ φοβούμεναι μηδεμίαν πτόησιν, “and are not put in fear by any terror” — φοβέω and πτόησις, panic: submission is not fear-driven; it flows from trust in God (Proverbs 3:25). τέκνον is from the children family — a child — one born of a parent, family by birth.","words":[{"strongs":"G4564","hebrew":"Σάῤῥα","translit":"Sárrha","english":"Sarah"},{"strongs":"G5219","hebrew":"ὑπακούω","translit":"hypakoúō","english":"obeyed"},{"strongs":"G11","hebrew":"Ἀβραάμ","translit":"Abraám","english":"Abraham"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"lord"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"},{"strongs":"G15","hebrew":"ἀγαθοποιέω","translit":"agathopoiéō","english":"do well"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"put in fear"},{"strongs":"G4423","hebrew":"πτόησις","translit":"ptóēsis","english":"terror"}]},{"v":7,"text":"You husbands, in the same way, live with your wives according to knowledge, giving honor to the woman, as to the weaker vessel, as being also joint heirs of the grace of life; that your prayers may not be hindered.","understanding":"οἱ ἄνδρες ὁμοίως, “You husbands, in the same way” — the same Christlike pattern binds husbands. συνοικοῦντες κατὰ γνῶσιν, “live with your wives according to knowledge” — γνῶσις, from the know family: understanding her, as distinct from ἐπίγνωσις, full knowledge of God. ἀπονέμοντες τιμήν, “giving honor” — τιμή, the honour of 2:17. ὡς ἀσθενεστέρῳ σκεύει τῷ γυναικείῳ, “to the woman, as to the weaker vessel” — σκεῦος, a vessel, a jar; ἀσθενής, weaker in physical strength and in social power, not in worth or spirit. ὡς καὶ συγκληρονόμοις χάριτος ζωῆς, “as being also joint heirs of the grace of life” — συγκληρονόμος, co-heir; ζωή, from the life family. ‘Weaker’ is immediately balanced by ‘equal heirs’. εἰς τὸ μὴ ἐγκόπτεσθαι τὰς προσευχὰς ὑμῶν, “that your prayers may not be hindered” — προσευχή, from the pray family; ἐγκόπτω, to cut into, as soldiers break up a road. A husband who dishonours his wife finds his prayers blocked.","words":[{"strongs":"G435","hebrew":"ἀνήρ","translit":"anḗr","english":"husbands"},{"strongs":"G3668","hebrew":"ὁμοίως","translit":"homoíōs","english":"in the same way"},{"strongs":"G4924","hebrew":"συνοικέω","translit":"synoikéō","english":"live with"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G632","hebrew":"ἀπονέμω","translit":"aponémō","english":"giving"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1134","hebrew":"γυναικεῖος","translit":"gynaikeîos","english":"woman"},{"strongs":"G772","hebrew":"ἀσθενής","translit":"asthenḗs","english":"weaker"},{"strongs":"G4632","hebrew":"σκεῦος","translit":"skeûos","english":"vessel"},{"strongs":"G4789","hebrew":"συγκληρονόμος","translit":"synklēronómos","english":"joint heirs"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayers"},{"strongs":"G1465","hebrew":"ἐγκόπτω","translit":"enkóptō","english":"hindered"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Finally, be all like-minded, compassionate, loving as brothers, tender hearted, courteous,","understanding":"τὸ δὲ τέλος, “Finally” — τέλος, from the perfect family: the goal, the sum of it all. Five qualities for the whole church: ὁμόφρονες, “like-minded” — one mind, the same direction; συμπαθεῖς, “compassionate” — from the mercy family: σύν + πάσχω, suffering with, feeling another's pain; φιλάδελφοι, “loving as brothers” — from the love family, the adjective of φιλαδελφία (1:22); εὔσπλαγχνοι, “tender hearted” — from the mercy family, compassion felt deep inside; φιλόφρονες, “courteous” — from the love family: friendly-minded. Many early manuscripts read ταπεινόφρονες instead, ‘humble-minded’, related to the ταπεινός of 5:5.","words":[{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"Finally"},{"strongs":"G3675","hebrew":"ὁμόφρων","translit":"homóphrōn","english":"like-minded"},{"strongs":"G4835","hebrew":"συμπαθής","translit":"sympathḗs","english":"compassionate"},{"strongs":"G5361","hebrew":"φιλάδελφος","translit":"philádelphos","english":"loving as brothers"},{"strongs":"G2155","hebrew":"εὔσπλαγχνος","translit":"eúsplanchnos","english":"tender hearted"},{"strongs":"G5391","hebrew":"φιλόφρων","translit":"philóphrōn","english":"courteous"}]},{"v":9,"text":"not rendering evil for evil, or insult for insult; but instead blessing; knowing that to this were you called, that you may inherit a blessing.","understanding":"μὴ ἀποδιδόντες κακὸν ἀντὶ κακοῦ ἢ λοιδορίαν ἀντὶ λοιδορίας, “not rendering evil for evil, or insult for insult” — κακός, from the evil family: harm; λοιδορία, verbal abuse, the noun of λοιδορέω in 2:23, where Christ ‘didn’t curse back’. τοὐναντίον δὲ εὐλογοῦντες, “but instead blessing” — εὐλογέω, to speak well of, to call down good. ὅτι εἰς τοῦτο ἐκλήθητε, “knowing that to this were you called” — the same calling as 2:21. ἵνα εὐλογίαν κληρονομήσητε, “that you may inherit a blessing” — bless, and you will be blessed.","words":[{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"rendering"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G3059","hebrew":"λοιδορία","translit":"loidoría","english":"insult"},{"strongs":"G2127","hebrew":"εὐλογέω","translit":"eulogéō","english":"blessing"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G2816","hebrew":"κληρονομέω","translit":"klēronoméō","english":"inherit"},{"strongs":"G2129","hebrew":"εὐλογία","translit":"eulogía","english":"blessing"}]},{"v":10,"text":"For, “He who would love life, and see good days, let him keep his tongue from evil, and his lips from speaking deceit.","understanding":"Peter quotes Psalm 34:12–16. “For, ‘He who would love life, and see good days’” — θέλω, to will; ἀγαπάω, from the love family; ζωή, from the life family; εἴδω, from the see family (and the know family): to see, to experience; ἡμέρα, from the time family; ἀγαθός, from the good family. “let him keep his tongue from evil” — παύω, to stop; γλῶσσα, the tongue of James 3; κακός, from the evil family. “and his lips from speaking deceit” — χεῖλος, lips; δόλος, the deceit of 2:1 and 2:22. Peter had learned the cost of a careless tongue in the high priest's courtyard.","words":[{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"would"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"see"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G3973","hebrew":"παύω","translit":"paúō","english":"keep"},{"strongs":"G1100","hebrew":"γλῶσσα","translit":"glōssa","english":"tongue"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G5491","hebrew":"χεῖλος","translit":"cheîlos","english":"lips"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaking"},{"strongs":"G1388","hebrew":"δόλος","translit":"dólos","english":"deceit"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Let him turn away from evil, and do good. Let him seek peace, and pursue it.","understanding":"“Let him turn away from evil, and do good” — ἐκκλίνω, to turn aside, to swerve; κακός, from the evil family, and ἀγαθός, from the good family. “Let him seek peace, and pursue it” — ζητέω, to look for; διώκω, to chase, the same verb as ‘persecute’: chase peace as hard as persecutors chase you. εἰρήνη, the wholeness of shalom.","words":[{"strongs":"G1578","hebrew":"ἐκκλίνω","translit":"ekklínō","english":"turn away"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"seek"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G1377","hebrew":"διώκω","translit":"diṓkō","english":"pursue"}]},{"v":12,"text":"For the eyes of the Lord are on the righteous, and his ears open to their prayer; but the face of the Lord is against those who do evil.”","understanding":"“For the eyes of the Lord are on the righteous, and his ears open to their prayer” — ὀφθαλμός and οὖς: God watches and listens; δίκαιος, righteous; δέησις, from the pray family: a specific petition, the word James used of powerful prayer (James 5:16). “but the face of the Lord is against those who do evil” — πρόσωπον, the face turned towards in favour or against in judgement; ποιοῦντας κακά, from the evil family.","words":[{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G3775","hebrew":"οὖς","translit":"oûs","english":"ears"},{"strongs":"G1162","hebrew":"δέησις","translit":"déēsis","english":"prayer"},{"strongs":"G4383","hebrew":"πρόσωπον","translit":"prósōpon","english":"face"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G2556","hebrew":"κακός","translit":"kakós","english":"evil"}]},{"v":13,"text":"Now who is he who will harm you, if you become imitators of that which is good?","understanding":"καὶ τίς ὁ κακώσων ὑμᾶς, “Now who is he who will harm you” — κακόω, from the evil family: to do evil to, to injure. ἐὰν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ μιμηταὶ γένησθε, “if you become imitators of that which is good?” — μιμητής, an imitator (many manuscripts read ζηλωταί, ‘zealous for’); ἀγαθός, from the good family. Generally, doing good protects; but Peter immediately admits the exception (v. 14). No one can do you ultimate harm.","words":[{"strongs":"G2559","hebrew":"κακόω","translit":"kakóō","english":"harm"},{"strongs":"G3402","hebrew":"μιμητής","translit":"mimētḗs","english":"imitators"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"}]},{"v":14,"text":"But even if you should suffer for righteousness’ sake, you are blessed. “Don’t fear what they fear, neither be troubled.”","understanding":"ἀλλ᾽ εἰ καὶ πάσχοιτε διὰ δικαιοσύνην, μακάριοι, “But even if you should suffer for righteousness’ sake, you are blessed” — πάσχω, from the suffer family; δικαιοσύνη; μακάριος, the word of the Beatitudes: Jesus' words, “Blessed are those who have been persecuted for righteousness’ sake” (Matthew 5:10). Then Isaiah 8:12: “Don’t fear what they fear, neither be troubled” — φόβος, φοβέω; ταράσσω, to stir up, agitate, as water is stirred.","words":[{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffer"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G5399","hebrew":"φοβέω","translit":"phobéō","english":"fear"},{"strongs":"G5015","hebrew":"ταράσσω","translit":"tarássō","english":"troubled"}]},{"v":15,"text":"But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts; and always be ready to give an answer to everyone who asks you a reason concerning the hope that is in you, with humility and fear:","understanding":"κύριον δὲ τὸν θεὸν ἁγιάσατε ἐν ταῖς καρδίαις ὑμῶν, “But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts” — ἁγιάζω, to set apart as holy: enthrone Him as Lord of your inner life; instead of fearing people, fear Him. The WEB follows manuscripts reading ‘the Lord God’; the earliest read κύριον τὸν Χριστόν, ‘Christ as Lord’, applying Isaiah 8:13 directly to Jesus. ἕτοιμοι ἀεὶ πρὸς ἀπολογίαν, “and always be ready to give an answer” — ἀπολογία, a defence, as in a law court. παντὶ τῷ αἰτοῦντι ὑμᾶς λόγον, “to everyone who asks you a reason” — αἰτέω, from the pray family, here ‘ask’ of people; λόγος, from the word family, here an account, a reasoned explanation. περὶ τῆς ἐν ὑμῖν ἐλπίδος, “concerning the hope that is in you” — the living hope of 1:3. μετὰ πραΰτητος καὶ φόβου, “with humility and fear” — πραΰτης, from the humble family: meekness, gentleness; φόβος towards God. ἁγιάζω is from the holy family — to make holy, to set apart for God, to treat as holy.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G37","hebrew":"ἁγιάζω","translit":"hagiázō","english":"sanctify"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"},{"strongs":"G2092","hebrew":"ἕτοιμος","translit":"hétoimos","english":"ready"},{"strongs":"G627","hebrew":"ἀπολογία","translit":"apología","english":"answer"},{"strongs":"G154","hebrew":"αἰτέω","translit":"aitéō","english":"asks"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"reason"},{"strongs":"G1680","hebrew":"ἐλπίς","translit":"elpís","english":"hope"},{"strongs":"G4240","hebrew":"πραΰτης","translit":"praÿtēs","english":"humility"},{"strongs":"G5401","hebrew":"φόβος","translit":"phóbos","english":"fear"}]},{"v":16,"text":"having a good conscience; that, while you are spoken against as evildoers, they may be disappointed who curse your good way of life in Christ.","understanding":"συνείδησιν ἔχοντες ἀγαθήν, “having a good conscience” — ἀγαθός, from the good family; συνείδησις, the inner awareness before God (2:19). ἵνα ἐν ᾧ καταλαλεῖσθε, “that, while you are spoken against” — καταλαλέω, as in 2:12. ὡς κακοποιῶν, “as evildoers” — from the evil family. καταισχυνθῶσιν, “they may be disappointed” — put to shame, the opposite of 2:6. οἱ ἐπηρεάζοντες ὑμῶν τὴν ἀγαθὴν ἐν Χριστῷ ἀναστροφήν, “who curse your good way of life in Christ” — ἐπηρεάζω, to insult, mistreat (Luke 6:28). Your answer is your words (v. 15); your proof is your life.","words":[{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G2635","hebrew":"καταλαλέω","translit":"katalaléō","english":"spoken against"},{"strongs":"G2555","hebrew":"κακοποιός","translit":"kakopoiós","english":"evildoers"},{"strongs":"G2617","hebrew":"καταισχύνω","translit":"kataischýnō","english":"disappointed"},{"strongs":"G1908","hebrew":"ἐπηρεάζω","translit":"epēreázō","english":"curse"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"way of life"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For it is better, if it is God’s will, that you suffer for doing well than for doing evil.","understanding":"κρεῖττον γάρ, “For it is better” — κρείττων, the ‘better’ of Hebrews. εἰ θέλοι τὸ θέλημα τοῦ θεοῦ, “if it is God’s will” — θέλημα: suffering is never outside God's will for His people. πάσχειν ἀγαθοποιοῦντας ἢ κακοποιοῦντας, “that you suffer for doing well than for doing evil” — πάσχω, from the suffer family; ἀγαθοποιέω, from the good family; κακοποιέω, from the evil family. Two opposite words built on the same pattern.","words":[{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffer"},{"strongs":"G15","hebrew":"ἀγαθοποιέω","translit":"agathopoiéō","english":"doing well"},{"strongs":"G2554","hebrew":"κακοποιέω","translit":"kakopoiéō","english":"doing evil"}]},{"v":18,"text":"Because Christ also suffered for sins once, the righteous for the unrighteous, that he might bring you to God; being put to death in the flesh, but made alive in the spirit;","understanding":"ὅτι καὶ Χριστὸς ἅπαξ περὶ ἁμαρτιῶν ἔπαθεν, “Because Christ also suffered for sins once” — πάσχω, from the suffer family (many manuscripts read ἀπέθανεν, ‘died’); ἅπαξ, once for all, never to be repeated (Hebrews 9:28); ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. δίκαιος ὑπὲρ ἀδίκων, “the righteous for the unrighteous” — substitution in four words. ἵνα ὑμᾶς προσαγάγῃ τῷ θεῷ, “that he might bring you to God” — προσάγω, to lead into the presence, as a priest leads a worshipper to the altar or a courtier brings someone before the king. θανατωθεὶς μὲν σαρκί, “being put to death in the flesh” — θανατόω; σάρξ, His human, earthly life. ζῳοποιηθεὶς δὲ πνεύματι, “but made alive in the spirit” — ζωοποιέω, from the life family: given life, in the realm of the Spirit — His resurrection life.","words":[{"strongs":"G530","hebrew":"ἅπαξ","translit":"hápax","english":"once"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G94","hebrew":"ἄδικος","translit":"ádikos","english":"unrighteous"},{"strongs":"G4317","hebrew":"προσάγω","translit":"proságō","english":"bring"},{"strongs":"G2289","hebrew":"θανατόω","translit":"thanatóō","english":"put to death"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G2227","hebrew":"ζωοποιέω","translit":"zōopoiéō","english":"made alive"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"}]},{"v":19,"text":"in which he also went and preached to the spirits in prison,","understanding":"ἐν ᾧ καὶ τοῖς ἐν φυλακῇ πνεύμασιν πορευθεὶς ἐκήρυξεν, “in which he also went and preached to the spirits in prison” — one of the hardest verses in the New Testament. πορεύομαι, to go; φυλακή, a prison; πνεῦμα, used in the New Testament without qualification almost always of spiritual beings rather than human souls; κηρύσσω, to proclaim as a herald. The strongest reading, in light of verse 22 and 2 Peter 2:4–5, is that the risen Christ, on His way to heaven, proclaimed His victory to the rebellious spirits imprisoned since the days of Noah (Genesis 6:1–4; Jude 6). Others understand it as Christ preaching through Noah to people of his day, or preaching to the dead between His death and resurrection; Scripture nowhere teaches a second chance after death.","words":[{"strongs":"G4198","hebrew":"πορεύομαι","translit":"poreúomai","english":"went"},{"strongs":"G2784","hebrew":"κηρύσσω","translit":"kērýssō","english":"preached"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirits"},{"strongs":"G5438","hebrew":"φυλακή","translit":"phylakḗ","english":"prison"}]},{"v":20,"text":"who before were disobedient, when God waited patiently in the days of Noah, while the ship was being built. In it, few, that is, eight souls, were saved through water.","understanding":"ἀπειθήσασίν ποτε, “who before were disobedient” — ἀπειθέω, the word of 2:7–8 and 3:1. ὅτε ἀπεξεδέχετο ἡ τοῦ θεοῦ μακροθυμία, “when God waited patiently” — literally ‘the patience of God waited’: μακροθυμία, from the endurance family: long temper, God's refusal to give up quickly (2 Peter 3:9); ἀπεκδέχομαι, to wait eagerly. ἐν ἡμέραις Νῶε κατασκευαζομένης κιβωτοῦ, “in the days of Noah, while the ship was being built” — ἡμέρα, from the time family; κιβωτός, the ark, a great box. εἰς ἣν ὀλίγοι, τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν ὀκτὼ ψυχαί, διεσώθησαν δι᾽ ὕδατος, “In it, few, that is, eight souls, were saved through water” — ψυχή, from the life family: persons; διασῴζω, to bring safely through. The same water that judged the world carried the ark.","words":[{"strongs":"G544","hebrew":"ἀπειθέω","translit":"apeithéō","english":"disobedient"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"patiently"},{"strongs":"G553","hebrew":"ἀπεκδέχομαι","translit":"apekdéchomai","english":"waited"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G3575","hebrew":"Νῶε","translit":"Nōe","english":"Noah"},{"strongs":"G2680","hebrew":"κατασκευάζω","translit":"kataskeuázō","english":"being built"},{"strongs":"G2787","hebrew":"κιβωτός","translit":"kibōtós","english":"ship"},{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"few"},{"strongs":"G3638","hebrew":"ὀκτώ","translit":"oktṓ","english":"eight"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G1295","hebrew":"διασώζω","translit":"diasṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"}]},{"v":21,"text":"This is a symbol of baptism, which now saves you—not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but the answer of a good conscience toward God, through the resurrection of Jesus Christ,","understanding":"ὃ καὶ ὑμᾶς ἀντίτυπον νῦν σῴζει βάπτισμα, “This is a symbol of baptism, which now saves you” — ἀντίτυπος, from the pattern family: the counterpart that matches the original type. The flood is the type; baptism is the antitype. σῴζω, from the heal family: to rescue and make whole. Then Peter guards against misunderstanding: οὐ σαρκὸς ἀπόθεσις ῥύπου, “not the putting away of the filth of the flesh” — not a bath, not the ritual in itself. ἀλλὰ συνειδήσεως ἀγαθῆς ἐπερώτημα εἰς θεόν, “but the answer of a good conscience toward God” — ἀγαθός, from the good family, as in verse 16; ἐπερώτημα, a request or pledge: the formal answer given at baptism, a commitment and an appeal to God. δι᾽ ἀναστάσεως Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “through the resurrection of Jesus Christ” — ἀνάστασις, from the rise family: the power that saves is Christ's resurrection, received by faith and confessed in baptism.","words":[{"strongs":"G499","hebrew":"ἀντίτυπον","translit":"antítypon","english":"symbol"},{"strongs":"G908","hebrew":"βάπτισμα","translit":"báptisma","english":"baptism"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saves"},{"strongs":"G595","hebrew":"ἀπόθεσις","translit":"apóthesis","english":"putting away"},{"strongs":"G4509","hebrew":"ῥύπος","translit":"rhýpos","english":"filth"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G4893","hebrew":"συνείδησις","translit":"syneídēsis","english":"conscience"},{"strongs":"G18","hebrew":"ἀγαθός","translit":"agathós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G1906","hebrew":"ἐπερώτημα","translit":"eperṓtēma","english":"answer"},{"strongs":"G386","hebrew":"ἀνάστασις","translit":"anástasis","english":"resurrection"}]},{"v":22,"text":"who is at the right hand of God, having gone into heaven, angels and authorities and powers being made subject to him.","understanding":"ὅς ἐστιν ἐν δεξιᾷ θεοῦ, “who is at the right hand of God” — the place of honour and authority (Psalm 110:1). πορευθεὶς εἰς οὐρανόν, “having gone into heaven” — the same ‘went’ as verse 19. ὑποταγέντων αὐτῷ ἀγγέλων καὶ ἐξουσιῶν καὶ δυνάμεων, “angels and authorities and powers being made subject to him” — ὑποτάσσω, the word Peter used for believers' submission (2:13, 18; 3:1), now used of every spiritual being. ἐξουσία and δύναμις, two words from the power family: ἐξουσία, the right to rule, and δύναμις, the ability to act. Every authority and every capability in the unseen realm is under Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G1188","hebrew":"δεξιός","translit":"dexiós","english":"right hand"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4198","hebrew":"πορεύομαι","translit":"poreúomai","english":"gone"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G1849","hebrew":"ἐξουσία","translit":"exousía","english":"authorities"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"powers"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"being made subject"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Sermon Without Words","verseRef":"v. 1-4","image":"Picture a woman in a pagan household who has become a Christian. Her husband mocks her faith, refuses to come to church and forbids her to talk about Jesus. She stops arguing. But day after day he watches: she is honest, kind, patient, unafraid. She does not dress to impress, yet there is a beauty about her he cannot explain. One evening, years later, he quietly asks, ‘Tell me about your God.’","meaning":"“so that, even if any don’t obey the Word, they may be won by the behavior of their wives without a word” (v. 1), “seeing your pure behavior in fear” (v. 2). The beauty that wins people is “in the hidden person of the heart, in the incorruptible adornment of a gentle and quiet spirit, which is in the sight of God very precious” (v. 4). Some people will never listen to your words, but they are watching your life."},{"title":"Blessing for Insult","verseRef":"v. 8-12","image":"Picture a man insulted in public. Everyone waits for him to hit back. Instead he pauses, looks at the man who insulted him, and says, ‘May God bless you and your family.’ The crowd falls silent. The insulter does not know what to do. Above them, unseen, the eyes of the Lord are watching, and His ears are open.","meaning":"“not rendering evil for evil, or insult for insult; but instead blessing; knowing that to this were you called, that you may inherit a blessing” (v. 9). “For the eyes of the Lord are on the righteous, and his ears open to their prayer” (v. 12). Blessing your enemies is not weakness; it is trust that God sees, hears and will judge."},{"title":"Ready to Answer","verseRef":"v. 14-16","image":"Picture a courtroom. A believer stands accused, the room hostile, the judge stern. Someone asks, ‘Why do you still have hope?’ The believer does not panic. With a calm voice and a gentle manner, she explains who Jesus is, what He has done and why her hope is alive. Her accusers have nothing to say, because her life matches her words.","meaning":"“But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts; and always be ready to give an answer to everyone who asks you a reason concerning the hope that is in you, with humility and fear” (v. 15). The best defence of the faith is a heart where Christ is Lord, a mind that can explain the hope, a manner that is gentle, and a conscience that is clean (v. 16)."},{"title":"The Ark and the Water","verseRef":"v. 18-22","image":"Picture rain pouring for days, rivers bursting, the whole world sinking under water. A great wooden ship rises on the flood, and inside it eight people are safe while everything outside perishes. Then picture a river where a new believer goes down under the water and comes up again, and above the scene a risen Christ ascending to the right hand of God, with every power in the universe bowing before Him.","meaning":"“Because Christ also suffered for sins once, the righteous for the unrighteous, that he might bring you to God” (v. 18). The flood was judgement, but it also carried Noah's family to safety. Baptism is its counterpart: not washing the body, but “the answer of a good conscience toward God, through the resurrection of Jesus Christ” (v. 21). The One you trusted is now at God's right hand, with “angels and authorities and powers being made subject to him” (v. 22)."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Wives and Husbands (v. 1-7)","body":"Wives, submit to your husbands so that unbelieving husbands may be won without a word by your pure conduct; let your beauty be the inner beauty of a gentle and quiet spirit, as with the holy women of old like Sarah; husbands, live with your wives with understanding, honouring them as fellow heirs of the grace of life, so that your prayers are not hindered."},{"label":"Blessing Instead of Revenge (v. 8-12)","body":"Be united, sympathetic, loving, compassionate and humble; do not repay evil for evil or insult for insult, but bless, for you were called to inherit a blessing; Psalm 34 promises that the Lord watches the righteous and opposes evildoers."},{"label":"Suffering for Righteousness (v. 13-17)","body":"Who will harm you if you are zealous for good? Even if you suffer for righteousness you are blessed; do not fear, but set apart Christ as Lord in your hearts, always ready to give a gentle and respectful defence of your hope, keeping a good conscience so that slanderers are put to shame; it is better to suffer for doing good than for doing evil."},{"label":"Christ's Suffering and Victory (v. 18-22)","body":"Christ suffered once for sins, the righteous for the unrighteous, to bring us to God; put to death in the flesh, made alive in the spirit, He proclaimed to the imprisoned spirits who disobeyed in Noah's day, when eight were saved through water; baptism, its counterpart, now saves — not by washing but as a good conscience's pledge to God through the resurrection of Jesus Christ, who has gone into heaven and sits at God's right hand with all angels, authorities and powers subject to Him."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Homes That Preach","paragraphs":["Peter applies the pattern of Christ's suffering and trust (2:21–25) to marriage. He speaks first to wives, many of whom were married to unbelievers — a dangerous position in a world where a wife was expected to follow her husband's religion. Peter does not tell them to leave or to preach at their husbands. He tells them to submit, “so that, even if any don’t obey the Word, they may be won by the behavior of their wives without a word” (v. 1). A godly life is the most persuasive sermon. Paul offers the same hope: “For how do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband?” (1 Corinthians 7:16).","Submission here is voluntary, directed to ‘your own husband’, motivated by reverence for God, and never a call to obey commands to sin or to live in terror — “and are not put in fear by any terror” (v. 6). Its beauty is inward: “the hidden person of the heart, in the incorruptible adornment of a gentle and quiet spirit” (v. 4). Gold fades and fashions change; a meek and settled spirit is precious to God forever.","Then Peter turns to husbands — remarkable in a culture that gave husbands almost total power. “You husbands, in the same way, live with your wives according to knowledge, giving honor to the woman, as to the weaker vessel, as being also joint heirs of the grace of life” (v. 7). The wife may be physically weaker and socially more vulnerable, but before God she is a co-heir, fully equal. Paul says husbands must love their wives “even as Christ also loved the assembly, and gave himself up for it” (Ephesians 5:25). And Peter adds a solemn warning: the husband who dishonours his wife will find his prayers hindered. How a man treats his wife affects his relationship with God."]},{"heading":"Bless, and Be Ready","paragraphs":["Verse 8 widens to the whole church: “Finally, be all like-minded, compassionate, loving as brothers, tender hearted, courteous.” Three of these words come from the love and mercy families. Then comes the hardest command: “not rendering evil for evil, or insult for insult; but instead blessing” (v. 9). This is the teaching of Jesus: “love your enemies, bless those who curse you, do good to those who hate you” (Matthew 5:44). Paul says the same: “Repay no one evil for evil” (Romans 12:17).","Peter supports it with Psalm 34, a psalm about a man who escaped danger by God's help. The one who wants to “love life, and see good days” must guard his tongue, turn from evil, do good and chase peace (vv. 10–11). James said the tongue is a fire; Peter, who once cursed and swore in the courtyard, now quotes the psalm with deep conviction.","Even so, believers may suffer. “But even if you should suffer for righteousness’ sake, you are blessed” (v. 14). The answer to fear is a heart where Christ is enthroned: “But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts; and always be ready to give an answer to everyone who asks you a reason concerning the hope that is in you, with humility and fear” (v. 15). This verse gives the church's whole approach to defending the faith: Christ as Lord inside, readiness to explain, a gentle and respectful manner, and a clear conscience that makes slander look foolish. Paul agrees: “Let your speech always be with grace, seasoned with salt, that you may know how you ought to answer each one” (Colossians 4:6)."]},{"heading":"The Spirits in Prison and the Victory of Christ","paragraphs":["Verses 18–22 begin with one of the clearest statements of the atonement in Scripture: “Because Christ also suffered for sins once, the righteous for the unrighteous, that he might bring you to God; being put to death in the flesh, but made alive in the spirit” (v. 18). The death was once for all. It was substitutionary — the righteous for the unrighteous. Its goal was relational — to bring you to God. And it ended in life — Christ was made alive.","Then come the most debated verses of the letter: “in which he also went and preached to the spirits in prison, who before were disobedient, when God waited patiently in the days of Noah” (vv. 19–20). Three main readings have been held. The strongest, given the word ‘spirits’ (usually used of supernatural beings), the mention of angels and powers in verse 22, and Peter's own words in 2 Peter 2:4–5, is that the risen Christ proclaimed His triumph to the rebellious spirits imprisoned since Noah's time — a herald announcing victory, not an offer of salvation. Others read it as Christ preaching through Noah to his generation, or preaching to the dead between His death and resurrection; but nowhere does Scripture offer a second chance of salvation after death, and Peter's purpose is to show Christ's total victory over every hostile power.","That victory gives meaning to baptism. Noah's family was brought safely through the water that judged the world. Baptism is its counterpart: “This is a symbol of baptism, which now saves you—not the putting away of the filth of the flesh, but the answer of a good conscience toward God, through the resurrection of Jesus Christ” (v. 21). Peter is careful: water does not wash away sin by itself. What saves is the risen Christ, received by faith and confessed in baptism as a believer's pledge and appeal to God. Paul says, “We were buried therefore with him through baptism into death, that just as Christ was raised from the dead through the glory of the Father, so we also might walk in newness of life” (Romans 6:4). And the chapter ends where every suffering believer needs to look: Christ “is at the right hand of God, having gone into heaven, angels and authorities and powers being made subject to him” (v. 22)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Christ Over Every Power","paragraphs":["Peter's picture of the exalted Christ is shared by Paul. God “raised him from the dead, and made him to sit at his right hand in the heavenly places” (Ephesians 1:20), “far above all rule, and authority, and power, and dominion, and every name that is named, not only in this age, but also in that which is to come” (Ephesians 1:21). At the cross Christ disarmed the powers: “having stripped the principalities and the powers, he made a show of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (Colossians 2:15). Peter's ‘spirits in prison’ heard the herald of that triumph.","Noah appears elsewhere as a sign of judgement and salvation. Jesus said, “As the days of Noah were, so will be the coming of the Son of Man” (Matthew 24:37). Hebrews says, “By faith, Noah, being warned about things not yet seen, moved with godly fear, prepared a ship for the saving of his house, through which he condemned the world” (Hebrews 11:7). And Peter returns to him in his second letter: God “didn’t spare the ancient world, but preserved Noah with seven others, a preacher of righteousness, when he brought a flood on the world of the ungodly” (2 Peter 2:5).","The call to answer with gentleness and to bless instead of curse is how Peter himself had to learn. In Gethsemane he drew a sword; Jesus said, “Put your sword back into its place, for all those who take the sword will die by the sword” (Matthew 26:52). Now the man who once fought with a sword teaches the church to fight with blessing, gentle answers and good lives."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Adorn","hebrew":"κοσμέω","translit":"kosméō","strongs":"G2885 (adorning κόσμος, kósmos, G2889)","verses":"v. 3, 5","insight":"Strong's: “to put in proper order, i.e. decorate (literally or figuratively); specially, to snuff (a wick).” κοσμέω and its noun κόσμος originally meant ‘order, arrangement, decoration’ — the beautiful order of the universe, which is why κόσμος came to mean ‘world’. In verse 3 the WEB translates κόσμος as ‘adorning’, its original sense; our word ‘cosmetic’ comes from it. Peter does not despise beauty; he relocates it. The adornment God values is the order and beauty of a gentle, quiet, trusting heart."},{"english":"Gentle","hebrew":"πραΰς","translit":"praÿs","strongs":"G4239 (quiet ἡσύχιος, hēsýchios, G2272)","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “mild, i.e. (by implication) humble.” πραΰς is meekness — strength under control, not weakness. Jesus used it of Himself: ‘gentle and humble in heart’ (Matthew 11:29). It belongs to the humble family. Paired with ἡσύχιος, ‘quiet, settled, at rest’, it describes a person who does not need to fight for position or panic under pressure, because she trusts God. Peter uses the noun πραΰτης in verse 15 for the manner of a believer's defence of the faith."},{"english":"Answer / defence","hebrew":"ἀπολογία","translit":"apología","strongs":"G627","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “a plea (\"apology\")”. ἀπολογία was the formal speech of defence in a court of law; Paul uses it of his defence before Agrippa (Acts 26:2) and of ‘the defence of the Good News’ (Philippians 1:16). From it comes ‘apologetics’, the reasoned defence of the faith. Peter expects every believer, not only scholars, to be ready to explain the hope within them — with reasons (λόγος), but also with meekness and reverence. A cold, proud argument wins debates and loses people."},{"english":"Symbol / antitype","hebrew":"ἀντίτυπον","translit":"antítypon","strongs":"G499 (baptism βάπτισμα, báptisma, G908)","verses":"v. 21","insight":"Strong's: “corresponding (\"antitype\"), i.e. a representative, counterpart”. ἀντίτυπος is from the pattern family: τύπος is the original impression, ἀντίτυπος the matching counterpart. Hebrews 9:24 uses it of the earthly holy place as a copy of the heavenly. Here the flood is the type and baptism its counterpart: in both, God brings His people through water that signifies judgement into new life. Baptism ‘saves’ because it is the sign and pledge of union with the risen Christ, not because water has power in itself."},{"english":"Answer / pledge","hebrew":"ἐπερώτημα","translit":"eperṓtēma","strongs":"G1906","verses":"v. 21","insight":"Strong's: “an inquiry.” ἐπερώτημα originally meant a question or request; in later Greek it was used of the formal question and answer by which a contract was made binding. That is probably the sense here: at baptism the believer answered the question of faith — ‘Do you believe? Do you renounce evil?’ — and so made a pledge to God from a good conscience. Many translations render it ‘appeal’ or ‘pledge’. Either way, baptism is not mere washing but a personal response of faith to God."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is dealing with your closest relationships, your response to insult, and your readiness to speak about your hope.","items":[{"title":"What Is Your Home Preaching?","body":"The Spirit asks what your spouse, your children or the people you live with see in you. Are they being won by your behaviour, or pushed away by it? Husbands: do you live with understanding and honour, or are your prayers being hindered (v. 7)? Wives: is your beauty in the hidden person of the heart (v. 4)? Single or married, ask Him what your daily conduct is saying about Christ to those closest to you."},{"title":"Who Can You Bless?","body":"Someone has insulted you or done you harm. Everything in you wants to repay it. The Spirit reminds you: “to this were you called, that you may inherit a blessing” (v. 9). Name the person. Then speak a blessing over them in prayer today — and if He leads you, in person. Watch what happens in your own heart."},{"title":"Could You Explain Your Hope?","body":"If a colleague asked you tomorrow, ‘Why do you still have hope?’, what would you say? The Spirit wants Christ enthroned in your heart and His hope ready on your lips (v. 15). Ask Him for an opportunity this week, and prepare a simple, honest answer — spoken with gentleness and respect."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Home Check","body":"Would those you live with say your faith makes you easier or harder to live with (vv. 1–7)? Is there an apology or an act of honour you owe someone at home?"},{"title":"The Revenge Check","body":"Have you repaid evil for evil or insult for insult this month (v. 9)? In what situation do you find it hardest to bless?"},{"title":"The Answer Check","body":"Could you explain in two minutes why you believe in Jesus and what your hope is (v. 15)? When you do speak about your faith, is it with gentleness and respect, or with pride and anger?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Write Your Two-Minute Answer","body":"This week, write down your answer to the question ‘What is the hope in you?’ in no more than 300 words: who Jesus is, what He has done for you, and why your hope is alive. Read it aloud until you can say it naturally. Then pray for one person to ask you, and be ready."},{"title":"Seven Days of Blessing","body":"For seven days, every time someone irritates, insults or mistreats you, respond with a silent prayer of blessing for them, and at least once with a spoken word of kindness. Keep a short record. At the end of the week, read verses 8–12 again and thank God for what He did in you."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You suffered for sins once for all, the righteous for the unrighteous, to bring me to God. Thank You that You were put to death in the flesh but made alive in the spirit, and that You now sit at the right hand of God with angels, authorities and powers subject to You. Let my home preach You. Make my conduct pure and reverent, my beauty that of a gentle and quiet spirit, and my love honouring and understanding, so that my prayers are not hindered. Make me like-minded, compassionate, loving, tender-hearted and humble. When I am insulted, give me grace not to insult back but to bless, for I was called to inherit a blessing. Guard my tongue from evil and my lips from deceit; help me turn from evil, do good, and chase after peace. When I suffer for doing right, let me know that I am blessed and not be afraid. I set You apart as Lord in my heart. Make me ready to give an answer to everyone who asks about the hope within me, with gentleness and respect and a good conscience. Thank You for the salvation You gave me through Your resurrection, and for the pledge I made to You in baptism. Keep me faithful to it. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Sarah, Daughter of Hope","ref":"Genesis 12:10-20; 18:1-15; 21:1-7","relatedVerses":"v. 5-6","summary":"Sarah followed Abraham from Ur to an unknown land, endured years of barrenness, and twice was put in danger by her husband's fear. When the Lord promised her a son in her old age, she laughed to herself, saying, ‘After I have grown old will I have pleasure, my lord being old also?’ — and Peter notes that even then she called him ‘lord’. Yet her hope was not finally in Abraham but in God, who kept His promise and gave her Isaac. Peter tells believing women that they are her daughters when they do good and are not terrified by fear: their trust, like hers, rests in God."},{"title":"Noah Builds the Ark","ref":"Genesis 6:5-8:22","relatedVerses":"v. 20-21","summary":"When the earth was filled with violence, God told Noah to build an ark and warned of a coming flood. For years Noah built while the world mocked, and God waited patiently. Then the rain came, the fountains of the deep burst open, and the waters covered the earth. Only eight people — Noah, his wife, his three sons and their wives — were brought safely through the water. The same flood that judged the world lifted the ark. Peter sees in it a picture of baptism: passing through the water of judgement into new life, saved by God's provision."},{"title":"Peter's Sword in the Garden","ref":"Matthew 26:47-56; John 18:10-11","relatedVerses":"v. 9, 15","summary":"When the soldiers came to arrest Jesus in Gethsemane, Peter drew his sword and cut off the ear of the high priest's servant, Malchus. Jesus stopped him: “Put your sword back into its place, for all those who take the sword will die by the sword” (Matthew 26:52), and healed the man's ear. Peter had tried to defend Jesus by force; within hours he was denying Him with curses. Now, years later, the same Peter writes that believers must not repay evil for evil, but bless, and must defend their hope with words spoken in gentleness and respect. He had learned that the kingdom is not defended with a sword."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Peter","chapter":4,"chapterTitle":"Armed with Christ's Mind in the Fiery Trial","themeTitle":"“Don’t Be Astonished at the Fiery Trial”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Therefore, since Christ suffered for us in the flesh, arm yourselves also with the same mind; for he who has suffered in the flesh has ceased from sin;","understanding":"Χριστοῦ οὖν παθόντος σαρκί, “Therefore, since Christ suffered for us in the flesh” — πάσχω, from the suffer family, picking up 3:18. καὶ ὑμεῖς τὴν αὐτὴν ἔννοιαν ὁπλίσασθε, “arm yourselves also with the same mind” — ὁπλίζω, to put on armour, a soldier's word; ἔννοια, a settled way of thinking, a resolve. ὅτι ὁ παθὼν σαρκὶ πέπαυται ἁμαρτίας, “for he who has suffered in the flesh has ceased from sin” — the one who has accepted suffering for Christ has broken with sin's rule; he has chosen God's will over comfort (Romans 6:7). ἁμαρτία, from the sin family.","words":[{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G3695","hebrew":"ὁπλίζω","translit":"hoplízō","english":"arm yourselves"},{"strongs":"G1771","hebrew":"ἔννοια","translit":"énnoia","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G3973","hebrew":"παύω","translit":"paúō","english":"ceased"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"}]},{"v":2,"text":"that you no longer should live the rest of your time in the flesh for the lusts of men, but for the will of God.","understanding":"εἰς τὸ μηκέτι ἀνθρώπων ἐπιθυμίαις ἀλλὰ θελήματι θεοῦ τὸν ἐπίλοιπον ἐν σαρκὶ βιῶσαι χρόνον, “that you no longer should live the rest of your time in the flesh for the lusts of men, but for the will of God” — βιόω, from the life family: to live out the course of an earthly life (βίος), as distinct from ζάω, simply to be alive. χρόνος, from the time family: the span of time left. The rest of your life has two possible masters: human desires (ἐπιθυμία) or God's θέλημα.","words":[{"strongs":"G1954","hebrew":"ἐπίλοιπος","translit":"epíloipos","english":"rest"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G980","hebrew":"βιόω","translit":"bióō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":3,"text":"For we have spent enough of our past time doing the desire of the Gentiles, and having walked in lewdness, lusts, drunken binges, orgies, carousings, and abominable idolatries.","understanding":"ἀρκετὸς γὰρ ὁ παρεληλυθὼς χρόνος, “For we have spent enough of our past time” — ἀρκετός, sufficient: the past was more than enough. χρόνος again, from the time family. τὸ βούλημα τῶν ἐθνῶν κατειργάσθαι, “doing the desire of the Gentiles” — βούλημα, what the pagan world wants; κατεργάζομαι, from the work family: to carry out fully. Then a list of pagan festival life: ἀσέλγεια, “lewdness” — shameless sensuality; ἐπιθυμία, “lusts”; οἰνοφλυγία, “drunken binges”; κῶμος, “orgies” — wild night processions; πότος, “carousings” — drinking parties; ἀθέμιτοι εἰδωλολατρίαι, “abominable idolatries” — forbidden worship. This was the readers' own former life.","words":[{"strongs":"G713","hebrew":"ἀρκετός","translit":"arketós","english":"enough"},{"strongs":"G3928","hebrew":"παρέρχομαι","translit":"parérchomai","english":"past"},{"strongs":"G5550","hebrew":"χρόνος","translit":"chrónos","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G1013","hebrew":"βούλημα","translit":"boúlēma","english":"desire"},{"strongs":"G2716","hebrew":"κατεργάζομαι","translit":"katergázomai","english":"doing"},{"strongs":"G1484","hebrew":"ἔθνος","translit":"éthnos","english":"Gentiles"},{"strongs":"G4198","hebrew":"πορεύομαι","translit":"poreúomai","english":"walked"},{"strongs":"G766","hebrew":"ἀσέλγεια","translit":"asélgeia","english":"lewdness"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G3632","hebrew":"οἰνοφλυγία","translit":"oinophlygía","english":"drunken binges"},{"strongs":"G2970","hebrew":"κῶμος","translit":"kōmos","english":"orgies"},{"strongs":"G4224","hebrew":"πότος","translit":"pótos","english":"carousings"},{"strongs":"G111","hebrew":"ἀθέμιτος","translit":"athémitos","english":"abominable"},{"strongs":"G1495","hebrew":"εἰδωλολατρεία","translit":"eidōlolatreía","english":"idolatries"}]},{"v":4,"text":"They think it is strange that you don’t run with them into the same excess of riot, blaspheming:","understanding":"ἐν ᾧ ξενίζονται, “They think it is strange” — ξενίζω, to find something foreign, surprising; the same verb appears in verse 12. μὴ συντρεχόντων ὑμῶν, “that you don’t run with them” — συντρέχω, to run together like a crowd in a stampede. εἰς τὴν αὐτὴν τῆς ἀσωτίας ἀνάχυσιν, “into the same excess of riot” — ἀνάχυσις, an overflowing flood; ἀσωτία, reckless waste, the word for the prodigal son's ‘riotous living’. βλασφημοῦντες, “blaspheming” — they slander the believers and, through them, God.","words":[{"strongs":"G3579","hebrew":"ξενίζω","translit":"xenízō","english":"think it is strange"},{"strongs":"G4936","hebrew":"συντρέχω","translit":"syntréchō","english":"run with"},{"strongs":"G401","hebrew":"ἀνάχυσις","translit":"anáchysis","english":"excess"},{"strongs":"G810","hebrew":"ἀσωτία","translit":"asōtía","english":"riot"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blaspheming"}]},{"v":5,"text":"who will give account to him who is ready to judge the living and the dead.","understanding":"οἳ ἀποδώσουσιν λόγον, “who will give account” — λόγος, from the word family, here a reckoning, an account rendered. τῷ ἑτοίμως ἔχοντι κρῖναι ζῶντας καὶ νεκρούς, “to him who is ready to judge the living and the dead” — ἑτοίμως, ready now; ζάω, from the life family. The scoffers will answer to Christ, the Judge (Acts 10:42). κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G591","hebrew":"ἀποδίδωμι","translit":"apodídōmi","english":"give"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"account"},{"strongs":"G2093","hebrew":"ἑτοίμως","translit":"hetoímōs","english":"ready"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judge"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"}]},{"v":6,"text":"For to this end the Good News was preached even to the dead, that they might be judged indeed as men in the flesh, but live as to God in the spirit.","understanding":"εἰς τοῦτο γὰρ καὶ νεκροῖς εὐηγγελίσθη, “For to this end the Good News was preached even to the dead” — εὐαγγελίζω. The strongest reading, following verse 5, is that the gospel was preached to people while they were alive who have since died: believers who were mocked and judged by people, and then died — as their pagan neighbours said, ‘What good did their faith do them?’ ἵνα κριθῶσι μὲν κατὰ ἀνθρώπους σαρκί, “that they might be judged indeed as men in the flesh” — condemned by human verdict, and they died like everyone else. ζῶσι δὲ κατὰ θεὸν πνεύματι, “but live as to God in the spirit” — ζάω, from the life family: alive to God. It is not a second chance after death, which Scripture never offers. κρίνω is from the judge family — to judge — to decide, try, condemn or vindicate.","words":[{"strongs":"G3498","hebrew":"νεκρός","translit":"nekrós","english":"dead"},{"strongs":"G2097","hebrew":"εὐαγγελίζω","translit":"euangelízō","english":"Good News was preached"},{"strongs":"G2919","hebrew":"κρίνω","translit":"krínō","english":"judged"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G2198","hebrew":"ζάω","translit":"záō","english":"live"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"spirit"}]},{"v":7,"text":"But the end of all things is near. Therefore be of sound mind, self-controlled, and sober in prayer.","understanding":"πάντων δὲ τὸ τέλος ἤγγικεν, “But the end of all things is near” — τέλος, from the perfect family: the goal, the end towards which history is moving; ἐγγίζω, the ‘at hand’ of James 5:8. σωφρονήσατε οὖν καὶ νήψατε εἰς προσευχάς, “Therefore be of sound mind, self-controlled, and sober in prayer” — two words from the self-control family: σωφρονέω, to keep a sound, balanced mind, and νήφω, to be sober, clear-headed, free from every kind of intoxication. προσευχή, from the pray family. The nearness of the end does not call for panic or speculation but for clear-headed prayer.","words":[{"strongs":"G5056","hebrew":"τέλος","translit":"télos","english":"end"},{"strongs":"G1448","hebrew":"ἐγγίζω","translit":"engízō","english":"near"},{"strongs":"G4993","hebrew":"σωφρονέω","translit":"sōphronéō","english":"be of sound mind"},{"strongs":"G3525","hebrew":"νήφω","translit":"nḗphō","english":"sober"},{"strongs":"G4335","hebrew":"προσευχή","translit":"proseuchḗ","english":"prayer"}]},{"v":8,"text":"And above all things be earnest in your love among yourselves, for love covers a multitude of sins.","understanding":"πρὸ πάντων, ‘above all things’ — the very phrase James used before his warning about oaths (James 5:12). τὴν εἰς ἑαυτοὺς ἀγάπην ἐκτενῆ ἔχοντες, “be earnest in your love among yourselves” — ἀγάπη, from the love family; ἐκτενής, stretched out, at full strength, as in 1:22. ὅτι ἀγάπη καλύπτει πλῆθος ἁμαρτιῶν, “for love covers a multitude of sins” — from Proverbs 10:12, the same proverb James used in his last verse (James 5:20). καλύπτω, to cover: love does not expose and broadcast others' sins but forgives, forbears and restores. ἁμαρτία, from the sin family.","words":[{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1618","hebrew":"ἐκτενής","translit":"ektenḗs","english":"earnest"},{"strongs":"G2572","hebrew":"καλύπτω","translit":"kalýptō","english":"covers"},{"strongs":"G4128","hebrew":"πλῆθος","translit":"plēthos","english":"multitude"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Be hospitable to one another without grumbling.","understanding":"φιλόξενοι εἰς ἀλλήλους, “Be hospitable to one another” — φιλόξενος, from the love family: a lover of strangers, the quality required of overseers (Titus 1:8). Travelling believers and the poor depended on open homes. ἄνευ γογγυσμοῦ, “without grumbling” — γογγυσμός, muttering under the breath. Hospitality given with complaint ruins the gift.","words":[{"strongs":"G5382","hebrew":"φιλόξενος","translit":"philóxenos","english":"hospitable"},{"strongs":"G1112","hebrew":"γογγυσμός","translit":"gongysmós","english":"grumbling"}]},{"v":10,"text":"As each has received a gift, employ it in serving one another, as good managers of the grace of God in its various forms.","understanding":"ἕκαστος καθὼς ἔλαβεν χάρισμα, “As each has received a gift” — χάρισμα, from the gift family: a gift of grace, every believer has at least one. εἰς ἑαυτοὺς αὐτὸ διακονοῦντες, “employ it in serving one another” — διακονέω, from the servant family: to wait on others. ὡς καλοὶ οἰκονόμοι ποικίλης χάριτος θεοῦ, “as good managers of the grace of God in its various forms” — οἰκονόμος, also from the servant family: a household steward who manages what belongs to the master; καλός, from the good family; ποικίλος, many-coloured, the ‘various’ trials of 1:6 — God's grace comes in as many colours as our troubles.","words":[{"strongs":"G1538","hebrew":"ἕκαστος","translit":"hékastos","english":"each"},{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G5486","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","english":"gift"},{"strongs":"G1247","hebrew":"διακονέω","translit":"diakonéō","english":"serving"},{"strongs":"G2570","hebrew":"καλός","translit":"kalós","english":"good"},{"strongs":"G3623","hebrew":"οἰκονόμος","translit":"oikonómos","english":"managers"},{"strongs":"G4164","hebrew":"ποικίλος","translit":"poikílos","english":"various forms"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"}]},{"v":11,"text":"If anyone speaks, let it be as it were the very words of God. If anyone serves, let it be as of the strength which God supplies, that in all things God may be glorified through Jesus Christ, to whom belong the glory and the dominion forever and ever. Amen.","understanding":"εἴ τις λαλεῖ, ὡς λόγια θεοῦ, “If anyone speaks, let it be as it were the very words of God” — λόγιον, from the word family: an oracle, a divine utterance (Romans 3:2). Speakers must handle their words as God's, not their own. εἴ τις διακονεῖ (διακονέω, from the servant family, as in v. 10), ὡς ἐξ ἰσχύος ἧς χορηγεῖ ὁ θεός, “If anyone serves, let it be as of the strength which God supplies” — ἰσχύς, from the power family: inherent strength, might; χορηγέω, to supply generously, originally to pay for a chorus. Then a doxology with two power-family words: ἡ δόξα καὶ τὸ κράτος, “the glory and the dominion” — κράτος, ruling might, power that prevails, as distinct from ἰσχύς, the strength God supplies for service. αἰῶνας, in the time family and the world family: ‘forever and ever’, the ages upon ages.","words":[{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaks"},{"strongs":"G3051","hebrew":"λόγιον","translit":"lógion","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1247","hebrew":"διακονέω","translit":"diakonéō","english":"serves"},{"strongs":"G2479","hebrew":"ἰσχύς","translit":"ischýs","english":"strength"},{"strongs":"G5524","hebrew":"χορηγέω","translit":"chorēgéō","english":"supplies"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorified"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2904","hebrew":"κράτος","translit":"krátos","english":"dominion"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Beloved, don’t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you, to test you, as though a strange thing happened to you.","understanding":"ἀγαπητοί, “Beloved” — from the love family. μὴ ξενίζεσθε τῇ ἐν ὑμῖν πυρώσει, “don’t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you” — ξενίζω, to treat something as foreign; πύρωσις, a burning, the refiner's fire (Proverbs 27:21), recalling the gold tested by fire (1:7). πρὸς πειρασμὸν ὑμῖν γινομένῃ, “to test you” — πειρασμός, from the test family. ὡς ξένου ὑμῖν συμβαίνοντος, “as though a strange thing happened to you” — ξένος, foreign. Suffering is not an alien intrusion into the Christian life; it is the normal path of those who follow Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"Beloved"},{"strongs":"G3579","hebrew":"ξενίζω","translit":"xenízō","english":"astonished"},{"strongs":"G4451","hebrew":"πύρωσις","translit":"pýrōsis","english":"fiery trial"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"test"},{"strongs":"G3581","hebrew":"ξένος","translit":"xénos","english":"strange"}]},{"v":13,"text":"But because you are partakers of Christ’s sufferings, rejoice; that at the revelation of his glory you also may rejoice with exceeding joy.","understanding":"ἀλλὰ καθὸ κοινωνεῖτε τοῖς τοῦ Χριστοῦ παθήμασιν, χαίρετε, “But because you are partakers of Christ’s sufferings, rejoice” — κοινωνέω, to share, to have fellowship in; πάθημα, from the suffer family. Paul longed for ‘the fellowship of his sufferings’ (Philippians 3:10). ἵνα καὶ ἐν τῇ ἀποκαλύψει τῆς δόξης αὐτοῦ χαρῆτε ἀγαλλιώμενοι, “that at the revelation of his glory you also may rejoice with exceeding joy” — ἀποκάλυψις, from the appear family; ἀγαλλιάω, leaping joy, as in 1:6, 8. Joy now in fellowship, joy then in glory.","words":[{"strongs":"G2841","hebrew":"κοινωνέω","translit":"koinōnéō","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"},{"strongs":"G5463","hebrew":"χαίρω","translit":"chaírō","english":"rejoice"},{"strongs":"G602","hebrew":"ἀποκάλυψις","translit":"apokálypsis","english":"revelation"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G21","hebrew":"ἀγαλλιάω","translit":"agalliáō","english":"exceeding joy"}]},{"v":14,"text":"If you are insulted for the name of Christ, you are blessed; because the Spirit of glory and of God rests on you. On their part he is blasphemed, but on your part he is glorified.","understanding":"εἰ ὀνειδίζεσθε ἐν ὀνόματι Χριστοῦ, μακάριοι, “If you are insulted for the name of Christ, you are blessed” — ὀνειδίζω, to reproach, the word James used for God not ‘reproaching’ those who ask (James 1:5); Jesus used it in the Beatitudes (Matthew 5:11). ὅτι τὸ τῆς δόξης καὶ τὸ τοῦ θεοῦ πνεῦμα ἐφ᾽ ὑμᾶς ἀναπαύεται, “because the Spirit of glory and of God rests on you” — ἀναπαύω, from the rest family: to settle and remain, as the Spirit rested on the Messiah (Isaiah 11:2) and the glory rested on the tabernacle. The last sentence, ‘On their part he is blasphemed, but on your part he is glorified’, is found in later manuscripts the WEB follows.","words":[{"strongs":"G3679","hebrew":"ὀνειδίζω","translit":"oneidízō","english":"insulted"},{"strongs":"G3686","hebrew":"ὄνομα","translit":"ónoma","english":"name"},{"strongs":"G3107","hebrew":"μακάριος","translit":"makários","english":"blessed"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G373","hebrew":"ἀναπαύω","translit":"anapaúō","english":"rests"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"blasphemed"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorified"}]},{"v":15,"text":"For let none of you suffer as a murderer, or a thief, or an evil doer, or a meddler in other men’s matters.","understanding":"μὴ γάρ τις ὑμῶν πασχέτω, “For let none of you suffer” — πάσχω, from the suffer family. Not all suffering is for Christ. ὡς φονεὺς ἢ κλέπτης ἢ κακοποιός, “as a murderer, or a thief, or an evil doer” — κακοποιός, from the evil family, the charge of 2:12 and 3:16, which must never be true. ἢ ὡς ἀλλοτριεπίσκοπος, “or a meddler in other men’s matters” — a rare word, perhaps coined: ‘an overseer of other people's business’, a busybody. Peter puts meddling beside murder: interfering in what is not your business brings deserved trouble.","words":[{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffer"},{"strongs":"G5406","hebrew":"φονεύς","translit":"phoneús","english":"murderer"},{"strongs":"G2812","hebrew":"κλέπτης","translit":"kléptēs","english":"thief"},{"strongs":"G2555","hebrew":"κακοποιός","translit":"kakopoiós","english":"evil doer"},{"strongs":"G244","hebrew":"ἀλλοτριεπίσκοπος","translit":"allotriepískopos","english":"meddler in other men’s matters"}]},{"v":16,"text":"But if one of you suffers for being a Christian, let him not be ashamed; but let him glorify God in this matter.","understanding":"εἰ δὲ ὡς Χριστιανός, “But if one of you suffers for being a Christian” — Χριστιανός, ‘a Christ-person’, a name first given at Antioch (Acts 11:26), probably by outsiders as a label; it appears only three times in the New Testament (Acts 26:28). μὴ αἰσχυνέσθω, “let him not be ashamed” — αἰσχύνομαι. δοξαζέτω δὲ τὸν θεὸν ἐν τῷ μέρει τούτῳ, “but let him glorify God in this matter” — the earliest manuscripts read ‘in this name’. What the world uses as an insult, the believer wears as an honour.","words":[{"strongs":"G5546","hebrew":"Χριστιανός","translit":"Christianós","english":"Christian"},{"strongs":"G153","hebrew":"αἰσχύνομαι","translit":"aischýnomai","english":"ashamed"},{"strongs":"G1392","hebrew":"δοξάζω","translit":"doxázō","english":"glorify"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3313","hebrew":"μέρος","translit":"méros","english":"matter"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For the time has come for judgment to begin with the household of God. If it begins first with us, what will happen to those who don’t obey the Good News of God?","understanding":"ὅτι ὁ καιρὸς τοῦ ἄρξασθαι τὸ κρίμα ἀπὸ τοῦ οἴκου τοῦ θεοῦ, “For the time has come for judgment to begin with the household of God” — καιρός, from the time family: the appointed moment, as distinct from χρόνος, the span of time (vv. 2–3). κρίμα, judgement; οἶκος, the house — the ‘spiritual house’ of 2:5. God's refining judgement begins with His own people (Ezekiel 9:6; Malachi 3:1–3). “If it begins first with us, what will happen to those who don’t obey the Good News of God?” — ἀπειθέω, refusing to be persuaded; εὐαγγέλιον. κρίμα is from the judge family — judgement as the verdict or sentence passed.","words":[{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"time"},{"strongs":"G756","hebrew":"ἄρχομαι","translit":"árchomai","english":"begin"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G3624","hebrew":"οἶκος","translit":"oîkos","english":"household"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G544","hebrew":"ἀπειθέω","translit":"apeithéō","english":"don’t obey"},{"strongs":"G2098","hebrew":"εὐαγγέλιον","translit":"euangélion","english":"Good News"}]},{"v":18,"text":"“If it is hard for the righteous to be saved, what will happen to the ungodly and the sinner?”","understanding":"Peter quotes Proverbs 11:31 in the Greek Old Testament: “If it is hard for the righteous to be saved, what will happen to the ungodly and the sinner?” — μόλις, with difficulty, through many trials; σῴζω, from the heal family: rescued, made whole; ἀσεβής, from the godliness family: the irreverent, the ungodly; ἁμαρτωλός, from the sin family. ποῦ φανεῖται, literally ‘where will he appear?’ — φαίνω, from the appear family: where will he stand on that day? Salvation is certain but not easy; for those outside it, the end is unthinkable.","words":[{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G3433","hebrew":"μόλις","translit":"mólis","english":"hard"},{"strongs":"G4982","hebrew":"σώζω","translit":"sṓzō","english":"saved"},{"strongs":"G765","hebrew":"ἀσεβής","translit":"asebḗs","english":"ungodly"},{"strongs":"G268","hebrew":"ἁμαρτωλός","translit":"hamartōlós","english":"sinner"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"happen"}]},{"v":19,"text":"Therefore let them also who suffer according to the will of God in doing good entrust their souls to him, as to a faithful Creator.","understanding":"ὥστε καὶ οἱ πάσχοντες κατὰ τὸ θέλημα τοῦ θεοῦ, “Therefore let them also who suffer according to the will of God” — πάσχω, from the suffer family; θέλημα, as in 3:17. ἐν ἀγαθοποιΐᾳ, “in doing good” — ἀγαθοποιΐα, from the good family: keep on doing good while you suffer. πιστῷ κτίστῃ παρατιθέσθωσαν τὰς ψυχὰς αὐτῶν, “entrust their souls to him, as to a faithful Creator” — παρατίθημι, to deposit something for safekeeping, the very word Jesus used on the cross: ‘Father, into your hands I commit my spirit’ (Luke 23:46). ψυχή, from the life family. κτίστης, Creator — only here in the New Testament: the One who made you can keep you.","words":[{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffer"},{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3908","hebrew":"παρατίθημι","translit":"paratíthēmi","english":"entrust"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G16","hebrew":"ἀγαθοποιΐα","translit":"agathopoiḯa","english":"doing good"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G2939","hebrew":"κτίστης","translit":"ktístēs","english":"Creator"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Putting On the Armour","verseRef":"v. 1-2","image":"Picture a soldier in the early morning, strapping on his breastplate, buckling his sword, fixing his helmet. He knows the battle ahead will be painful. He has already decided he will not run. His mind is made up before the trumpet sounds.","meaning":"“Therefore, since Christ suffered for us in the flesh, arm yourselves also with the same mind” (v. 1). The weapon Peter gives is not a sword but a mindset — Christ's resolve to do the Father's will whatever it costs. If you decide in advance that obedience matters more than comfort, temptation loses much of its grip, and you can live “the rest of your time in the flesh … for the will of God” (v. 2)."},{"title":"The Party You Left","verseRef":"v. 3-5","image":"Picture a group of old friends heading out on a Friday night for drink, noise and whatever happens next. They knock on your door as they always used to. You smile and say you are not coming. They stare at you, puzzled, then laugh and start mocking — ‘What happened to you? Have you become a saint?’ — as they walk off into the night.","meaning":"“They think it is strange that you don’t run with them into the same excess of riot, blaspheming” (v. 4). Peter says the past has had more than enough of our time (v. 3). Their mockery is not the last word: they “will give account to him who is ready to judge the living and the dead” (v. 5). You are not missing out; you have been set free."},{"title":"The Many-Coloured Grace","verseRef":"v. 8-11","image":"Picture a household where the master has left on a long journey. He has given each steward a different store: one has keys to the granary, another the wine cellar, another the guest rooms, another the money chest. When guests arrive, the stewards work together — one cooks, one serves, one prepares the beds — and the house hums with welcome. None of the goods are theirs; all are the master's.","meaning":"“As each has received a gift, employ it in serving one another, as good managers of the grace of God in its various forms” (v. 10). Your gift is not your property; it is a store of the Master's grace entrusted to you for others. Speak as if speaking God's words; serve in the strength He supplies, “that in all things God may be glorified through Jesus Christ” (v. 11)."},{"title":"The Fiery Trial","verseRef":"v. 12-14","image":"Picture a believer arrested in a Roman city, dragged before a jeering crowd, and mocked for the name ‘Christian’. Flames from a nearby fire light up the faces of the crowd. Yet the believer's face is calm, almost radiant — as if a glory unseen by the crowd is resting on him.","meaning":"“Beloved, don’t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you, to test you, as though a strange thing happened to you” (v. 12). “But because you are partakers of Christ’s sufferings, rejoice” (v. 13). “If you are insulted for the name of Christ, you are blessed; because the Spirit of glory and of God rests on you” (v. 14). In the fire you are never alone; His glory rests on you."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Armed with Christ's Mind (v. 1-6)","body":"Since Christ suffered in the flesh, arm yourselves with the same resolve, living the rest of your time for God's will, not human desires; the past was enough for pagan excess, and those who mock you for not joining them will give account to the Judge of the living and the dead; the gospel was preached to those now dead so that, though judged by people, they live to God."},{"label":"Living in View of the End (v. 7-11)","body":"The end of all things is near, so be clear-minded and sober for prayer; above all keep love constant, for love covers many sins; be hospitable without grumbling; use every gift to serve others as stewards of God's varied grace, speaking as God's words and serving in His strength, so that God is glorified through Jesus Christ."},{"label":"Suffering as a Christian (v. 12-19)","body":"Do not be surprised by the fiery trial; rejoice as you share Christ's sufferings, for you will rejoice at His glory; if insulted for Christ's name you are blessed, for the Spirit of glory rests on you; do not suffer as a wrongdoer, but if as a Christian, do not be ashamed but glorify God; judgement begins with God's household; so let those who suffer by God's will entrust themselves to their faithful Creator while doing good."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Settled Mind and a Clean Break","paragraphs":["Chapter 3 ended with Christ's suffering and victory. Chapter 4 draws the conclusion: “Therefore, since Christ suffered for us in the flesh, arm yourselves also with the same mind” (v. 1). The Christian life is warfare, and the first weapon is a decision. Christ resolved to do His Father's will even when it led to the cross. When believers adopt that same resolve, they have made a clean break with sin's rule; the rest of their life is for God's will, not human desires (v. 2). Paul says, “For he who has died has been freed from sin” (Romans 6:7).","That decision makes believers look strange. Peter's readers had once joined in the drinking, sexual excess and idol feasts of their towns (v. 3). Now they stay home, and their old friends are both puzzled and offended: “They think it is strange that you don’t run with them into the same excess of riot, blaspheming” (v. 4). The ridicule hurts, but Peter reminds them who has the final word: their mockers “will give account to him who is ready to judge the living and the dead” (v. 5).","Verse 6 answers a painful question. If faith in Christ does not stop believers from dying, what good is it? Peter says the gospel was preached to those who have now died, so that although they were judged by human standards in the flesh — mocked and buried like everyone else — they live to God in the spirit. This is not a second chance after death, which Scripture never teaches; it is the assurance that believers who die are alive to God."]},{"heading":"Love, Hospitality and Gifts at the End of the Age","paragraphs":["“But the end of all things is near” (v. 7). The New Testament writers lived with a strong sense that Christ's return could be soon. Peter's response is not panic, date-setting or withdrawal, but sober prayer: “Therefore be of sound mind, self-controlled, and sober in prayer.” Jesus said the same: “Watch therefore, for you don’t know in what hour your Lord comes” (Matthew 24:42). Paul: “The night is far gone, and the day is near” (Romans 13:12).","Above all, believers must keep loving one another at full stretch, “for love covers a multitude of sins” (v. 8). Peter, who had been forgiven a great deal, knew what it was to have sins covered rather than exposed. Love shows itself in open homes — “Be hospitable to one another without grumbling” (v. 9) — and in using every gift for others.","Every believer has received a χάρισμα, a gift of grace (v. 10). The gifts vary, like God's grace itself, which Peter calls ποικίλη, many-coloured. Paul says, “Now there are various kinds of gifts, but the same Spirit” (1 Corinthians 12:4). Peter divides them into two broad kinds: speaking gifts and serving gifts. Speakers must speak “as it were the very words of God”; servers must serve “as of the strength which God supplies” (v. 11). The goal is not the fame of the gifted but the glory of God: “that in all things God may be glorified through Jesus Christ, to whom belong the glory and the dominion forever and ever. Amen.”"]},{"heading":"The Fiery Trial and the Faithful Creator","paragraphs":["Peter now speaks directly about the persecution his readers are facing. “Beloved, don’t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you, to test you, as though a strange thing happened to you” (v. 12). Suffering for Christ is not a sign that something has gone wrong; it is part of following a crucified Lord. In fact, it is cause for joy: “But because you are partakers of Christ’s sufferings, rejoice; that at the revelation of his glory you also may rejoice with exceeding joy” (v. 13). The apostles had learned this early: after being flogged, they left the council “rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer dishonor for Jesus’ name” (Acts 5:41).","Peter adds a precious promise: “If you are insulted for the name of Christ, you are blessed; because the Spirit of glory and of God rests on you” (v. 14). As God's glory once rested on the tabernacle, the Spirit of glory rests on suffering believers. But Peter is honest: not all suffering is holy. Believers must never suffer for murder, theft, wrongdoing — or meddling in other people's business (v. 15). But “if one of you suffers for being a Christian, let him not be ashamed; but let him glorify God in this matter” (v. 16).","Then a sobering perspective: “For the time has come for judgment to begin with the household of God” (v. 17). Suffering is God's refining judgement on His own house, purifying it. If even believers are saved only through such trials, “what will happen to the ungodly and the sinner?” (v. 18). The chapter ends with the believer's response: “Therefore let them also who suffer according to the will of God in doing good entrust their souls to him, as to a faithful Creator” (v. 19). The word ‘entrust’ is the word Jesus used on the cross: “Father, into your hands I commit my spirit!” (Luke 23:46). In suffering, do what Jesus did — keep doing good, and place yourself in your Father's hands."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"Sharing His Sufferings, Sharing His Glory","paragraphs":["Peter's call to rejoice in suffering comes straight from Jesus: “Blessed are you when people reproach you, persecute you, and say all kinds of evil against you falsely, for my sake” (Matthew 5:11), “Rejoice, and be exceedingly glad, for great is your reward in heaven” (Matthew 5:12). Paul expresses the same longing: “that I may know him, and the power of his resurrection, and the fellowship of his sufferings” (Philippians 3:10), and he promises, “if indeed we suffer with him, that we may also be glorified with him” (Romans 8:17).","The name ‘Christian’ appears only three times in the New Testament. “The disciples were first called Christians in Antioch” (Acts 11:26). King Agrippa used it with a sneer: “With a little persuasion are you trying to make me a Christian?” (Acts 26:28). And Peter tells believers not to be ashamed of it (v. 16). What began as a nickname became a badge of honour.","The command to use gifts as stewards echoes Paul's teaching: “Having gifts differing according to the grace that was given to us” (Romans 12:6), and his call to hospitality: “contributing to the needs of the saints; given to hospitality” (Romans 12:13). And the closing image — entrusting the soul to a faithful Creator — takes us to Calvary, where Jesus, “crying with a loud voice, said, ‘Father, into your hands I commit my spirit!’” (Luke 23:46)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Arm yourselves","hebrew":"ὁπλίζω","translit":"hoplízō","strongs":"G3695 (mind ἔννοια, énnoia, G1771)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “to equip (with weapons (middle voice and figuratively)).” ὁπλίζω comes from ὅπλον, a weapon or piece of armour. Peter uses it only here: the believer's armour is a mind (ἔννοια) — a settled resolve — like Christ's. Paul speaks of ‘the armor of light’ (Romans 13:12) and ‘the whole armor of God’ (Ephesians 6:11). Peter's point is that suffering is easier to bear when the decision to obey has already been made. A mind armed in advance does not surrender at the first blow."},{"english":"Gift","hebrew":"χάρισμα","translit":"chárisma","strongs":"G5486 (manager οἰκονόμος, oikonómos, G3623)","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “a (divine) gratuity, i.e. deliverance (from danger or passion); (specially), a (spiritual) endowment, i.e. (subjectively) religious qualification, or (objectively) miraculous faculty.” χάρισμα comes from χάρις, grace: a gift of grace. It belongs to the gift family. Peter pairs it with οἰκονόμος, from the servant family: a household manager who handles the master's goods. Your abilities, opportunities and spiritual gifts are not trophies but stewardships, to be used for others and accounted for."},{"english":"Fiery trial","hebrew":"πύρωσις","translit":"pýrōsis","strongs":"G4451 (strange ξένος, xénos, G3581)","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “ignition, i.e. (specially), smelting (figuratively, conflagration, calamity as a test).” πύρωσις is a burning, used of the furnace in which metal is refined (Proverbs 27:21). Peter links it to the gold tested by fire in 1:7. The word ‘strange’ (ξένος) and the verb ‘be astonished’ (ξενίζω) come from the same root: suffering is not a foreigner who has wandered into the Christian life. It is a familiar part of the road. The fire refines; it does not destroy."},{"english":"Christian","hebrew":"Χριστιανός","translit":"Christianós","strongs":"G5546","verses":"v. 16","insight":"Strong's: “a Christian, i.e. follower of Christ.” The ending -ιανός marked someone as a partisan or follower of a person, like ‘Herodians’. The name was first given at Antioch (Acts 11:26), probably by outsiders, and used mockingly by Agrippa (Acts 26:28). By Peter's time it could be a criminal charge: Roman writers soon record Christians being punished simply for the name. Peter turns the insult into an honour: to suffer as a ‘Christ-one’ is to glorify God."},{"english":"Entrust","hebrew":"παρατίθημι","translit":"paratíthēmi","strongs":"G3908 (Creator κτίστης, ktístēs, G2939)","verses":"v. 19","insight":"Strong's: “to place alongside, i.e. present (food, truth); by implication, to deposit (as a trust or for protection).” παρατίθημι was used of depositing money with a trusted person for safekeeping. Jesus used it with His last breath: ‘Father, into your hands I commit my spirit’ (Luke 23:46). Peter tells suffering believers to do the same with their souls — to deposit them with God, the faithful κτίστης, the Creator. The One who made you is able and faithful to keep what you entrust to Him."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is preparing you for opposition, sharpening your love, and teaching you how to entrust yourself to God in the fire.","items":[{"title":"Is Your Mind Armed?","body":"The Spirit asks whether you have settled in advance that you will obey God whatever it costs, or whether you decide again every time temptation or pressure comes. “arm yourselves also with the same mind” (v. 1). What would change this week if you went into each day already resolved to do God's will rather than your own desires?"},{"title":"What Gift Are You Hoarding?","body":"You have received a gift of grace — perhaps teaching, encouraging, giving, organising, hospitality, prayer, practical help. The Spirit asks: are you using it to serve others, or keeping it to yourself? “As each has received a gift, employ it in serving one another” (v. 10). Name your gift and one person who needs it this week."},{"title":"Into Whose Hands?","body":"If you are in a fiery trial now, the Spirit wants you to know it is not strange, and that His glory rests on you (vv. 12–14). He invites you to do what Jesus did: “entrust their souls to him, as to a faithful Creator” (v. 19). Speak it aloud today: ‘Father, into Your hands I commit my spirit — and this situation.’ Then keep doing good."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Old-Life Check","body":"Is there anything from your ‘past time’ (v. 3) — habits, places, friendships, entertainment — that is still pulling you back? Are you embarrassed when others think your new life is strange (v. 4)?"},{"title":"The Love Check","body":"Do you cover others' sins with love, or expose and repeat them (v. 8)? When did you last open your home without grumbling (v. 9)?"},{"title":"The Suffering Check","body":"When you suffer, is it for being a Christian, or for wrongdoing, foolishness or meddling (vv. 15–16)? Are you ashamed of the name of Christ anywhere in your life?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Employ Your Gift This Week","body":"Write down what you believe your main gift of grace is (v. 10). Then plan one specific way to use it this week to serve someone in your church — a meal, a visit, a word of encouragement, a practical task, teaching a child, praying with someone. Do it as a steward, not an owner, and ask God for His strength (v. 11)."},{"title":"The Entrusting Prayer","body":"Each evening this week, before you sleep, take your current trial or worry and pray the words of Luke 23:46 and 1 Peter 4:19: ‘Father, into Your hands I entrust my soul and this situation. You are my faithful Creator.’ Then write down one good thing you will do tomorrow despite the pressure."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, You suffered in the flesh for me. Arm me with Your mind, so that I live the rest of my time not for human desires but for the will of God. The past has had more than enough of my life; I will not run back into the flood of excess, even when others find it strange and speak evil of me. I know they will give account to You, the Judge of the living and the dead. The end of all things is near, so keep me clear-minded and sober for prayer. Above all, stretch my love to full strength, because love covers a multitude of sins. Make my home open and my heart free from grumbling. Thank You for the gift of grace You have given me; help me use it to serve others as a good steward of Your many-coloured grace — speaking as Your words and serving in the strength You supply, so that You are glorified in everything. When the fiery trial comes, let me not be astonished. Let me rejoice to share in Your sufferings, knowing I will rejoice at the revelation of Your glory. If I am insulted for Your name, let me know that the Spirit of glory rests on me. Let me never suffer for wrongdoing, and never be ashamed to be called a Christian. Like You on the cross, I entrust my soul to my faithful Creator, and I will keep on doing good. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Apostles Rejoice After Flogging","ref":"Acts 5:17-42","relatedVerses":"v. 13-16","summary":"The high priest had Peter and the apostles arrested for preaching about Jesus. An angel released them, and they went straight back to teaching in the temple. Brought before the council again, Peter declared that they must obey God rather than men. The council had them flogged and ordered them not to speak in Jesus' name. “They therefore departed from the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer dishonor for Jesus’ name” (Acts 5:41). Peter writes chapter 4 as a man who had felt the whip and found joy in it."},{"title":"Stephen Entrusts His Spirit","ref":"Acts 6:8-7:60","relatedVerses":"v. 14, 19","summary":"Stephen, full of grace and power, was falsely accused before the council. As he spoke, all who sat in the council saw his face like the face of an angel. When he finished, they rushed at him, dragged him out of the city and stoned him. Looking up, he saw the glory of God and Jesus standing at the right hand of God. As the stones fell he prayed, ‘Lord Jesus, receive my spirit!’ and, kneeling, ‘Lord, don’t hold this sin against them!’ He is a picture of verse 14 — the Spirit of glory resting on a believer insulted for Christ — and verse 19, entrusting his soul to God while doing good to his enemies."},{"title":"Jesus Commits His Spirit to the Father","ref":"Luke 23:44-49","relatedVerses":"v. 1, 19","summary":"At noon darkness covered the land until three in the afternoon, and the curtain of the temple was torn in two. Then Jesus, “crying with a loud voice, said, ‘Father, into your hands I commit my spirit!’ Having said this, he breathed his last” (Luke 23:46). The verb ‘commit’ is the same verb Peter uses in verse 19, ‘entrust’. Jesus did not entrust Himself to the crowd, the governor or His friends, but to His Father, the faithful Creator. He suffered in the flesh (v. 1) and handed Himself to God. Peter tells every suffering believer to follow those steps."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"1 Peter","chapter":5,"chapterTitle":"Shepherds, Humility, and the God of All Grace","themeTitle":"“Casting All Your Worries on Him, Because He Cares for You”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Therefore I exhort the elders among you, as a fellow elder, and a witness of the sufferings of Christ, and who will also share in the glory that will be revealed.","understanding":"πρεσβυτέρους οὖν ἐν ὑμῖν παρακαλῶ, “Therefore I exhort the elders among you” — πρεσβύτερος, an older man, then a recognised leader of a congregation (James 5:14). ὁ συμπρεσβύτερος, “as a fellow elder” — Peter, an apostle, stands beside them, not above them. καὶ μάρτυς τῶν τοῦ Χριστοῦ παθημάτων, “and a witness of the sufferings of Christ” — μάρτυς, an eyewitness and one who testifies; πάθημα, from the suffer family. He saw the arrest, the trial and the cross. ὁ καὶ τῆς μελλούσης ἀποκαλύπτεσθαι δόξης κοινωνός, “and who will also share in the glory that will be revealed” — κοινωνός, a partner; ἀποκαλύπτω, from the appear family: to unveil. Sufferings, then glory — the pattern of 1:11. μάρτυς is from the witness family — a witness — the person who testifies; by extension one who dies for the testimony, a martyr.","words":[{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elders"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhort"},{"strongs":"G4850","hebrew":"συμπρεσβύτερος","translit":"sympresbýteros","english":"fellow elder"},{"strongs":"G3144","hebrew":"μάρτυς","translit":"mártys","english":"witness"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"},{"strongs":"G2844","hebrew":"κοινωνός","translit":"koinōnós","english":"share"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G601","hebrew":"ἀποκαλύπτω","translit":"apokalýptō","english":"revealed"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Shepherd the flock of God which is among you, exercising the oversight, not under compulsion, but voluntarily, not for dishonest gain, but willingly;","understanding":"ποιμάνατε τὸ ἐν ὑμῖν ποίμνιον τοῦ θεοῦ, “Shepherd the flock of God which is among you” — ποιμαίνω, the very word Jesus said to Peter: ‘Tend my sheep’ (John 21:16). The flock belongs to God, not the elders. ἐπισκοποῦντες, “exercising the oversight” — ἐπισκοπέω, to watch over, the verb of ἐπίσκοπος, ‘overseer’ (2:25). Three contrasts follow. μὴ ἀναγκαστῶς ἀλλὰ ἑκουσίως, “not under compulsion, but voluntarily” — not as a reluctant duty. μηδὲ αἰσχροκερδῶς ἀλλὰ προθύμως, “not for dishonest gain, but willingly” — αἰσχροκερδῶς, greedy for shameful profit; προθύμως, eagerly.","words":[{"strongs":"G4165","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","english":"Shepherd"},{"strongs":"G4168","hebrew":"ποίμνιον","translit":"poímnion","english":"flock"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G1983","hebrew":"ἐπισκοπέω","translit":"episkopéō","english":"exercising the oversight"},{"strongs":"G317","hebrew":"ἀναγκαστῶς","translit":"anankastōs","english":"under compulsion"},{"strongs":"G1596","hebrew":"ἑκουσίως","translit":"hekousíōs","english":"voluntarily"},{"strongs":"G147","hebrew":"αἰσχροκερδῶς","translit":"aischrokerdōs","english":"for dishonest gain"},{"strongs":"G4290","hebrew":"προθύμως","translit":"prothýmōs","english":"willingly"}]},{"v":3,"text":"neither as lording it over those entrusted to you, but making yourselves examples to the flock.","understanding":"μηδ᾽ ὡς κατακυριεύοντες τῶν κλήρων, “neither as lording it over those entrusted to you” — κατακυριεύω, to dominate, the exact verb Jesus used of Gentile rulers: ‘it shall not be so among you’ (Mark 10:42–43). κλῆρος, a lot, a portion assigned: each congregation is God's allotted portion entrusted to the elders. ἀλλὰ τύποι γινόμενοι τοῦ ποιμνίου, “but making yourselves examples to the flock” — τύπος, from the pattern family: the impression left by a die, a model to copy, as distinct from ὑπογραμμός, the tracing-copy of 2:21. Elders lead by example, not by force.","words":[{"strongs":"G2634","hebrew":"κατακυριεύω","translit":"katakyrieúō","english":"lording it over"},{"strongs":"G2819","hebrew":"κλῆρος","translit":"klēros","english":"those entrusted"},{"strongs":"G5179","hebrew":"τύπος","translit":"týpos","english":"examples"},{"strongs":"G4168","hebrew":"ποίμνιον","translit":"poímnion","english":"flock"}]},{"v":4,"text":"When the chief Shepherd is revealed, you will receive the crown of glory that doesn’t fade away.","understanding":"καὶ φανερωθέντος τοῦ ἀρχιποίμενος, “When the chief Shepherd is revealed” — φανερόω, from the appear family: to be made visible, brought into the open, as distinct from ἀποκαλύπτω in verse 1, the unveiling. ἀρχιποίμην, ‘chief shepherd’, a title found only here: the under-shepherds answer to Him. κομιεῖσθε τὸν ἀμαράντινον τῆς δόξης στέφανον, “you will receive the crown of glory that doesn’t fade away” — στέφανος, the victor's wreath; ἀμαράντινος, unfading, a near relative of the ‘unfading’ inheritance of 1:4. Athletes' wreaths of leaves withered in days (1 Corinthians 9:25); this one never will. στέφανος is from the crown family — a wreath or garland — the victor’s prize, a mark of honour and reward.","words":[{"strongs":"G5319","hebrew":"φανερόω","translit":"phaneróō","english":"revealed"},{"strongs":"G750","hebrew":"ἀρχιποίμην","translit":"archipoímēn","english":"chief Shepherd"},{"strongs":"G2865","hebrew":"κομίζω","translit":"komízō","english":"receive"},{"strongs":"G262","hebrew":"ἀμαράντινος","translit":"amarántinos","english":"doesn’t fade away"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G4735","hebrew":"στέφανος","translit":"stéphanos","english":"crown"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Likewise, you younger ones, be subject to the elder. Yes, all of you clothe yourselves with humility, to subject yourselves to one another; for “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble.”","understanding":"ὁμοίως, νεώτεροι, ὑποτάγητε πρεσβυτέροις, “Likewise, you younger ones, be subject to the elder” — νεώτερος, the comparative of νέος, from the new family: young in age, as distinct from καινός, new in kind. πάντες δὲ ἀλλήλοις τὴν ταπεινοφροσύνην ἐγκομβώσασθε, “Yes, all of you clothe yourselves with humility” — ταπεινοφροσύνη, from the humble family: lowliness of mind; ἐγκομβόομαι, to tie on with a knot, like a slave's apron. Peter remembered Jesus tying a towel round His waist to wash their feet (John 13:4). Then Proverbs 3:34, the verse James also quoted: “God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble” — ἀντιτάσσομαι, from the resist family; ταπεινός, from the humble family.","words":[{"strongs":"G3668","hebrew":"ὁμοίως","translit":"homoíōs","english":"Likewise"},{"strongs":"G3501","hebrew":"νέος","translit":"néos","english":"younger ones"},{"strongs":"G5293","hebrew":"ὑποτάσσω","translit":"hypotássō","english":"be subject"},{"strongs":"G4245","hebrew":"πρεσβύτερος","translit":"presbýteros","english":"elder"},{"strongs":"G1463","hebrew":"ἐγκομβόομαι","translit":"enkombóomai","english":"clothe yourselves"},{"strongs":"G5012","hebrew":"ταπεινοφροσύνη","translit":"tapeinophrosýnē","english":"humility"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G498","hebrew":"ἀντιτάσσομαι","translit":"antitássomai","english":"resists"},{"strongs":"G5244","hebrew":"ὑπερήφανος","translit":"hyperḗphanos","english":"proud"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G5011","hebrew":"ταπεινός","translit":"tapeinós","english":"humble"}]},{"v":6,"text":"Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time;","understanding":"ταπεινώθητε οὖν ὑπὸ τὴν κραταιὰν χεῖρα τοῦ θεοῦ, “Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God” — ταπεινόω, from the humble family, as in James 4:10; κραταιός, from the power family: ruling might, the adjective of κράτος (v. 11), the language of the exodus, God's ‘mighty hand’ that brought Israel out of Egypt. The hand that disciplines is the hand that delivers. ἵνα ὑμᾶς ὑψώσῃ ἐν καιρῷ, “that he may exalt you in due time” — ὑψόω, to lift high; καιρός, from the time family: God's appointed moment, not yours.","words":[{"strongs":"G5013","hebrew":"ταπεινόω","translit":"tapeinóō","english":"Humble yourselves"},{"strongs":"G2900","hebrew":"κραταιός","translit":"krataiós","english":"mighty"},{"strongs":"G5495","hebrew":"χείρ","translit":"cheír","english":"hand"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5312","hebrew":"ὑψόω","translit":"hypsóō","english":"exalt"},{"strongs":"G2540","hebrew":"καιρός","translit":"kairós","english":"due time"}]},{"v":7,"text":"casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you.","understanding":"πᾶσαν τὴν μέριμναν ὑμῶν ἐπιρρίψαντες ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν, “casting all your worries on him” — μέριμνα, anxiety, the care that pulls the mind in different directions (Matthew 6:25); ἐπιρρίπτω, to throw upon, the word used of throwing cloaks on the colt Jesus rode (Luke 19:35). The participle connects it to verse 6: humbling yourself includes handing over your anxieties. ὅτι αὐτῷ μέλει περὶ ὑμῶν, “because he cares for you” — μέλει, it matters to Him, you are His concern.","words":[{"strongs":"G3956","hebrew":"πᾶς","translit":"pâs","english":"all"},{"strongs":"G3308","hebrew":"μέριμνα","translit":"mérimna","english":"worries"},{"strongs":"G1977","hebrew":"ἐπιῤῥίπτω","translit":"epirrhíptō","english":"casting"},{"strongs":"G3199","hebrew":"μέλω","translit":"mélō","english":"cares"}]},{"v":8,"text":"Be sober and self-controlled. Be watchful. Your adversary, the devil, walks around like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour.","understanding":"νήψατε, γρηγορήσατε, “Be sober and self-controlled. Be watchful.” — νήφω, from the self-control family: clear-headed, as in 1:13 and 4:7; γρηγορέω, from the sleep family: to stay awake, the word Jesus used in Gethsemane when Peter slept (Matthew 26:40–41). ὁ ἀντίδικος ὑμῶν διάβολος, “Your adversary, the devil” — ἀντίδικος, an opponent in a lawsuit, the accuser; διάβολος, the slanderer. ὡς λέων ὠρυόμενος περιπατεῖ, “walks around like a roaring lion” — ὠρύομαι, to roar, as a hungry lion does to frighten prey. ζητῶν τινα καταπιεῖν, “seeking whom he may devour” — καταπίνω, to gulp down, swallow whole. The lion looks for the stray, the isolated, the sleepy.","words":[{"strongs":"G3525","hebrew":"νήφω","translit":"nḗphō","english":"sober"},{"strongs":"G1127","hebrew":"γρηγορεύω","translit":"grēgoreúō","english":"watchful"},{"strongs":"G476","hebrew":"ἀντίδικος","translit":"antídikos","english":"adversary"},{"strongs":"G1228","hebrew":"διάβολος","translit":"diábolos","english":"devil"},{"strongs":"G5612","hebrew":"ὠρύομαι","translit":"ōrýomai","english":"roaring"},{"strongs":"G3023","hebrew":"λέων","translit":"léōn","english":"lion"},{"strongs":"G4043","hebrew":"περιπατέω","translit":"peripatéō","english":"walks around"},{"strongs":"G2212","hebrew":"ζητέω","translit":"zētéō","english":"seeking"},{"strongs":"G2666","hebrew":"καταπίνω","translit":"katapínō","english":"devour"}]},{"v":9,"text":"Withstand him steadfast in your faith, knowing that your brothers who are in the world are undergoing the same sufferings.","understanding":"ᾧ ἀντίστητε στερεοὶ τῇ πίστει, “Withstand him steadfast in your faith” — ἀνθίστημι, from the resist family: to stand your ground, the very word of James 4:7, ‘Resist the devil’ and of Ephesians 6:13; στερεός, firm, solid as rock. Faith is the stance. εἰδότες, “knowing” — εἴδω, from the know family (and the see family). τὰ αὐτὰ τῶν παθημάτων τῇ ἐν κόσμῳ ὑμῶν ἀδελφότητι ἐπιτελεῖσθαι, “that your brothers who are in the world are undergoing the same sufferings” — πάθημα, from the suffer family; κόσμος, from the world family; ἐπιτελέω, from the perfect family: to be carried through to completion. You are not alone; your suffering is shared worldwide, and it is being brought to its intended end.","words":[{"strongs":"G436","hebrew":"ἀνθίστημι","translit":"anthístēmi","english":"Withstand"},{"strongs":"G4731","hebrew":"στερεός","translit":"stereós","english":"steadfast"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G3804","hebrew":"πάθημα","translit":"páthēma","english":"sufferings"},{"strongs":"G81","hebrew":"ἀδελφότης","translit":"adelphótēs","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G2005","hebrew":"ἐπιτελέω","translit":"epiteléō","english":"undergoing"}]},{"v":10,"text":"But may the God of all grace, who called you to his eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a little while, perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you.","understanding":"ὁ δὲ θεὸς πάσης χάριτος, “But may the God of all grace” — grace for every need, the many-coloured grace of 4:10. ὁ καλέσας ὑμᾶς εἰς τὴν αἰώνιον αὐτοῦ δόξαν ἐν Χριστῷ, “who called you to his eternal glory by Christ Jesus” — αἰώνιος, from the time family: belonging to the endless age. ὀλίγον παθόντας, “after you have suffered a little while” — πάσχω, from the suffer family; ὀλίγον, a little, as in 1:6. Then four verbs: καταρτίσει, “perfect” — καταρτίζω, from the perfect family: to mend, to restore, to fit out completely, the word for mending nets (Mark 1:19); στηρίξει, “establish” — make firm, the word Jesus used to Peter: ‘establish your brothers’ (Luke 22:32); σθενώσει, “strengthen” — σθενόω, from the power family; θεμελιώσει, “settle” — set on a foundation.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"little while"},{"strongs":"G3958","hebrew":"πάσχω","translit":"páschō","english":"suffered"},{"strongs":"G2675","hebrew":"καταρτίζω","translit":"katartízō","english":"perfect"},{"strongs":"G4741","hebrew":"στηρίζω","translit":"stērízō","english":"establish"},{"strongs":"G4599","hebrew":"σθενόω","translit":"sthenóō","english":"strengthen"},{"strongs":"G2311","hebrew":"θεμελιόω","translit":"themelióō","english":"settle"}]},{"v":11,"text":"To him be the glory and the power forever and ever. Amen.","understanding":"αὐτῷ τὸ κράτος εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων, “To him be the glory and the power forever and ever” — κράτος, from the power family: ruling might, the strength that prevails, matching the ‘mighty hand’ of verse 6. αἰών, in the time family and the world family: ‘to the ages of the ages’. The WEB follows manuscripts that include ‘the glory’. The one who allows the lion to roar holds all the power.","words":[{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2904","hebrew":"κράτος","translit":"krátos","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Through Silvanus, our faithful brother, as I consider him, I have written to you briefly, exhorting, and testifying that this is the true grace of God in which you stand.","understanding":"διὰ Σιλουανοῦ ὑμῖν τοῦ πιστοῦ ἀδελφοῦ, “Through Silvanus, our faithful brother” — Silvanus is the Latin form of Silas, Paul's companion (Acts 15:40) and co-sender of 1 and 2 Thessalonians. ‘Through’ may mean he carried the letter, helped write it, or both; this may explain the polished Greek of a Galilean fisherman. ὡς λογίζομαι, “as I consider him” — Peter's own judgement. δι᾽ ὀλίγων ἔγραψα, “I have written to you briefly” — ὀλίγος, few words. παρακαλῶν καὶ ἐπιμαρτυρῶν, “exhorting, and testifying” — the two aims of the letter. ταύτην εἶναι ἀληθῆ χάριν τοῦ θεοῦ, εἰς ἣν ἑστήκατε, “that this is the true grace of God in which you stand” — ἵστημι, to stand firm. Suffering does not mean they have fallen out of grace; it is the true grace.","words":[{"strongs":"G4610","hebrew":"Σιλουανός","translit":"Silouanós","english":"Silvanus"},{"strongs":"G4103","hebrew":"πιστός","translit":"pistós","english":"faithful"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G3049","hebrew":"λογίζομαι","translit":"logízomai","english":"consider"},{"strongs":"G3641","hebrew":"ὀλίγος","translit":"olígos","english":"briefly"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G3870","hebrew":"παρακαλέω","translit":"parakaléō","english":"exhorting"},{"strongs":"G1957","hebrew":"ἐπιμαρτυρέω","translit":"epimartyréō","english":"testifying"},{"strongs":"G227","hebrew":"ἀληθής","translit":"alēthḗs","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G2476","hebrew":"ἵστημι","translit":"hístēmi","english":"stand"}]},{"v":13,"text":"She who is in Babylon, chosen together with you, greets you; and so does Mark, my son.","understanding":"ἀσπάζεται ὑμᾶς ἡ ἐν Βαβυλῶνι συνεκλεκτή, “She who is in Babylon, chosen together with you, greets you” — ‘she’ is most naturally the church (ἐκκλησία is feminine), ‘chosen together’ with the elect exiles of 1:1. Babylon is almost certainly a code name for Rome, the capital of the empire that would soon persecute the church, as in Revelation 17–18; early church tradition places Peter in Rome at the end of his life. Literal Babylon in Mesopotamia was largely deserted by then. The exiles in the Dispersion are greeted by the church in the new ‘Babylon’. καὶ Μᾶρκος ὁ υἱός μου, ‘and Mark, my son’ — John Mark (Acts 12:12), Peter's spiritual son, whose Gospel early writers said preserved Peter's preaching.","words":[{"strongs":"G897","hebrew":"Βαβυλών","translit":"Babylṓn","english":"Babylon"},{"strongs":"G4899","hebrew":"συνεκλεκτός","translit":"syneklektós","english":"chosen together"},{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"greets"},{"strongs":"G3138","hebrew":"Μάρκος","translit":"Márkos","english":"Mark"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"son"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Greet one another with a kiss of love. Peace be to you all who are in Christ Jesus. Amen.","understanding":"ἀσπάσασθε ἀλλήλους ἐν φιλήματι ἀγάπης, “Greet one another with a kiss of love” — two words from the love family in one phrase: φίλημα, a kiss, the sign of φιλέω-affection, and ἀγάπη, self-giving love. Paul calls it a ‘holy kiss’ (Romans 16:16). The greeting of family, across every barrier of class and race. εἰρήνη ὑμῖν πᾶσιν τοῖς ἐν Χριστῷ, “Peace be to you all who are in Christ Jesus” — εἰρήνη, the peace of 1:2. The letter that began with ‘grace and peace’ ends with peace — for suffering exiles who are ‘in Christ’.","words":[{"strongs":"G782","hebrew":"ἀσπάζομαι","translit":"aspázomai","english":"Greet"},{"strongs":"G5370","hebrew":"φίλημα","translit":"phílēma","english":"kiss"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"Peace"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Shepherd's Apron","verseRef":"v. 1-5","image":"Picture an upper room on the night before the cross. The disciples are arguing about who is the greatest. Jesus rises, takes off His outer robe, ties a towel round His waist and kneels with a basin to wash their dusty feet. Peter protests, then gives in. Decades later, an old Peter writes to the elders of the churches: shepherd the flock, do not lord it over them — and tie on humility like an apron.","meaning":"“Shepherd the flock of God which is among you, exercising the oversight, not under compulsion, but voluntarily, not for dishonest gain, but willingly” (v. 2), “neither as lording it over those entrusted to you, but making yourselves examples to the flock” (v. 3). “Yes, all of you clothe yourselves with humility” (v. 5). Leadership in Christ's church looks like a servant's towel, not a ruler's sceptre — and it is rewarded with “the crown of glory that doesn’t fade away” (v. 4)."},{"title":"Throwing the Burden","verseRef":"v. 6-7","image":"Picture a traveller staggering up a hill under a huge pack, bent double, sweating, stumbling. At the top stands a strong man who says, ‘Give it to me.’ The traveller hesitates, then heaves the pack off his shoulders and throws it into the strong man's arms. He stands up straight for the first time in miles.","meaning":"“Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time” (v. 6), “casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you” (v. 7). Humility is not only bowing before God; it is admitting you cannot carry your own anxieties. The ‘mighty hand’ that humbles you is the same hand that catches what you throw. And He does it because you matter to Him."},{"title":"The Prowling Lion","verseRef":"v. 8-9","image":"Picture a lion at the edge of a herd at dusk, roaring to frighten them. The animals scatter in panic; one young one, separated from the rest, freezes in the tall grass. The lion's eyes fix on it. But a little way off, a group stands close together, alert and unmoving, facing the lion — and the lion turns away.","meaning":"“Be sober and self-controlled. Be watchful. Your adversary, the devil, walks around like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour” (v. 8). “Withstand him steadfast in your faith, knowing that your brothers who are in the world are undergoing the same sufferings” (v. 9). The devil's roar is meant to scatter and isolate you. Stay alert, stand firm in faith, and stay close to the flock."},{"title":"The Mended Net","verseRef":"v. 10","image":"Picture a fisherman's net after a night of storm, torn in many places, tangled and useless. On the shore an old fisherman sits patiently, mending every hole, knot by knot, until the net is whole again and stronger than before. Then he hangs it on firm posts set deep in the ground to dry.","meaning":"“But may the God of all grace, who called you to his eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a little while, perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you” (v. 10). The word ‘perfect’ is the word for mending nets. Peter, the old fisherman, knew it well. Whatever your suffering has torn, the God of all grace will mend, make firm, strengthen and set on a foundation."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Shepherds of God's Flock (v. 1-4)","body":"As a fellow elder and witness of Christ's sufferings, Peter urges elders to shepherd God's flock willingly, eagerly and as examples, not for gain or by domineering, and promises them the unfading crown of glory when the Chief Shepherd appears."},{"label":"Clothed with Humility (v. 5-7)","body":"Younger people should submit to elders, and all should clothe themselves with humility, for God opposes the proud and gives grace to the humble; humble yourselves under God's mighty hand, casting every anxiety on Him, because He cares for you."},{"label":"Stand Against the Lion (v. 8-11)","body":"Be sober and watchful, for the devil prowls like a roaring lion; resist him, firm in faith, knowing believers everywhere suffer the same; after a little suffering, the God of all grace will restore, establish, strengthen and settle you; to Him be the power forever."},{"label":"Final Greetings (v. 12-14)","body":"Peter has written briefly through Silvanus to testify that this is the true grace of God; the church in Babylon and Mark send greetings; greet one another with a kiss of love; peace to all in Christ."}],"sections":[{"heading":"A Fellow Elder and a Witness","paragraphs":["Peter closes the letter by speaking to the church's leaders. He does not appeal to his rank as the leading apostle. He calls himself “a fellow elder, and a witness of the sufferings of Christ, and who will also share in the glory that will be revealed” (v. 1). That is the pattern of the whole letter in one man: he saw Christ's sufferings, and he will share Christ's glory.","His command is the one Jesus gave him by the lake: “Shepherd the flock of God which is among you” (v. 2). The flock is God's, not the elders'. Paul told the Ephesian elders the same: “to shepherd the assembly of the Lord and God which he purchased with his own blood” (Acts 20:28). Peter names three temptations of leadership — serving reluctantly, serving for money, and serving by domination — and three cures: willingness, eagerness and example. Jesus had warned the disciples, “You know that they who are recognized as rulers over the nations lord it over them” (Mark 10:42), “But it shall not be so among you, but whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant” (Mark 10:43).","The reward is not here: “When the chief Shepherd is revealed, you will receive the crown of glory that doesn’t fade away” (v. 4). Pastors and leaders often serve with little recognition. Peter reminds them that the Chief Shepherd sees, and that His crown, unlike every earthly reward, never withers. Hebrews calls Jesus “the great shepherd of the sheep” (Hebrews 13:20); Peter calls Him the Chief Shepherd, under whom every elder is an under-shepherd."]},{"heading":"Humility, Anxiety and the Mighty Hand","paragraphs":["Humility is not only for leaders. “Likewise, you younger ones, be subject to the elder. Yes, all of you clothe yourselves with humility, to subject yourselves to one another; for ‘God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble’” (v. 5). The rare verb for ‘clothe yourselves’ described tying on an apron. Peter had watched Jesus do it: He “wrapped a towel around his waist” (John 13:4), “Then he poured water into the basin, and began to wash the disciples’ feet” (John 13:5). Humility is the servant's apron every believer must wear.","Peter quotes the same Proverb James quoted (James 4:6), and like James he follows it with a command: “Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time” (v. 6). For suffering believers, God's ‘mighty hand’ may feel heavy. But it is the hand that rescued Israel from Egypt, and it will lift them up at the right time — His time, not theirs.","Then comes one of the most loved verses in Scripture: “casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you” (v. 7). In the Greek it is joined to verse 6: humbling yourself means admitting that you cannot carry your anxieties alone. Pride says, ‘I can handle it.’ Humility throws the load onto God. Jesus said, “Therefore I tell you, don’t be anxious for your life” (Matthew 6:25), and Paul, “In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God” (Philippians 4:6)."]},{"heading":"The Lion and the God of All Grace","paragraphs":["Humility before God goes together with vigilance against the devil — as in James 4:6–7. “Be sober and self-controlled. Be watchful. Your adversary, the devil, walks around like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour” (v. 8). Peter knew this from experience. In Gethsemane Jesus told him to watch and pray, and he slept. That night Satan sifted him, and he fell. Jesus had warned him and prayed for him (Luke 22:31–32). Now the restored Peter warns the church: stay awake.","The response is not panic but resistance: “Withstand him steadfast in your faith, knowing that your brothers who are in the world are undergoing the same sufferings” (v. 9). The word is the same as James's ‘Resist the devil’ and Paul's: “Therefore put on the whole armor of God, that you may be able to withstand in the evil day” (Ephesians 6:13). And believers do not stand alone: their suffering is shared by the whole worldwide family of God.","The final promise gathers up the whole letter: “But may the God of all grace, who called you to his eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a little while, perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you” (v. 10). Suffering is “a little while”; glory is “eternal”. And the four verbs describe what God does in His people through suffering: He mends what is torn, makes firm what is shaky, strengthens what is weak and sets on a foundation what was unsettled. One of the words, ‘establish’, is exactly what Jesus told Peter to do: “You, when once you have turned again, establish your brothers” (Luke 22:32). In this letter, Peter did it."]},{"heading":"Silvanus, Babylon and Mark","paragraphs":["The letter ends with names. Silvanus, Paul's companion Silas, either carried the letter or helped write it, or both (v. 12). Peter states his purpose plainly: “exhorting, and testifying that this is the true grace of God in which you stand.” Suffering does not mean they have lost God's favour. It is the true grace, and they must stand in it.","“She who is in Babylon, chosen together with you, greets you” (v. 13). The church in ‘Babylon’ sends greetings. The strongest reading is that Babylon is a code name for Rome, the capital of the world power that would soon persecute the church; Revelation uses the same name for Rome. Early tradition says Peter died in Rome under Nero. A few have taken Babylon literally (the Mesopotamian city) or as a Roman military post in Egypt, but neither fits the evidence as well. The exiles of 1:1 are greeted by fellow exiles in the capital of the world that rejects them.","And “Mark, my son” sends greetings too. John Mark had once abandoned Paul and Barnabas on a mission (Acts 15:37–39), yet later Paul wrote, “Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service” (2 Timothy 4:11). Early Christian writers say Mark's Gospel records Peter's preaching. Two men who had both failed — one by denial, one by desertion — stand together at the end of the letter, restored and useful. The God of all grace really does mend nets."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Chief Shepherd and the Restored Fisherman","paragraphs":["Peter's charge to the elders is the fruit of Jesus' charge to him by the lake. “He said to him again a second time, ‘Simon, son of Jonah, do you love me?’ He said to him, ‘Yes, Lord; you know that I have affection for you.’ He said to him, ‘Tend my sheep’” (John 21:16). The verb ‘tend’ is ποιμαίνω — the same word Peter uses in verse 2. Jesus is “the great shepherd of the sheep” (Hebrews 13:20); elders must care for the flock as those “who will give account” (Hebrews 13:17).","The crown of glory that never fades belongs with the other crowns of the New Testament: “they do it to receive a corruptible crown, but we an incorruptible” (1 Corinthians 9:25); “there is stored up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will give to me on that day” (2 Timothy 4:8); and James's “crown of life, which the Lord promised to those who love him” (James 1:12).","And the promise of verse 10 echoes Jesus' prayer for Peter: “but I prayed for you, that your faith wouldn’t fail. You, when once you have turned again, establish your brothers” (Luke 22:32). The man whose faith was kept from failing by Christ's prayer now prays the same over the churches: that the God of all grace will establish them. The kiss of love (v. 14) is the family greeting Paul also commanded: “Greet one another with a holy kiss” (Romans 16:16)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Shepherd","hebrew":"ποιμαίνω","translit":"poimaínō","strongs":"G4165 (chief shepherd ἀρχιποίμην, archipoímēn, G750)","verses":"v. 2, 4","insight":"Strong's: “to tend as a shepherd of (figuratively, superviser)”. ποιμαίνω covers everything a shepherd does: feeding, leading, protecting, seeking the lost and tending the wounded. Jesus used it when He restored Peter (John 21:16). Peter now passes the command to every elder, and points them to the ἀρχιποίμην, the Chief Shepherd, a title found nowhere else. Pastors are under-shepherds of a flock that belongs to God and will answer to the Shepherd who owns it."},{"english":"Clothe yourselves","hebrew":"ἐγκομβόομαι","translit":"enkombóomai","strongs":"G1463 (humility ταπεινοφροσύνη, tapeinophrosýnē, G5012)","verses":"v. 5","insight":"Strong's: “to engirdle oneself (for labor), i.e. figuratively (the apron as being a badge of servitude) to wear (in token of mutual deference)”. The word comes from κόμβος, a knot: the apron a slave tied on to work. Peter had seen Jesus tie a towel round His waist and wash feet. Every believer, not only leaders, is to put on ταπεινοφροσύνη — lowliness of mind, from the humble family — as a working garment, ready to serve one another."},{"english":"Worries / anxiety","hebrew":"μέριμνα","translit":"mérimna","strongs":"G3308 (casting ἐπιῤῥίπτω, epirrhíptō, G1977; cares μέλω, mélō, G3199)","verses":"v. 7","insight":"Strong's: “solicitude.” μέριμνα is from a root meaning to be divided: the anxiety that pulls the mind in many directions at once. Jesus warned that ‘the cares of this age’ choke the word (Mark 4:19). Peter's remedy is decisive: ἐπιρρίπτω, ‘throw upon’, a deliberate act of handing over. And the reason is personal: αὐτῷ μέλει, ‘it matters to Him’. God is not indifferent to what troubles you."},{"english":"Adversary","hebrew":"ἀντίδικος","translit":"antídikos","strongs":"G476 (devil διάβολος, diábolos, G1228)","verses":"v. 8","insight":"Strong's: “an opponent (in a lawsuit); specially, Satan (as the arch-enemy).” ἀντίδικος was a legal word: the opposing party in court, the accuser. The name Satan means ‘adversary’ and διάβολος means ‘slanderer’. Peter combines the courtroom and the wilderness: an accuser who prowls like a lion. But believers have an Advocate (1 John 2:1), and the lion can be resisted by those who stand firm in faith."},{"english":"Perfect / restore","hebrew":"καταρτίζω","translit":"katartízō","strongs":"G2675 (establish στηρίζω, stērízō, G4741; strengthen σθενόω, sthenóō, G4599; settle θεμελιόω, themelióō, G2311)","verses":"v. 10","insight":"Strong's: “to complete thoroughly, i.e. repair (literally or figuratively) or adjust.” καταρτίζω was used of mending nets (Mark 1:19), setting broken bones, and equipping a ship. It belongs to the perfect family: bringing something to the completeness it was made for. Peter, a fisherman, uses it first among four verbs: mend, establish, strengthen, settle. Suffering tears; God repairs, and leaves His people stronger and more firmly founded than before."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this final chapter the Holy Spirit is calling you to humble service, to hand over your anxieties, and to stand firm against the enemy — trusting the God of all grace to finish what He started.","items":[{"title":"Whose Flock Is It?","body":"Whether you lead a church, a ministry, a family or a small group, the Spirit reminds you that the people in your care belong to God. Are you serving willingly, eagerly and as an example — or reluctantly, for what you get out of it, or by controlling others (vv. 2–3)? Ask Him where you need to take off a ruler's robe and tie on a servant's apron."},{"title":"What Are You Still Carrying?","body":"Name the worries you are carrying today — money, health, family, the future, a relationship. The Spirit invites you to throw them onto God. “casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you” (v. 7). Pride keeps carrying; humility hands over. Which worry will you throw to Him now, and leave there?"},{"title":"Is the Lion Circling?","body":"The enemy looks for the sleepy and the isolated. Where have you become careless — spiritually drowsy, prayerless, cut off from other believers? “Be sober and self-controlled. Be watchful” (v. 8). The Spirit calls you to wake up, stand firm in faith and stay close to the flock. And He promises that after a little while, the God of all grace will mend and strengthen you."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Servant Check","body":"In the places where you have any authority, do people experience you as a servant or as a boss (vv. 2–3)? What would ‘clothing yourself with humility’ look like this week (v. 5)?"},{"title":"The Anxiety Check","body":"What anxieties are you carrying that you have never actually handed to God (v. 7)? How often does worry keep you awake or steal your joy?"},{"title":"The Vigilance Check","body":"Where are you most vulnerable to the devil's attack — isolation, tiredness, resentment, a secret sin (v. 8)? Who are the brothers and sisters you stand with (v. 9)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"The Casting List","body":"Tonight, write down every worry on your mind, however small. Then, one by one, pray over each: ‘Lord, I throw this onto You, because You care for me.’ Cross each one out as you pray. Put the paper in your Bible at 1 Peter 5. When a worry returns, open to that page and throw it again."},{"title":"Stand With the Flock","body":"This week, reach out to one believer who may be isolated or struggling — a call, a message, a visit. Tell them they are not alone, pray with them, and remind them of verse 10. Then commit to meeting regularly with other believers, so that you are not the one the lion finds alone."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Chief Shepherd, Lord Jesus, thank You for the elders and leaders You have given me, and for every way You have made me responsible for others. Make me a shepherd who serves willingly, eagerly and as an example, never for gain and never by domination. Clothe me with humility like the apron You wore when You washed Your disciples' feet. You oppose the proud and give grace to the humble, so I humble myself under Your mighty hand, trusting You to lift me up in Your time. I cast all my worries on You now, because You care for me. Keep me sober and watchful, for my adversary prowls like a roaring lion. Help me resist him, firm in faith, and remind me that my brothers and sisters around the world are suffering the same things. God of all grace, You called me to Your eternal glory in Christ. After I have suffered a little while, mend what is torn in me, make me firm, strengthen me and settle me on Your foundation. To You be the power forever and ever. Thank You that this is the true grace of God, and I stand in it. Peace to all who are in Christ. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Peter Asleep in Gethsemane","ref":"Matthew 26:36-46","relatedVerses":"v. 8","summary":"On the night of His arrest, Jesus took Peter, James and John deeper into the garden and asked them to watch with Him while He prayed. Three times He returned and found them sleeping. To Peter He said, ‘What, couldn’t you watch with me for one hour? Watch and pray, that you don’t enter into temptation.’ Within hours Peter, unprepared, denied Him three times. Years later Peter writes, “Be sober and self-controlled. Be watchful” (v. 8). He uses the same word Jesus used in the garden. He had learned what happens when a disciple sleeps while the lion prowls."},{"title":"Jesus Restores the Shepherd","ref":"John 21:1-19","relatedVerses":"v. 1-4, 10","summary":"After the resurrection, Peter went back to fishing. After a night of catching nothing, Jesus called from the shore, filled their net, and cooked breakfast on a charcoal fire. Then He asked Peter three times, ‘Do you love me?’ and three times gave him a charge: “Feed my lambs” (John 21:15), “Tend my sheep” (John 21:16), “Feed my sheep” (John 21:17). He also told Peter how he would die. In chapter 5 the restored fisherman, now an elder and a witness, passes on the same charge — shepherd the flock of God — and promises the same restoration he received: the God of all grace will mend, establish, strengthen and settle."},{"title":"Mark, the Deserter Made Useful","ref":"Acts 12:12; 13:13; 15:36-41; 2 Timothy 4:11","relatedVerses":"v. 13","summary":"John Mark grew up in Jerusalem; when Peter escaped from prison, he went to the house of Mark's mother, where believers were praying. Mark joined Paul and Barnabas on their first missionary journey but turned back home partway through. Later Paul refused to take him again, and the disagreement split Paul and Barnabas. But Mark did not stay a failure. Peter calls him ‘my son’, and Paul, near the end of his life, wrote, “Take Mark, and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for service” (2 Timothy 4:11). The God of all grace had restored him — just as He had restored Peter."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"1 Peter","titleEmojis":"🔥👑","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Through Suffering to Glory, Held by a Living Hope","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of 1 Peter in one overarching thought, it is this: because God has given you new birth into a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus, you can live as a holy exile in a hostile world — doing good, blessing those who curse you, following the steps of the suffering Christ, and entrusting your soul to a faithful Creator — knowing that after a little while of suffering comes eternal glory.","paragraphs":["First Peter was written to Christians who had lost their place in society. They had turned from the idols and the parties of their towns, and their neighbours now called them strange, slandered them and sometimes made them suffer. Peter did not tell them to fight back, withdraw or blend in. He told them who they were — chosen, born again, a royal priesthood, God's own people — and how to live: holy, humble, good and hopeful, following the steps of a Saviour who suffered first.","The letter is built on one pattern, which Peter states in the first chapter: “the sufferings of Christ, and the glories that would follow them” (1 Peter 1:11). What was true of Christ becomes true of His people. They suffer “for a little while” (1 Peter 1:6), and they are called “to his eternal glory by Christ Jesus” (1 Peter 5:10). Every chapter moves along that line: from the trial that proves faith like gold to the crown of glory that never fades.","The writer's own story is written between the lines. Peter had once rebuked Jesus for speaking of the cross, slept in Gethsemane, drawn a sword, denied his Lord with curses and wept bitterly. The risen Christ restored him with three questions and three commands to feed His sheep. In this letter the restored shepherd feeds the flock. He writes as “a witness of the sufferings of Christ” (1 Peter 5:1), and as a man who knew what it was for a dead hope to come alive.","Peter's name means ‘rock’, yet he never claims to be the foundation. He gives that place to Christ, “a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God, precious” (1 Peter 2:4), and makes every believer a living stone in the same house. The letter's last word is the promise he himself had experienced: the God of all grace will “perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you” (1 Peter 5:10) — the God who mends broken fishermen mends broken nets."]},"map":{"intro":"First Peter is the twenty-first book of the New Testament, the second of the letters written to the scattered church. It stands after James and before 2 Peter, and it is written to the same people James addressed — believers in ‘the Dispersion’.","sections":[{"title":"James Tested Faith; Peter Anchored Hope","body":"James and 1 Peter share many themes: joy in trials, faith proved like gold, humility before God, resisting the devil, and good works. Both quote Proverbs 3:34. But their emphasis differs. James asks, ‘Is your faith real? Show it by works.’ Peter asks, ‘How do you keep going when your faith costs you everything?’ and answers with hope: a living hope, an unfading inheritance and a coming glory. Read together, they give the church both a test and a hope."},{"title":"Israel's Titles for the Whole Church","body":"Peter applies to a largely Gentile church the titles God gave Israel at Sinai: “a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession” (1 Peter 2:9). He quotes Hosea's reversal — “who in time past were no people, but now are God’s people” (1 Peter 2:10) — and calls them exiles of the Dispersion. The story of Israel, with its exodus, wilderness wandering and exile, has become the story of every believer: redeemed by the blood of a lamb, on pilgrimage, awaiting the true homeland."},{"title":"Hard Passages Read Honestly","body":"First Peter contains some of the most debated verses in the New Testament. This study has given the strongest reading and named the alternatives. The ‘spirits in prison’ (3:19) are most likely the rebellious spirits of Noah's day, to whom the risen Christ proclaimed His victory; others hold different views, but Scripture nowhere offers a second chance after death. Baptism ‘saves’ (3:21) not as a washing but as a good conscience's pledge to God through Christ's resurrection. The gospel preached ‘to the dead’ (4:6) most likely means to believers who heard it in life and have since died. And ‘Babylon’ (5:13) is almost certainly a code name for Rome."},{"title":"Word Families in 1 Peter","body":"First Peter added three new word families to this study: birth (ἀναγεννάω ‘born again’, ἀρτιγέννητος ‘newborn’, with γεννάω, ἀποκυέω and τίκτω), redeem (λυτρόω, λύτρον, ἀπολύτρωσις, ἀγοράζω, ἐξαγοράζω) and suffer (πάσχω, πάθημα, συμπάσχω, κακοπαθέω) — Peter's own vocabulary of suffering. Its key contrasts include τηρέω and φρουρέω (the inheritance kept, the heirs guarded, 1:4–5), καιρός and χρόνος (1:5, 1:17), φιλαδελφία and ἀγάπη (1:22), λόγος and ῥῆμα (1:23, 25), ἀποκαλύπτω and φανερόω (1:20; 5:1, 4), ὑπογραμμός and τύπος (2:21; 5:3) and ἰσχύς and κράτος (4:11)."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: 1 Peter ended with suffering for a little while, eternal glory, and a God who establishes His people. The next two books are 2 Peter, Peter's last letter, written as he faced death, warning against false teachers and calling believers to grow while they wait for the day of the Lord; and 1 John, a letter about fellowship with God, walking in the light, love for one another and assurance of eternal life.","books":[{"book":"2 Peter","connection":"Peter writes again: “This is now, beloved, the second letter that I have written to you; and in both of them I stir up your sincere mind by reminding you” (2 Peter 3:1). He knows his death is near: “knowing that the putting off of my tent comes swiftly, even as our Lord Jesus Christ made clear to me” (2 Peter 1:14). The first letter dealt with pressure from outside — persecution; the second deals with danger from inside — false teachers. He begins with God's provision: “seeing that his divine power has granted to us all things that pertain to life and godliness” (2 Peter 1:3), and calls believers to add virtue to faith in a ladder of growth. He writes again as an eyewitness: “we were eyewitnesses of his majesty” (2 Peter 1:16). And the hope of 1 Peter becomes the hope of a new creation: “But, according to his promise, we look for new heavens and a new earth, in which righteousness dwells” (2 Peter 3:13). Watch for how Peter guards the truth while the church waits."},{"book":"1 John","connection":"John, Peter's companion in the boat, at the tomb and before the council, writes as an eyewitness too: “That which was from the beginning, that which we have heard, that which we have seen with our eyes, that which we saw, and our hands touched, concerning the Word of life” (1 John 1:1). Peter called believers born again; John explores what it means to be children of God: “See how great a love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God!” (1 John 3:1). Peter commanded fervent love; John says, “He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God, for God is love” (1 John 4:8). And where Peter gave hope to the suffering, John gives assurance to the doubting: “These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, that you may know that you have eternal life” (1 John 5:13). Watch for the tests of true faith John gives — truth, obedience and love."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"A Living Hope and an Unfading Inheritance","anchorRef":"1 Peter 1:3-9","body":"God “caused us to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (1 Peter 1:3), to an inheritance that cannot decay, be stained or fade, kept in heaven — while believers themselves are guarded by God's power through faith. Trials for a little while prove faith like gold in the fire, and result in praise and glory when Christ is revealed. Believers love Him without having seen Him, and rejoice with joy beyond words."},{"title":"Holy Exiles, Living Stones, a Royal Priesthood","anchorRef":"1 Peter 1:13-2:10","body":"Because God is holy, His people are to be holy in all their conduct (1 Peter 1:15–16), knowing they were redeemed not with silver or gold but with the precious blood of Christ. Born again of imperishable seed, they crave the word like newborns, and are built as living stones into a spiritual house on Christ, the rejected but precious cornerstone. They are “a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession” (1 Peter 2:9), called to proclaim His excellence."},{"title":"Good Conduct Before a Watching World","anchorRef":"1 Peter 2:11-3:17","body":"As foreigners, believers abstain from fleshly desires and live such good lives that slanderers see their good works and glorify God (1 Peter 2:12). They respect authority, serve faithfully even under harsh masters, win unbelieving spouses without a word, honour their wives as co-heirs, bless those who insult them, and are always ready to give a gentle answer “concerning the hope that is in you” (1 Peter 3:15)."},{"title":"Following the Steps of the Suffering Christ","anchorRef":"1 Peter 2:21-25; 3:18-22; 4:1, 12-19","body":"Christ suffered for us, leaving an example: no sin, no deceit, no curse in return, but trust in the righteous Judge (1 Peter 2:21–23). He bore our sins on the tree and by His wounds we are healed. He suffered once for sins to bring us to God, and now reigns with every power subject to Him. So believers arm themselves with His mind, are not surprised by the fiery trial, rejoice to share His sufferings, and “entrust their souls to him, as to a faithful Creator” (1 Peter 4:19)."},{"title":"Humility, Vigilance and the God of All Grace","anchorRef":"1 Peter 5:1-11","body":"Elders shepherd God's flock as willing servants and examples. All believers clothe themselves with humility, for God opposes the proud. They humble themselves under God's mighty hand, “casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you” (1 Peter 5:7), stay sober and watchful, and resist the prowling lion, firm in faith. And the God of all grace, after a little while, will perfect, establish, strengthen and settle them."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of 1 Peter practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":"1","title":"Live From Your Identity, Not Your Circumstances","lesson":"The Lesson: “But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession, that you may proclaim the excellence of him who called you out of darkness into his marvelous light” (1 Peter 2:9).","practice":"The Practice: Each morning for the next month, before checking your phone, read 1 Peter 1:3–5 and 2:9 aloud and say: ‘I am born again to a living hope. I am chosen, royal, holy and God's own. My inheritance is kept and I am guarded.’ When criticism, rejection or hardship comes during the day, return to those words before you respond."},{"step":"2","title":"Answer Insult With Blessing and Good Works","lesson":"The Lesson: “not rendering evil for evil, or insult for insult; but instead blessing; knowing that to this were you called, that you may inherit a blessing” (1 Peter 3:9).","practice":"The Practice: Identify one person who has mistreated or slandered you. For thirty days, pray a specific blessing over them each day, and look for one concrete good work you can do for them or in their sight. Prepare your two-minute answer about the hope within you (3:15), and ask God for an opportunity to share it with gentleness."},{"step":"3","title":"Cast, Watch and Entrust","lesson":"The Lesson: “casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you” (1 Peter 5:7), and “entrust their souls to him, as to a faithful Creator” (1 Peter 4:19).","practice":"The Practice: Every evening this month, write down your worries and throw them onto God one by one in prayer, then pray Jesus' words, ‘Father, into Your hands I commit my spirit.’ Meet weekly with at least one other believer for prayer, so that you are never alone when the lion prowls. When suffering comes, say aloud: ‘This is for a little while; His glory is eternal.’"}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ! According to Your great mercy You have caused me to be born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus from the dead. I declare that my inheritance is incorruptible, undefiled and unfading, kept in heaven for me, and that I am guarded by Your power through faith. My trials are for a little while, and they are proving my faith more precious than gold. I love Jesus though I have not seen Him, and I rejoice with joy beyond words. I was redeemed not with silver or gold but with the precious blood of Christ, the spotless Lamb, so I will be holy in all my conduct, because You are holy. I am a living stone in Your spiritual house, a member of a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, Your own possession, and I will proclaim the excellence of the One who called me out of darkness into His marvellous light. I will live as a pilgrim, abstaining from desires that war against my soul and doing good before a watching world. When I am insulted, I will bless. I will follow the steps of Jesus, who did not sin, did not curse back and did not threaten, but entrusted Himself to the righteous Judge; He bore my sins on the tree, and by His wounds I am healed. I set Christ apart as Lord in my heart, and I will be ready to give an answer for the hope within me, with gentleness and respect. I will not be surprised by the fiery trial; I will rejoice to share Christ's sufferings, for the Spirit of glory rests on me. I clothe myself with humility, I humble myself under Your mighty hand, and I cast all my worries on You, because You care for me. I will stay sober and watchful and resist the devil, firm in faith. And I declare that after I have suffered a little while, You, the God of all grace, will perfect, establish, strengthen and settle me. To You be the glory and the power forever and ever. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
